COURSES = [
{'slug': 'ai-for-beginners-course', 'title': 'Free AI Course for Beginners &amp; Over-60s | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'Learn what AI chatbots like ChatGPT really are, what they do brilliantly, and what never to tell them. Free, senior-paced lessons with a printable certificate.', 'h1': 'AI, <em class="grad grad--green">without the mystery</em>', 'heroLede': 'A free, friendly course from the family-run firm that has been untangling technology in Bournemouth since 1995. Seven short lessons explain what AI chatbots really are, what they do brilliantly and what to keep to yourself &mdash; all at your own pace, with a printable certificate at the end. No jargon, nothing to install, nothing to pay.', 'icon': '&#129302;', 'courseTitle': 'AI, Without the Mystery', 'tagline': 'Free &middot; 7 short lessons &middot; about 40 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>Welcome &mdash; and well done for being curious. AI is the biggest change to computers since the internet arrived, yet most explanations of it seem designed to confuse. This course is different. It was written for people who would rather have things explained properly, at a comfortable pace, with no jargon and no showing off.</p><p>Work through the lessons in order, or dip in wherever you like. After each one there is a gentle &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; question &mdash; not a test, just a chance to feel the ideas click into place. There is no timer and no pass mark, and your certificate is waiting at the end.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#128173;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 7', 'title': 'What AI chat actually is', 'body': '<p>You have probably heard the letters AI everywhere lately &mdash; on the news, from the grandchildren, perhaps at the surgery. AI stands for artificial intelligence, and the kind everyone is talking about is the chat kind: a website where you type a question in ordinary English and a helpful answer comes back, rather like a text conversation.</p><p>Here is the secret that takes away the mystery. An AI chatbot is not a mind, and it is not magic. It is a computer program that has read an enormous amount of writing &mdash; books, articles, websites &mdash; and become remarkably good at predicting which words usually come next. Think of it as a very well-read helper with an extraordinary memory for how sentences go.</p><p>That is why it can write a lovely letter in seconds &mdash; and also why it sometimes gets facts wrong, which we will come to in Lesson 4. It does not know things the way you do; it is simply very good at putting words together. Once you see it that way, it stops being frightening and starts being useful.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'A neighbour tells you AI chatbots are thinking machines that know everything. What is closest to the truth?', 'opts': [{'t': 'They are clever programs that predict words &mdash; very well-read, but not minds, and not always right', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Spot on. Keep that picture in your head and the rest of this course will feel like common sense.'}, {'t': 'They are thinking machines that know everything and never make mistakes', 'ok': False, 'why': 'An easy impression to get &mdash; they certainly sound sure of themselves. But they are word-predictors, not minds, and they do make mistakes. Lesson 4 will show you exactly how to handle that.'}, {'t': 'They are a passing fad that will be gone by Christmas', 'ok': False, 'why': 'AI helpers look like they are here to stay &mdash; which is exactly why a gentle course like this is worth your time. No rush, though: they will still be there whenever you are ready.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128075;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 7', 'title': 'Meeting an AI helper safely', 'body': '<p>Here is some lovely news: you do not need to buy anything or install anything. The three best-known AI helpers all have free versions you can use in your web browser, at the time of writing:</p><ul><li><strong>ChatGPT</strong>, made by OpenAI &mdash; at <strong>chatgpt.com</strong></li><li><strong>Microsoft Copilot</strong> &mdash; at <strong>copilot.microsoft.com</strong>, and also built into recent versions of Windows</li><li><strong>Google Gemini</strong> &mdash; at <strong>gemini.google.com</strong></li></ul><p>They all work in much the same way: a box where you type your question, a button to send it (usually an arrow symbol), and the reply appears underneath. Some ask you to sign in with an email address for the free version. The exact screens vary by app and change over time, so take it slowly and read what is in front of you &mdash; there is never any rush.</p><p>Our advice: pick <em>one</em> and get comfortable with it. There is no wrong choice &mdash; they are more alike than different &mdash; and you cannot break anything by asking a question. (They all work on Apple devices too, but our examples stick to Windows and Android.)</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You would like to try an AI helper for the very first time. What is the gentlest way to start?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Buy a subscription first, because the free versions must be a trick', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A sensible instinct to be wary &mdash; but in this case the big three genuinely offer free versions at the time of writing. Start free; you may never need anything more.'}, {'t': 'Open one of the free ones &mdash; such as copilot.microsoft.com &mdash; in your web browser and type a simple question', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect. Nothing to install, nothing to pay, and nothing can go wrong from asking a question. That is genuinely all there is to it.'}, {'t': 'Download an app from a link in an email advert', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Good caution needed here: links in adverts and unexpected emails are how dodgy apps spread. If you ever want an app on your phone, get it from the official Google Play Store and check the reviews first &mdash; but for these helpers, the website version is all you need.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#127775;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 7', 'title': 'What it is brilliant at', 'body': '<p>This is the joyful part. An AI helper is like having a patient, well-read friend on call, and it shines at everyday jobs:</p><ul><li><strong>Drafting letters</strong> &mdash; a complaint about a late delivery, a note to the council, a thank-you that strikes just the right tone</li><li><strong>Explaining jargon</strong> &mdash; paste in a baffling sentence from a bill or a letter and ask, &ldquo;What does this mean in plain English?&rdquo;</li><li><strong>Recipes from leftovers</strong> &mdash; tell it what is in the fridge and ask what you could make tonight</li><li><strong>Hobby research</strong> &mdash; family history, garden birds, crossword clues, the history of your street</li><li><strong>Poems and verses</strong> &mdash; a funny rhyme for a birthday card, written about the actual person</li></ul><p>The trick that makes it all work: you can always ask again. Say &ldquo;shorter, please&rdquo;, &ldquo;make it friendlier&rdquo; or &ldquo;explain that more simply&rdquo; and it will redo the whole thing without a flicker of impatience. It never gets tired, and it never thinks a question is silly.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You want to complain politely to a company about a late delivery, but the words just will not come. What could you try?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Tell the chatbot what happened and ask for a polite draft, then read it, tweak it and send it yourself', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. It does the wording; you stay in charge. Most people find the draft needs only a tweak or two &mdash; and asking for a rewrite costs nothing.'}, {'t': 'Ask the chatbot to sort the whole thing out, and assume the job is done', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A very understandable hope! But the chatbot can only put words on your screen &mdash; it cannot post letters or ring the company. Think of it as a writing partner: it drafts, you check and send.'}, {'t': 'Struggle on alone &mdash; a machine could never help with something personal like this', 'ok': False, 'why': 'You might be pleasantly surprised. Finding the right words is one of the things AI helpers do best. You supply the facts and the feelings; it supplies the sentences; the final say is always yours.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#129300;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 7', 'title': 'Its big weakness: confidently wrong', 'body': '<p>Now for the most important lesson in the course. AI chatbots sometimes get things wrong &mdash; and here is the tricky part: they get things wrong <em>confidently</em>. A wrong answer arrives in exactly the same fluent, friendly tone as a right one. Computer people call these mistakes &ldquo;hallucinations&rdquo;, which is just a fancy word for the chatbot making something up without realising it has.</p><p>Why does it happen? Remember Lesson 1: the chatbot predicts words. Usually that produces the truth, because it has read so much. But sometimes it produces something that merely <em>sounds</em> true &mdash; a made-up date, a wrong dose, a phone number that does not exist.</p><p>So here is the golden rule of the whole course: <strong>double-check any fact that matters before you act on it.</strong> For anything medical, legal or to do with money, treat the chatbot as a starting point only &mdash; then check with your pharmacist, your GP, your bank or another source you trust. A birthday poem needs no checking. A pill always does.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">You ask about some berries growing in your garden. The chatbot replies:</p><p>&ldquo;Good news &mdash; those berries are perfectly safe to eat.&rdquo;</p></div>', 'q': 'It sounds completely certain. What do you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Eat them &mdash; the chatbot sounded very sure of itself', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That confident tone is exactly the trap. Chatbots sound just as sure when they are wrong as when they are right. Anything that could affect your health deserves a proper human check first.'}, {'t': 'Ask the same chatbot again &mdash; if it says yes twice, it must be right', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A clever thought, but it can happily repeat the same mistake twice. Confidence is not the same as accuracy &mdash; for anything that matters, check with a source outside the chatbot.'}, {'t': 'Treat it as a guess, not gospel &mdash; check with a trusted source such as a gardening book, a garden centre or an expert before anyone eats a thing', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. When the stakes are real &mdash; food, medicines, money, the law &mdash; a chatbot&rsquo;s answer is a starting point, never the final word. You have just learned the most valuable habit in this course.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128274;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 7', 'title': 'What never to tell it', 'body': '<p>Chatting with an AI helper can feel private, like writing in a diary. It is not. Your conversations travel over the internet to the company&rsquo;s computers, and may be stored or used to improve the service. Nothing sinister &mdash; but it leads to one simple rule:</p><p><strong>Never type anything into a chatbot that you would not write on a postcard.</strong> That means:</p><ul><li>No passwords or PINs &mdash; ever, for anything</li><li>No card numbers or bank details</li><li>No full set of personal details together &mdash; full name plus address plus date of birth</li><li>Nothing deeply private that you would hate a stranger to read</li></ul><p>The good news: you almost never need any of that. Asking for help with a letter to your bank? Write &ldquo;[account number]&rdquo; as a placeholder and fill in the real one by hand afterwards. And remember: no genuine company will ever ask you to type a password or a one-time code into a chat &mdash; a request like that is always a red flag.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">You are asking a chatbot for help:</p><p>&ldquo;Please write a firm but polite letter to my bank about a payment that went missing on the 3rd of June&hellip;&rdquo;</p></div>', 'q': 'Should you include your account number and password so the letter is complete?', 'opts': [{'t': 'No &mdash; just tell it the story, write [account number] as a placeholder, and add the real details by hand afterwards', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect. The chatbot never needs your real details to write an excellent letter &mdash; placeholders do the job, and your private information stays exactly where it belongs: with you.'}, {'t': 'Yes &mdash; the more detail it has, the better the letter will be', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A natural thought, but this is the one to avoid. A chatbot is not a private diary &mdash; and no letter ever needs your password anyway. Remember the postcard rule: if you would not write it on a postcard, do not type it into a chat.'}, {'t': 'Include the account number but keep the password back', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Halfway there &mdash; good instinct on the password! But bank details belong on the postcard-rule list too. A placeholder like [account number] gets you a perfect letter with nothing private shared at all.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128737;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 7', 'title': 'AI scams to watch for', 'body': '<p>AI has handed scammers some new tools, and forewarned is forearmed. The big one to know about is <strong>voice cloning</strong>: from a short clip of someone speaking &mdash; a video shared online is enough &mdash; software can fake a phone call that sounds exactly like a loved one. Combine that with the well-known &ldquo;Hi Mum&rdquo; trick on WhatsApp, where a message from a &ldquo;child&rdquo; on a &ldquo;new number&rdquo; asks for money urgently, and you can see the danger. (One more WhatsApp rule worth knowing: never read out or pass on a six-digit code that arrives by text message &mdash; that code is the key to your account, and tricking people into sharing it is how accounts get stolen.)</p><p>The defence is wonderfully old-fashioned: <strong>agree a family codeword</strong> &mdash; a word only your family knows. Any emergency call asking for money? Ask for the codeword. And whatever the voice says, hang up and ring the person back on the number you already have for them. Real family will not mind one bit.</p><p>AI can also produce fake photos and videos that look startlingly real. So the golden rule of scam safety applies double now: anything urgent, emotional and about money deserves a slow, calm double-check. And if the worst happens, act quickly and without a shred of embarrassment: call your bank straight away &mdash; dialling <strong>159</strong> connects you safely to most UK banks &mdash; forward scam texts free to <strong>7726</strong>, forward scam emails to <strong>report@phishing.gov.uk</strong>, and report it to <strong>Action Fraud</strong> on <strong>0300 123 2040</strong> (in Scotland, tell Police Scotland on 101). And remember: banks will never ask you to move money to a &ldquo;safe account&rdquo; &mdash; anyone who does is a scammer.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">Your phone rings. A voice that sounds exactly like your grandson says:</p><p>&ldquo;Gran, it&rsquo;s me &mdash; I&rsquo;m in trouble and my phone&rsquo;s broken. I need &pound;300 today. Please don&rsquo;t tell Mum.&rdquo;</p></div>', 'q': 'It truly sounds like him. What do you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Send the money at once &mdash; you would never forgive yourself if it were real', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That feeling is precisely what the scam runs on &mdash; urgency and love. But a two-minute call-back on a trusted number costs a real grandson nothing and saves you from a cruel trick. Slow down first, always.'}, {'t': 'Stay calm, ask for the family codeword, then hang up and ring him back on the number you already have', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. The codeword and the call-back defeat even a perfect voice clone. If he is really in trouble, he will still be in trouble in two minutes &mdash; and now you will know it is truly him.'}, {'t': 'Keep him talking and ask questions only he would know the answers to', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A clever instinct &mdash; but scammers do their homework, and family details often sit on social media. The test that never fails is simpler: hang up and ring back on the number you already trust.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#9749;', 'tag': 'Lesson 7 of 7', 'title': 'Try it today: your first questions', 'body': '<p>The best way to take the mystery out of AI is simply to have a go. Put the kettle on, open ChatGPT, Copilot or Gemini, and try any of these:</p><ul><li>&ldquo;Explain what a QR code is, in plain English, for someone in their seventies.&rdquo;</li><li>&ldquo;I have eggs, cheese, a leek and some tired-looking potatoes. What could I cook tonight?&rdquo;</li><li>&ldquo;Write a short, funny poem for my sister&rsquo;s 70th birthday. She loves gardening and strong tea.&rdquo;</li><li>&ldquo;Suggest some gentle exercises I can do sitting down.&rdquo; (Anything health-related, run past your GP too &mdash; Lesson 4!)</li><li>&ldquo;Tell me something interesting about the history of Bournemouth.&rdquo;</li></ul><p>Two habits to keep from day one. First: if the answer misses the mark, just ask again in different words &mdash; it never gets impatient, and neither should you. Second: enjoy the help, but double-check anything that matters. Do that, and you are using AI more wisely than most people half your age.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'Which of these makes the best first question for an AI helper?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Whether you should sell your house &mdash; including your address and savings details so it can advise properly', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Two lessons rolled into one here! Big money decisions belong with qualified humans you trust, and personal details never belong in a chat. By all means ask it to explain house-selling jargon &mdash; just keep the private details and the final decision with people.'}, {'t': 'Nothing at all, in case you break something', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Here is the freeing truth: you cannot break anything by asking a question. The worst that can happen is a dull answer &mdash; in which case you simply ask again. Go on, have a go.'}, {'t': 'Something you have always wondered about, asked in your own words &mdash; and &ldquo;explain it simply&rdquo; if the answer is too clever', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Lovely choice. Curiosity is the perfect starting point, and asking for a simpler answer is not cheating &mdash; it is exactly how these helpers are meant to be used.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': '<p>Congratulations &mdash; you have finished the course, and you now understand AI better than a great many people who use it every day. If you keep just one habit, make it this: <strong>enjoy the help, but double-check any fact that matters before you act on it</strong> &mdash; and never share passwords, card details or full personal details with a chatbot.</p><p>And remember, you are never on your own with technology. Real, friendly help is one phone call away on <strong>01202 775566</strong>, and we run relaxed in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth. Do come and say hello.</p>', 'certCourseName': 'AI, Without the Mystery', 'certSubline': 'Understanding AI helpers: what they are, what they do well, what to keep private, and how to spot AI scams', 'checklist': ['Remember what AI chat is: a well-read helper that predicts words - clever, but not a mind and not always right.', 'Try one free helper in your web browser: chatgpt.com, copilot.microsoft.com or gemini.google.com. Nothing to install.', 'Use it for the good stuff: drafting letters, explaining jargon, recipes from leftovers, hobby research, birthday poems.', 'Double-check any fact that matters before acting on it - especially health, legal and money questions.', 'Never type passwords, PINs, card numbers or bank details into a chatbot.', 'Keep full personal details out of chats - use placeholders like [account number] in draft letters.', 'Agree a family codeword to defeat AI voice-cloning calls asking for money.', 'For any urgent call about money: hang up and ring the person back on a number you already trust.', 'If a scam strikes: dial 159 to reach your bank, forward scam texts to 7726 and scam emails to report@phishing.gov.uk, and tell Action Fraud on 0300 123 2040.', 'Stuck or unsure? Call 365 Techies on 01202 775566 - friendly local help since 1995.'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is this course really free?', 'a': '<p>Completely free, with no catch and no card details asked for. We are a family-run IT firm that has looked after Bournemouth homes and businesses since 1995, and this course is our way of helping local people feel confident with new technology.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I need to install anything or create an account?', 'a': '<p>Nothing at all for this course &mdash; it runs right here in your web browser. The AI helpers themselves (ChatGPT, Microsoft Copilot, Google Gemini) may ask you to sign in with an email address for their free versions, but that is entirely up to you and can wait until you feel ready.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is the certificate a qualification?', 'a': '<p>It is a 365 Techies certificate of completion &mdash; a lovely thing to print, keep or show the grandchildren, but not an accredited qualification. The real prize is the confidence and the safe habits you take away with you.</p>'}, {'q': 'I have never used AI before. Is this too advanced for me?', 'a': '<p>Quite the opposite &mdash; it was written especially for complete beginners. Every idea is explained in plain English, every lesson is short, and there is no timer, no pass mark and no way to fail. You set the pace, and you can revisit any lesson as often as you like.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is AI actually safe to use?', 'a': '<p>Yes &mdash; used with the habits in this course. Ask it anything you like, but double-check facts that matter, keep passwords and bank details out of your chats, and never let it make medical, legal or money decisions on its own. Follow those rules and it is a genuinely helpful companion.</p>'}, {'q': 'Can I get help in person?', 'a': '<p>Absolutely. We run relaxed, friendly in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth &mdash; no question too small. Call us on 01202 775566 and we will find a time that suits you.</p>'}], 'crumbName': 'AI, Without the Mystery'},
{'slug': 'whatsapp-course-for-beginners', 'title': 'Free WhatsApp Course for Beginners &amp; Over 60s | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'A free, friendly WhatsApp course for older beginners. Learn messages, photos, voice notes, video calls and staying safe, at your own pace, with a certificate.', 'h1': 'WhatsApp, <em class="grad grad--green">step by step</em>', 'heroLede': 'The messages, the photos, the video calls &mdash; all easier than you think, and all free over Wi-Fi. This free course walks you through WhatsApp one gentle lesson at a time, entirely at your own pace, with no jargon and nothing to break. Finish the lessons and print a certificate with your name on it.', 'icon': '&#128172;', 'courseTitle': 'WhatsApp, Step by Step', 'tagline': 'Free · 7 short lessons · about 35 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>Welcome &mdash; and well done for being here. WhatsApp is how millions of families share their days: quick messages, photos of the grandchildren, video calls that make the miles disappear. If you&rsquo;ve ever felt left out of the family group chat, this course is for you.</p><p>There&rsquo;s no rush and no test you can fail. Each lesson is short, written in plain English, and ends with one gentle &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; question. Have your phone next to you if you can &mdash; and remember, you cannot break anything by trying.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#128075;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 7', 'title': 'What WhatsApp is (and why your family loves it)', 'body': '<p>WhatsApp is a free app for sending messages and photos, and for making voice and video calls over the internet. On your phone it&rsquo;s the green icon with a white telephone inside a speech bubble.</p><p>Because it works over your home Wi-Fi, there&rsquo;s nothing extra to pay &mdash; a message to your neighbour costs the same as a video call to a grandchild in Australia: nothing. That&rsquo;s why families everywhere use it for sharing photos, keeping group chats going and seeing each other&rsquo;s faces.</p><p>It&rsquo;s private, too. WhatsApp messages and calls are end-to-end encrypted, which simply means they&rsquo;re scrambled so that only you and the person you&rsquo;re talking to can read or hear them.</p><p>This course describes WhatsApp as it appears on Android phones, the kind most of our customers have. WhatsApp works on Apple iPhones too &mdash; it&rsquo;s the same app and looks very similar, so everything here still applies.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'Your son lives in Australia and suggests a WhatsApp video call. What will it cost you?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Nothing extra, as long as I&rsquo;m connected to my home Wi-Fi', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. WhatsApp calls travel over the internet, so a call to Australia costs no more than one to next door &mdash; nothing at all on Wi-Fi.'}, {'t': 'Expensive international call rates, so best keep it short', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Good news &mdash; that&rsquo;s the old way! WhatsApp calls go over the internet, so on your home Wi-Fi they cost nothing, however far away family are and however long you chat.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128172;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 7', 'title': 'Sending your first message', 'body': '<p>Open WhatsApp by tapping the green icon. You&rsquo;ll see a list of your chats &mdash; conversations you&rsquo;ve had, newest at the top. If the list is empty, that&rsquo;s fine: yours is a fresh start.</p><p>To start a new chat, look for the new-chat button &mdash; usually a round green button with a speech bubble or plus sign on it (its exact look varies a little by phone maker and app version). Tap it, then choose the person you want from your contact list.</p><p>Tap the long typing box at the bottom, type your message, and when you&rsquo;re happy, tap the send button &mdash; the little arrow beside the box. Here&rsquo;s the most reassuring part: <strong>nothing is sent until you tap that arrow</strong>. You can take five seconds or five minutes. WhatsApp will wait.</p><p>After sending, you&rsquo;ll see small ticks next to your message: one tick means it has been sent, two ticks mean it has arrived on their phone.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You&rsquo;ve typed a message to your friend but haven&rsquo;t tapped the arrow yet. Has it been sent?', 'opts': [{'t': 'No &mdash; nothing goes anywhere until I tap the send arrow', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Spot on. You can pause, reread and change your mind as often as you like. WhatsApp only sends when you tap the arrow &mdash; you are always in charge.'}, {'t': 'Yes &mdash; it sends as I type, so I need to be quick', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Happily not! Nothing is sent until you tap the arrow, so there&rsquo;s no need to rush. Take all the time you like &mdash; the message simply sits there waiting for you.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128247;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 7', 'title': 'Photos and voice notes (a gift for tired fingers)', 'body': '<p>To send a photo, open the chat and look next to the typing box for a camera or paperclip icon. The camera takes a new photo on the spot; the paperclip (on some phones a plus sign) lets you choose one already saved on your phone. Pick your photo, add a caption if you like, and tap send.</p><p>Now for many people&rsquo;s favourite discovery: <strong>voice notes</strong>. Instead of typing, press and hold the microphone icon beside the typing box, speak your message, then let go to send it. Your voice arrives just as you said it &mdash; warm, quick, and no fiddly typing. For anyone who finds small keyboards a chore, this is genuinely liberating.</p><p>If a recording goes wrong halfway through, don&rsquo;t worry &mdash; slide your finger towards the bin to cancel it and start again. Nobody ever hears the false starts.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'Typing feels slow and fiddly today. What&rsquo;s the easiest way to send a longer message?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Hold down the microphone button and simply speak my message', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Yes! Voice notes are made for exactly this. Speak naturally, let go, and it&rsquo;s sent &mdash; many people try one and never look back.'}, {'t': 'Struggle on with typing &mdash; there&rsquo;s no other way', 'ok': False, 'why': 'There&rsquo;s a much easier way! Press and hold the microphone icon beside the typing box and just talk. Voice notes are one of WhatsApp&rsquo;s kindest features.'}, {'t': 'Wait for a grandchild to visit and type it for me', 'ok': False, 'why': 'They&rsquo;d be happy to help &mdash; but you don&rsquo;t need to wait for anyone. Hold the microphone button and speak; your message sends the moment you let go.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128249;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 7', 'title': 'Video calling the family', 'body': '<p>Seeing faces is what WhatsApp does best. Open the chat with the person you want, then tap the <strong>video camera icon</strong> near the top of the screen. That&rsquo;s it &mdash; the call starts ringing on their phone.</p><p>Two small tricks make a big difference. First, light: sit facing a window or lamp, not with it behind you, so your face is bright rather than in shadow. Second, steadiness: prop the phone against something &mdash; a pile of books, a mug, a little stand &mdash; rather than holding it at arm&rsquo;s length. Your arm (and the picture) will thank you.</p><p>When someone video calls you, your phone rings and shows an answer button &mdash; tap (or on some phones swipe) the green accept button and say hello. On Wi-Fi the whole thing is free, however long you natter, and nobody minds what the kitchen looks like behind you.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'On a video call, your daughter says your face is just a dark shadow. What&rsquo;s the fix?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Turn so the window or lamp is in front of me, lighting my face', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect. Light in front, not behind &mdash; it&rsquo;s the one tip that makes everyone instantly clearer. Add a propped-up phone and you&rsquo;ll look like a natural.'}, {'t': 'Sit with my back to the bright window', 'ok': False, 'why': 'So close &mdash; it&rsquo;s the other way round! With the window behind you, the camera sees only a silhouette. Face the light and your family will see you beautifully.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128106;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 7', 'title': 'Groups, without the overwhelm', 'body': '<p>A group is one shared chat where everyone in it &mdash; the whole family, the book club, the bowls team &mdash; sees every message. It&rsquo;s wonderful for sharing news and photos with everybody at once.</p><p>But a lively group can ping all day, and that can feel like a lot. Here&rsquo;s the secret nobody tells beginners: <strong>you can mute a group, and it is not rude</strong>. Open the group and find the Mute option &mdash; usually by tapping the group&rsquo;s name at the top of the chat (menus vary a little by phone and app version) &mdash; then choose how long you&rsquo;d like the quiet to last, from a few hours right up to always. The messages still arrive; they just arrive quietly, ready for when you fancy a browse with a cup of tea.</p><p>And a gentle rule for your own peace of mind: you don&rsquo;t have to reply to everything. Nobody expects it &mdash; not even the person who sends forty photos of their dahlias.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'The family group pings all day and it&rsquo;s wearing you out. What&rsquo;s the kind, sensible fix?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Mute the group &mdash; messages still arrive, just quietly, for me to read when it suits', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. Muting is the polite volume knob of WhatsApp. You stay in the group and miss nothing &mdash; you simply read on your own schedule.'}, {'t': 'Leave the group &mdash; it&rsquo;s the only way to get some peace', 'ok': False, 'why': 'You&rsquo;d miss all the photos! There&rsquo;s a gentler option: mute the group. Everything still arrives, silently, and nobody is any the wiser.'}, {'t': 'Reply to every single message so nobody is offended', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That would exhaust anyone! Group chats aren&rsquo;t like conversation &mdash; dipping in when you feel like it is completely normal, and nobody keeps score.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128737;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 7', 'title': 'Staying safe on WhatsApp', 'body': '<p>WhatsApp itself is safe &mdash; but scammers use it, just as they use the phone and the post. The one to know is the <strong>&ldquo;Hi Mum&rdquo; scam</strong> (or &ldquo;Hi Dad&rdquo;). A message arrives from an unknown number: &ldquo;Hi Mum, I&rsquo;ve broken my phone &mdash; this is my new number&rdquo;. Soon after comes an urgent request for money. It feels real because it uses your love against you.</p><p>The golden rule: <strong>never send money because of a message alone</strong>. Ring your child or grandchild on the number you&rsquo;ve always had for them &mdash; the old number &mdash; or ring another family member. A real emergency survives a two-minute phone call; a scam doesn&rsquo;t. Many families also agree a private codeword only they know, which beats even the AI voice-cloning tricks scammers now use to fake family emergency calls.</p><p>Two more habits. If a stranger&rsquo;s message looks fishy, open the chat and use <strong>Report</strong> and <strong>Block</strong> &mdash; WhatsApp offers both for any contact. And never share the <strong>6-digit code</strong> WhatsApp texts you &mdash; not with anyone, even someone claiming to be family or WhatsApp itself. That code is the key to your account, and handing it over is exactly how accounts get stolen.</p><p>And if a scammer ever does catch you out, you&rsquo;re in good company and it is never your fault &mdash; these tricks fool sharp, kind people every day. Ring your bank straight away (dialling <strong>159</strong> connects you securely to most UK banks), then report it to <strong>Action Fraud on 0300 123 2040</strong>. Acting quickly gives your bank the best chance of stopping the money.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">A message arrives from a number you don&rsquo;t recognise:</p><p>&ldquo;Hi Mum, it&rsquo;s me &mdash; dropped my phone down the loo so this is my new number! Bit of a nightmare&hellip; my card&rsquo;s blocked and a bill is due today. Could you send &pound;850? I&rsquo;ll pay you back Friday, promise xx&rdquo;</p></div>', 'q': 'What do you do first?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Ring my child on the number I&rsquo;ve always had for them (or ring another family member) before doing anything else', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect &mdash; that phone call is the scam-killer. If it really is them, you&rsquo;ve lost two minutes. If it isn&rsquo;t, you&rsquo;ve just saved &pound;850. Then report and block the new number.'}, {'t': 'Send the money &mdash; it sounds urgent and they promised to pay it back', 'ok': False, 'why': 'This is the well-known &ldquo;Hi Mum&rdquo; scam, and it fools kind, loving people every single day &mdash; that&rsquo;s exactly who it&rsquo;s aimed at. Always ring the old number first; a real emergency can wait two minutes.'}, {'t': 'Reply to the new number and ask if it&rsquo;s really them', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A good instinct to check &mdash; but a scammer will simply answer &ldquo;yes, it&rsquo;s me!&rdquo;. The only check that works is ringing the number you&rsquo;ve always had for them, or another family member.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#129529;', 'tag': 'Lesson 7 of 7', 'title': 'A little housekeeping', 'body': '<p>Photos and videos are the joy of WhatsApp &mdash; and, over time, the thing that fills a phone up. If your phone grumbles about storage, WhatsApp&rsquo;s settings include storage tools that show which chats take up the most space and let you clear out old photos and videos you no longer need. Deleting them from your phone doesn&rsquo;t remove them from the sender&rsquo;s phone, so nothing precious is lost to the family.</p><p>It&rsquo;s also worth a quick privacy check. In WhatsApp&rsquo;s <strong>Settings</strong>, under <strong>Privacy</strong>, you can choose who sees your profile photo &mdash; the choices include everyone, just your contacts, or nobody at all. &ldquo;My contacts&rdquo; is a comfortable choice for most people &mdash; family see your smiling face, strangers don&rsquo;t.</p><p>Finally, when your phone offers an update &mdash; for WhatsApp through the Google Play Store, or for Android itself &mdash; say yes. Updates are free, and they patch the security holes scammers hunt for. An up-to-date phone is a safer phone.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You&rsquo;d rather strangers couldn&rsquo;t see your profile photo. Is that possible?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Yes &mdash; in WhatsApp&rsquo;s privacy settings I can limit it to my contacts, or hide it completely', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Quite right. It&rsquo;s a one-minute change under Privacy in WhatsApp&rsquo;s settings, and then only the people you choose see your photo.'}, {'t': 'No &mdash; profile photos are always public', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Good news: you have full control. WhatsApp&rsquo;s privacy settings let you show your photo to everyone, just your contacts, or nobody at all.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': '<p>Well done &mdash; you&rsquo;ve finished the whole course! You can now send messages, share photos, speak a voice note, video call the family, tame a noisy group and spot the scams. That&rsquo;s a proper set of skills, learned entirely at your own pace.</p><p>If you keep just one habit, keep this one: <strong>if any message asks for money or a code, stop and ring the person on the number you&rsquo;ve always had for them</strong>. And remember &mdash; help is only ever one phone call away on <strong>01202 775566</strong>, and we run friendly in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth.</p>', 'certCourseName': 'WhatsApp, Step by Step', 'certSubline': 'Messaging, photos, voice notes, video calls, groups and staying safe on WhatsApp', 'checklist': ['Look for the green icon with a white phone in a speech bubble.', 'Nothing sends until you tap the send arrow, so take your time.', 'Hold the microphone button to speak a voice note instead of typing.', 'Send photos with the camera or paperclip icon next to the typing box.', 'On video calls, face the light and prop your phone steady.', 'Mute noisy groups. It is not rude and you still get every message.', 'New number asking for money? Ring the old number first. Agree a family codeword too.', 'Never share your 6-digit WhatsApp code with anyone at all.', 'Sent money to a scammer? Ring your bank first (dial 159), then Action Fraud on 0300 123 2040.', 'Say yes to free updates. Stuck? Call 365 Techies on 01202 775566.'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is WhatsApp really free?', 'a': '<p>Yes. The app is free, and messages and calls cost nothing when you&rsquo;re on Wi-Fi &mdash; even to family abroad. Away from Wi-Fi it uses your mobile data allowance instead, so if you&rsquo;re mostly at home, it costs you nothing at all.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I need a computer for this course?', 'a': '<p>No &mdash; just a comfy chair. The course reads nicely on a phone, tablet or computer screen, but WhatsApp itself lives on your phone, so it helps to have the phone beside you as you go through the lessons.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is the certificate a qualification?', 'a': '<p>It&rsquo;s a 365 Techies certificate of completion &mdash; a lovely marker of what you&rsquo;ve learned, with your name on it, ready to print. It isn&rsquo;t a formal qualification, but it&rsquo;s certainly something to be proud of and pop on the fridge.</p>'}, {'q': 'I don&rsquo;t have WhatsApp on my phone yet. What do I do?', 'a': '<p>On an Android phone, open the <strong>Google Play Store</strong>, search for WhatsApp and tap Install &mdash; it&rsquo;s free. You&rsquo;ll confirm your mobile number, and WhatsApp will text you a 6-digit code to type in (keep that code to yourself, always). If you&rsquo;d like a friendly hand setting it up, that&rsquo;s exactly what our Kinson lessons are for.</p>'}, {'q': 'What if I make a mistake and send something silly?', 'a': '<p>It happens to everyone, and it&rsquo;s never a disaster. If you spot it soon after sending, WhatsApp even lets you delete the message for everyone in the chat &mdash; and honestly, family are usually just delighted to hear from you. There&rsquo;s no mistake on WhatsApp that can&rsquo;t be fixed or laughed off.</p>'}, {'q': 'Can I get help in person?', 'a': '<p>Yes! We run relaxed, in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth, and we&rsquo;re always at the end of the phone on <strong>01202 775566</strong>. No question is too small &mdash; we&rsquo;ve been helping local people with their technology since 1995.</p>'}], 'crumbName': 'WhatsApp, Step by Step'},
{'slug': 'android-phone-course-for-beginners', 'title': 'Free Android Phone Course for Beginners | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'A free Android phone course for older beginners &mdash; home screen, settings, apps, photos, updates and phone scams. Learn at your own pace, free certificate.', 'h1': 'Your Android phone, <em class="grad grad--green">tamed</em>', 'heroLede': 'A free, gentle course for anyone who wants to feel at home on their Android phone. Seven short lessons you can take entirely at your own pace &mdash; and a printable certificate with your name on it when you finish. No jargon, no rushing, no silly questions.', 'icon': '&#128241;', 'courseTitle': 'Your Android Phone, Tamed', 'tagline': 'Free · 7 short lessons · about 25 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>Welcome &mdash; and well done for being here. This course is for you if your Android phone sometimes feels like it belongs to someone cleverer. It doesn&rsquo;t. By the end of these seven short lessons, it will feel like yours.</p><p>Each lesson takes a few minutes, there&rsquo;s a gentle &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; question after each one, and you can stop for a cup of tea whenever you like &mdash; your place is saved. One thing before we start: <strong>you cannot break your phone by learning about it.</strong> Promise.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#127968;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 7', 'title': 'The home screen, without fear', 'body': '<p>Here&rsquo;s the most important thing in this whole course: <strong>you cannot easily break your phone by tapping things</strong>. Apps are just doors &mdash; tapping one opens it, and you can always walk straight back out again.</p><p>Every Android phone has two faithful rescuers. The <strong>Home</strong> control &mdash; a button at the bottom, or a quick swipe up from the bottom edge, depending on your phone &mdash; takes you straight back to your familiar home screen from anywhere. The <strong>Back</strong> control &mdash; an arrow, or a swipe in from the side edge &mdash; takes you one step back. Makers such as Samsung, Google and Motorola arrange these slightly differently, but every Android phone has them.</p><p>So give yourself permission to explore. Tap an app, have a look around, then press Home. Nothing is lost, nothing is broken &mdash; you&rsquo;re just opening and closing doors.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#128241; On your phone</p>You tap something by accident and a screen you&rsquo;ve never seen before appears. Your heart sinks a little.</div>', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the best thing to do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Turn the phone off and leave it in a drawer', 'ok': False, 'why': 'You haven&rsquo;t broken anything &mdash; promise! Nothing that appears on screen has damaged your phone, and there&rsquo;s a much gentler way back.'}, {'t': 'Press Home (or swipe up) and start again from the home screen', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. Home always brings you back to familiar ground, wherever you&rsquo;ve wandered. That&rsquo;s why you can explore without fear.'}, {'t': 'Tap buttons quickly until something looks familiar', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Very understandable, but hurried tapping can lead you further in. One calm press of Home is all it takes.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#9881;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 7', 'title': 'A few settings that change everything', 'body': '<p>A handful of settings make your phone dramatically nicer to use &mdash; and the best one takes two minutes: <strong>make the text bigger</strong>. Open the <strong>Settings</strong> app (its icon looks like a gear or cog), look for <strong>Display</strong>, then something like <strong>Font size</strong> or <strong>Text size</strong>, and slide it up until reading feels effortless. The exact wording and place vary a little by maker &mdash; and if you can&rsquo;t spot it, most phones have a search box at the top of Settings where you can type &ldquo;font&rdquo;. Either way it&rsquo;s in there, and it&rsquo;s the single best change most people ever make.</p><ul><li><strong>Volume</strong> &mdash; the buttons on the side of the phone turn the sound up and down</li><li><strong>Brightness</strong> &mdash; swipe down from the very top of the screen and you&rsquo;ll usually find a brightness slider. A brighter screen is easier to read, but uses more battery</li></ul><p>And a word about the internet. <strong>Wi-Fi</strong> is your home broadband &mdash; once your phone is connected, using it costs nothing extra. <strong>Mobile data</strong> is the internet over the phone network, which comes out of your monthly allowance &mdash; your phone switches to it automatically when you&rsquo;re out and about. At home, Wi-Fi is your friend.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'The writing on your phone always feels too small to read comfortably. What&rsquo;s the best fix?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Put up with it &mdash; that&rsquo;s just how phones are', 'ok': False, 'why': 'You really don&rsquo;t have to! Bigger text is built into every Android phone, it&rsquo;s free, and it takes about two minutes to switch on.'}, {'t': 'Open Settings and turn the text size up', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Spot on. It&rsquo;s usually under Display &mdash; the wording varies a little by phone, but it&rsquo;s always there. The single best change most people make.'}, {'t': 'Buy a new phone with a bigger screen', 'ok': False, 'why': 'No need to spend a penny &mdash; the phone you already own can show much bigger text. Try the free fix first.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128717;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 7', 'title': 'Apps and the Google Play Store, safely', 'body': '<p>Apps come from the <strong>Google Play Store</strong> &mdash; the official app shop that comes on Android phones (its icon is a colourful play-button triangle). Sticking to the Play Store is the golden rule: apps there are checked by Google, which makes it far safer than anything a website pop-up tries to offer you.</p><p>Before installing, take a quick look at two things:</p><ul><li><strong>Reviews and downloads</strong> &mdash; lots of downloads and plenty of happy reviews is a very good sign</li><li><strong>Permissions</strong> &mdash; the things an app asks to use. A crossword app asking for your contacts and messages is asking for far more than it needs, and that&rsquo;s a reason to pick a different one</li></ul><p>And here&rsquo;s the freeing part: <strong>uninstalling is easy and completely safe</strong>. On most phones you press and hold the app&rsquo;s icon and choose <strong>Uninstall</strong> (the exact steps vary a little by maker). Trying an app costs you nothing &mdash; if you don&rsquo;t like it, off it goes, and your phone is none the worse.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#128717;&#65039; In the Play Store</p>Two crossword apps look almost identical. One has thousands of cheerful reviews and asks for nothing unusual. The other has hardly any reviews and wants access to your contacts and messages.</div>', 'q': 'Which one would you install?', 'opts': [{'t': 'The well-reviewed one that asks for very little', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect judgement. Lots of happy users and modest permissions are exactly the signs to look for.'}, {'t': 'The other one &mdash; maybe it needs my contacts for a good reason', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A crossword puzzle has no business reading your contacts or messages. When an app asks for far more than it needs, choose a different one &mdash; there&rsquo;s always another.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128247;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 7', 'title': 'Photos: taking, finding, keeping', 'body': '<p>Your phone&rsquo;s camera is probably the best camera you&rsquo;ve ever owned. Open the <strong>Camera</strong> app, point, and tap the big round button &mdash; and on many phones a <strong>volume button</strong> takes the photo too, which is easier to press without wobbling.</p><p>Finding photos again is the bit that worries people, and it&rsquo;s easier than you&rsquo;d think. They all land in your photos app &mdash; usually <strong>Google Photos</strong> or an app called <strong>Gallery</strong>, depending on your phone &mdash; neatly sorted by date, newest first. Google Photos even lets you search by typing a word like &ldquo;dog&rdquo; or &ldquo;beach&rdquo;.</p><p>If your phone ever warns that <strong>storage is nearly full</strong>, don&rsquo;t start deleting in a hurry. Google Photos can back your pictures up to your Google account, and once they&rsquo;re safely backed up it offers a <strong>free up space</strong> option (at the time of writing) that removes only the copies already saved &mdash; your photos stay safe and viewable in the app.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'Your phone warns that storage is nearly full. What&rsquo;s the wisest move?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Quickly delete photos until the message goes away', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Deleting in a hurry is how treasured photos get lost. There&rsquo;s a calmer way that keeps every single picture.'}, {'t': 'Back photos up first &mdash; for example with Google Photos &mdash; then use its option to free up space', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. Back up first, then let the app clear only the copies that are already safe. Nothing precious is lost.'}, {'t': 'Ignore the warning &mdash; it&rsquo;s probably nothing', 'ok': False, 'why': 'The warning is genuine &mdash; a completely full phone can start to misbehave. But there&rsquo;s no rush: back up first, then free space safely.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128267;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 7', 'title': 'Keeping your phone healthy', 'body': '<p><strong>Updates are free, and they protect you.</strong> When your phone offers a software update, it&rsquo;s mostly delivering security repairs &mdash; think of a locksmith quietly upgrading your locks. Let it install, ideally on Wi-Fi with the phone charging, and carry on. The same goes for app updates from the Play Store.</p><p><strong>Restarting cures a surprising amount.</strong> If the phone feels slow, the keyboard lags, or an app misbehaves, switch it off and on again. It sounds far too simple to work &mdash; it genuinely works, and it&rsquo;s always the first thing to try.</p><p>And some honesty about <strong>batteries</strong>: they wear gradually with age, and that&rsquo;s normal, not a fault. Modern phones manage their own charging sensibly, so charging overnight is fine. The biggest battery user is usually the screen, so slightly lower brightness means a longer day. And if an older phone barely lasts the morning, a battery replacement is often far cheaper than a new phone.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#128276; Notification</p>System update available. Your phone will restart during installation.</div>', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the best response?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Ignore it &mdash; updates just slow old phones down', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A very common worry, but updates are mostly free security repairs &mdash; skipping them leaves the door unlocked. They&rsquo;re well worth having.'}, {'t': 'Let it install, ideally on Wi-Fi with the phone charging', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect. Updates are free and protective, and Wi-Fi plus a charger is the comfortable way to take them. Then carry on as normal.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128737;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 7', 'title': 'Phone scams and staying safe', 'body': '<p><strong>Calls from unknown numbers:</strong> you never have to answer. Let it ring &mdash; anyone genuine will leave a voicemail or try again, while scammers usually just move on. If you do answer and the caller pressures you, asks about your bank, or claims your phone or computer &ldquo;has a problem&rdquo;, simply hang up. Hanging up is never rude when someone is trying to trick you. And you can forward scam <em>texts</em> to <strong>7726</strong> &mdash; a free service that reports them straight to your mobile network.</p><p><strong>&ldquo;Your phone has a virus!&rdquo; pop-ups:</strong> while browsing the web you may meet an alarming, flashing warning urging you to tap a button or install a cleaner app. It&rsquo;s an advert dressed up as an emergency &mdash; a web page cannot actually see viruses on your phone. <strong>Don&rsquo;t tap anything on it.</strong> Close that page &mdash; or close the whole browser app and press Home &mdash; and the &ldquo;emergency&rdquo; simply vanishes.</p><p>Finally, set a <strong>screen lock</strong> &mdash; a PIN, pattern or fingerprint. If your phone is ever lost, it means whoever picks it up cannot get into your messages, photos or banking. You&rsquo;ll find it in Settings under something like <strong>Security</strong> (the wording varies by maker). A fingerprint, on phones that offer one, is both the easiest to use and the hardest to guess.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#9888;&#65039; Pop-up while browsing</p>WARNING! Your Android is infected with 5 viruses! Tap CLEAN NOW immediately, before your photos are deleted!</div>', 'q': 'What do you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Tap CLEAN NOW &mdash; it says my photos are at risk', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That panic is the whole trick &mdash; a web page can&rsquo;t see viruses on your phone. Tapping is the only thing that could cause trouble, so leave it well alone.'}, {'t': 'Don&rsquo;t tap anything &mdash; close the page (or the whole browser) and carry on', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. It&rsquo;s a fake advert dressed as an emergency. Close it, press Home, and it&rsquo;s gone &mdash; your phone was never infected.'}, {'t': 'Install the cleaner app it recommends', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Anything a pop-up pushes you to install is best avoided. If you ever want a security app, go to the Play Store yourself and pick a well-reviewed one.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#129309;', 'tag': 'Lesson 7 of 7', 'title': 'When to ask for help (and why it&rsquo;s smart)', 'body': '<p>Everyone gets stuck &mdash; everyone. The people who become confident with their phones aren&rsquo;t the ones who never hit a snag; they&rsquo;re the ones who ask. Asking is a skill, not a failing.</p><ul><li><strong>Write down what happened</strong> &mdash; what you tapped, what the screen said. Even a rough note makes helping you ten times easier</li><li><strong>Keep a little list of questions</strong> for when you next see family, a friend, or us</li><li><strong>Never let anyone rush you</strong> &mdash; and never hand over your phone or its PIN to a stranger who rings up claiming they&rsquo;ll fix it. Genuine helpers don&rsquo;t call out of the blue</li></ul><p>And remember, you have us. We&rsquo;re 365 Techies, a family firm in Bournemouth since 1995. Friendly help is one phone call away on <strong>01202 775566</strong>, and we run relaxed in-person lessons at the <strong>Kinson Community Centre</strong> in Bournemouth &mdash; bring your own phone and ask anything. There is no such thing as a silly question. We mean that.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You&rsquo;ve tried restarting, you&rsquo;re still stuck, and you&rsquo;re starting to feel a bit silly. What now?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Give up &mdash; phones clearly aren&rsquo;t for me', 'ok': False, 'why': 'They absolutely are for you &mdash; you&rsquo;ve just met a snag, and everyone does. One question to the right person usually clears it in minutes.'}, {'t': 'Note down what happened and ask someone &mdash; family, a friend, or give us a ring', 'ok': True, 'why': 'That&rsquo;s exactly it. Asking is what confident phone users do &mdash; and we&rsquo;re always at the end of the phone on 01202 775566.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': '<p>Congratulations &mdash; you&rsquo;ve finished the course, and your phone is officially tamed. If you keep just one habit, keep this one: <strong>explore without fear</strong>. You can&rsquo;t easily break anything, and the Home button (or swipe) will always bring you safely back.</p><p>And if you&rsquo;re ever stuck, friendly help is one phone call away on <strong>01202 775566</strong> &mdash; or join our relaxed in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth.</p>', 'certCourseName': 'Your Android Phone, Tamed', 'certSubline': 'Home screen confidence, essential settings, safe apps, photos, updates and phone scam awareness', 'checklist': ['Explore freely: the Home button or swipe always rescues you', 'Make the text bigger in Settings (usually under Display)', 'Install apps only from the Google Play Store', 'Check reviews and permissions before installing an app', 'Back up photos before freeing up space', 'Let free software updates install', 'Restart the phone first when anything misbehaves', 'Never tap virus warning pop-ups: close the page instead', 'Set a screen lock: a PIN or a fingerprint', 'When stuck, ask: 365 Techies is on 01202 775566'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is the course really free?', 'a': '<p>Completely free &mdash; no catch, and nothing to sign up for. We&rsquo;re a family IT firm in Bournemouth, and courses like this are our way of helping local people feel confident with technology. If you ever want more help, you know where we are.</p>'}, {'q': 'Which phones does it cover?', 'a': '<p>Any Android phone &mdash; Samsung, Google, Motorola and others. Menus and wording differ a little between makers, so we teach by what buttons <em>do</em> rather than exact taps. If you have an iPhone, Apple offers similar features, but this course is written for Android.</p>'}, {'q': 'Could I break my phone by practising?', 'a': '<p>It&rsquo;s very hard to break anything by tapping and exploring &mdash; that&rsquo;s lesson one! The Home button (or swipe) always takes you back to the start, and nothing in this course asks you to do anything risky.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I get a certificate?', 'a': '<p>Yes &mdash; when you finish, type your first name and print your 365 Techies certificate of completion. It&rsquo;s our own friendly certificate &mdash; not an accredited or formal qualification &mdash; and plenty of people pop it on the fridge.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I have to finish it in one sitting?', 'a': '<p>Not at all &mdash; go entirely at your own pace. Your progress is saved on your device, so you can close the page and pick up exactly where you left off, cup of tea in hand, another day.</p>'}, {'q': 'What if I get stuck or would rather learn with a real person?', 'a': '<p>Ring us on 01202 775566 &mdash; friendly help is one phone call away. We also run relaxed in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth, where you can bring your own phone and ask anything at all.</p>'}], 'crumbName': 'Your Android Phone, Tamed'},
{'slug': 'online-banking-safely-course', 'title': 'Free Online Banking Safety Course for Over-60s | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'A free, gentle online banking course for older beginners. Learn the golden rules that keep your money safe, at your own pace, with a certificate at the end.', 'h1': 'Online banking, <em class="grad grad--green">without the worry</em>', 'heroLede': 'Many people avoid online banking because of scare stories &mdash; this free course gives you the honest picture, and the few golden rules that genuinely keep your money safe. Work through seven short lessons at your own pace, then print your certificate with your name on it. No jargon, no rush, no catch.', 'icon': '&#127974;', 'courseTitle': 'Online Banking, Safely', 'tagline': 'Free &middot; 7 short lessons &middot; about 40 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>Welcome &mdash; and if you feel a little nervous about this one, you are in the best possible company. Online banking is the subject people most often tell us they have been avoiding, usually because of frightening stories in the news. This course was written to give you the honest picture: what online banking actually is, why your money is protected, and the small set of rules that keep it that way.</p><p>There&rsquo;s no test, no timer, and nothing you can break. Take the lessons in order or dip in and out &mdash; and if anything leaves you stuck, we&rsquo;re one phone call away on 01202 775566.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#127974;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 7', 'title': 'Why everyone keeps mentioning online banking', 'body': '<p>If your local branch has closed, you are not alone &mdash; a great many bank branches have shut across the UK in recent years, and banks now do most of their day-to-day work through the phone in your pocket or the computer on your desk. That can feel like a door closing. This course is about the door that opened instead.</p><p>Online banking simply means looking at your own bank account on a screen instead of over a counter. You can check your balance, see what has gone in and out, and pay a bill &mdash; from your armchair, at any hour, with no bus ride and no queue.</p><p>And your money is not floating loose on the internet. UK banks are strictly regulated, an official UK compensation scheme stands behind the money in your account (up to a set limit) in the unlikely event a bank itself ever failed, and if money ever left your account without your permission, your bank would normally have to put it back. Looking at your account on a screen does not make the money in it any less yours &mdash; or any less safe.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'A friend says: &ldquo;I&rsquo;d never look at my bank account online &mdash; one wrong tap and my money could vanish.&rdquo; What&rsquo;s the truth?', 'opts': [{'t': 'She&rsquo;s right &mdash; it&rsquo;s safer to avoid online banking completely', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It&rsquo;s a very understandable worry &mdash; but avoiding it doesn&rsquo;t actually make you safer. Scams nearly always arrive by phone call or text, not because someone looked at their balance. In fact, being able to check your account easily helps you spot problems sooner.'}, {'t': 'Simply looking at your account can&rsquo;t move your money anywhere &mdash; and UK accounts are protected by law', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. Checking a balance or reading a statement moves nothing at all. Money only moves when you deliberately set up a payment and confirm it &mdash; and later lessons show you how to do that safely too.'}, {'t': 'It&rsquo;s safe, but only if you buy a brand-new computer first', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Good news &mdash; no new kit needed. Any reasonably up-to-date phone, tablet or computer is fine. What keeps you safe isn&rsquo;t the age of the machine; it&rsquo;s the handful of golden rules coming up in Lesson 4.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128241;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 7', 'title': 'The app or the website?', 'body': '<p>There are two front doors to online banking: the bank&rsquo;s <strong>app</strong> (a button on your phone or tablet) and the bank&rsquo;s <strong>website</strong> (typed into a computer). Both are genuinely safe when used properly &mdash; but for most people, the app is the easier and safer way in.</p><p>Why? An app downloaded from the official store &mdash; the <strong>Google Play Store</strong> on Android phones &mdash; talks directly to your bank. There is no web address to mistype and no fake lookalike page to stumble onto. Most banking apps unlock with your fingerprint, your face or a short PIN, so there is less typing to get wrong. (Apple devices have their own equivalent app store.)</p><p>If you prefer the website, that&rsquo;s fine too: type the bank&rsquo;s address in yourself, or use a bookmark you saved earlier. The one rule that never changes: <strong>never log in through a link that arrived in a text or an email</strong> &mdash; that is exactly how fake sites catch people out.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">Text message received</p><p>&ldquo;Your banking app is out of date and will stop working. Download the new secure version here: bank-secure-update.com&rdquo;</p></div>', 'q': 'You want your bank&rsquo;s app on your Android phone. Where should it come from?', 'opts': [{'t': 'The link in the text &mdash; it sounds urgent and official', 'ok': False, 'why': 'This is exactly the trap, and it fools plenty of people &mdash; real banks don&rsquo;t send app downloads by text. No harm in having wondered, though: the fact that you paused to think is the whole skill.'}, {'t': 'The Google Play Store, searching for the bank by name', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Spot on. On Android phones, the Play Store is the official, checked source &mdash; and apps you already have normally keep themselves up to date through it, so a text like this is never genuine. Forward it free to 7726 and delete it.'}, {'t': 'Any website that offers the app as a free download', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A reasonable guess, but apps from random websites can be fakes dressed up to look real. Stick to the official store &mdash; on Android, that&rsquo;s the Google Play Store &mdash; and you sidestep the problem entirely.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#127793;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 7', 'title': 'Setting up, gently', 'body': '<p>Here is the most reassuring thing we tell people at our lessons in Kinson: <strong>you cannot break your bank account by looking at it.</strong> Tapping around, opening menus, reading screens &mdash; none of it moves a penny. Money only ever moves when you deliberately set up a payment and confirm it.</p><p>You don&rsquo;t have to set things up alone, either. Your bank will help you register &mdash; most offer help over the phone, or in a branch if you can reach one. They do this every day, for people of every age, and they are patient about it. You&rsquo;ll usually need your account details and your bank card handy, and the bank will guide you through choosing a password and getting the app working.</p><p>Then go at your own pace. Set it up one day, just have a look around the next, check your balance the day after. There are no prizes for rushing &mdash; and no penalties at all for taking your time.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You&rsquo;ve just opened your new banking app for the first time, and you&rsquo;re nervous about pressing the wrong thing. Which of these is true?', 'opts': [{'t': 'One stray tap could send your money somewhere by accident', 'ok': False, 'why': 'This is the single most common fear we hear &mdash; and happily, it&rsquo;s wrong. A payment needs several deliberate steps and a final confirmation before anything moves. Browsing, tapping and reading are completely safe.'}, {'t': 'Looking around can&rsquo;t move your money &mdash; payments always need deliberate steps and a confirmation', 'ok': True, 'why': 'That&rsquo;s it exactly. Explore as freely as you like &mdash; it&rsquo;s the best way to get comfortable. The app is a window onto your account, not a trapdoor under it.'}, {'t': 'You should only open the app when the bank telephones and tells you to', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Gently, no &mdash; in fact it&rsquo;s closer to the other way round. Your app is yours to open whenever you please. A caller instructing you to open it and do things is a warning sign we&rsquo;ll cover in the next lesson.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128274;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 7', 'title': 'The golden rules your bank lives by', 'body': '<p>These few rules are worth more than everything else in this course put together. Your bank will <strong>never</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Ask you to move money to a &ldquo;safe account&rdquo;.</strong> Safe accounts do not exist. Anyone telling you your money is at risk and must be moved is a criminal &mdash; every single time, no exceptions.</li><li><strong>Ask for your full PIN or full password.</strong> The real bank may ask for certain characters, but never the whole thing.</li><li><strong>Mind you hanging up.</strong> A genuine bank employee is entirely happy for you to put the phone down and call back on a number you trust. Only a scammer objects to that.</li></ul><p>One more rule, about <strong>one-time codes</strong> &mdash; the short codes (often six digits) your bank sends by text or shows in its app when you log in or pay. They exist for you to <em>type into a screen</em>, never to read out loud. No bank employee, no police officer, nobody genuine will ever ask you to say one down the phone. A voice asking for a code is a scammer &mdash; every single time.</p><p>Finally, remember the number <strong>159</strong>. If you ever get a call claiming to be your bank, hang up, wait a moment for the line to clear, then dial 159 &mdash; it puts you safely through to your own bank so you can check. At the time of writing, most major UK banks are connected to it.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">Phone call, 2:15pm</p><p>&ldquo;This is the fraud team at your bank. Criminals have accessed your account this afternoon. We need you to move your money to a safe account right now, while there&rsquo;s still time.&rdquo;</p></div>', 'q': 'What do you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Do as they say quickly &mdash; the fraud team knows best', 'ok': False, 'why': 'The urgency is the giveaway, and it&rsquo;s designed to be convincing &mdash; this exact call has fooled very clever people. But the rule has no exceptions: banks never, ever ask you to move money to a safe account. Only criminals do.'}, {'t': 'Hang up, wait a moment, then dial 159 to reach your real bank', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect. &ldquo;Safe accounts&rdquo; do not exist, so the call cannot be genuine &mdash; and hanging up on a real bank employee never causes offence. Dialling 159 yourself means you know exactly who you&rsquo;re talking to.'}, {'t': 'Ask the caller to prove who they are by reading out some of your details', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A fair instinct &mdash; but scammers often already know your name and some details, which is precisely what makes them sound real. The safe move is simpler and stronger: hang up and dial 159 yourself.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#9989;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 7', 'title': 'Paying someone: the name check', 'body': '<p>When you set up a payment to someone new, most UK banks now run a helpful check called <strong>Confirmation of Payee</strong>. You type in the sort code, the account number and the name of the person or company &mdash; and the bank tells you whether that name matches the real account before any money moves.</p><p>Treat the answer like a traffic light. A full match means carry on. A partial match or <strong>no match means stop</strong> &mdash; don&rsquo;t send a penny until you&rsquo;ve spoken to the person on a phone number you already know and checked the details together.</p><p>Paying someone new for the first time deserves a triple-check anyway: read the account number back digit by digit, and for a larger amount, many people send &pound;1 first and confirm it arrived before sending the rest. Sensible, not paranoid.</p><p>Worth knowing in general terms: paying by <strong>card</strong> comes with strong protections if something goes wrong, and credit cards add extra legal protection on larger purchases. A <strong>bank transfer</strong> is more like handing over cash &mdash; much harder to claw back &mdash; so transfers deserve the most care of all, especially to someone new.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">Setting up a payment</p><p>You&rsquo;re paying a gardener &pound;180 for the first time. You type in his details and your bank replies: &ldquo;The name on this account does not match the name you entered.&rdquo;</p></div>', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the wise move?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Send it anyway &mdash; banks get these checks wrong all the time', 'ok': False, 'why': 'The check is there precisely for moments like this, and it&rsquo;s usually right. A no-match doesn&rsquo;t mean disaster &mdash; but it does mean pause. One quick phone call costs nothing; a payment to the wrong account is hard to undo.'}, {'t': 'Stop, and ring the gardener on the number you already have to re-check the details', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. Often it&rsquo;s just a typo or a business trading under a different name &mdash; but sometimes it&rsquo;s a fraudster who has slipped you fake details. Either way, one phone call sorts it before any money moves.'}, {'t': 'Send &pound;1 first as a test, then send the rest', 'ok': False, 'why': 'The &pound;1 test is a lovely habit when the name matches and you just want belt and braces &mdash; but when the name doesn&rsquo;t match, even &pound;1 shouldn&rsquo;t go anywhere yet. Check with the gardener first, then test away.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#127917;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 7', 'title': 'The bank that isn&rsquo;t your bank', 'body': '<p>Bank-impersonation scams almost always arrive one of two ways: an <strong>urgent phone call</strong> (&ldquo;your account has been compromised&rdquo;) or an <strong>alarming text</strong> (&ldquo;a payment has been attempted &mdash; click here to verify&rdquo;). The costume changes; the script never does. First frighten you, then rush you.</p><p>Two tricks make these convincing. Scammers can make a call or text <em>appear</em> to come from your bank&rsquo;s real number &mdash; it&rsquo;s called spoofing, and it means the number on your screen proves nothing, even if the text lands in the same thread as genuine messages. And they may know your name or other details about you. Neither means it&rsquo;s really the bank.</p><p>Happily, the defence never changes either. Suspicious call? Hang up, wait, dial <strong>159</strong>. Suspicious text? Don&rsquo;t tap the link &mdash; forward it free to <strong>7726</strong>, then delete it. Suspicious email? Send it to <strong>report@phishing.gov.uk</strong>. And if you want the truth about your account, open your banking app yourself or call the number on the back of your card. The Take Five campaign sums it up nicely: stop, challenge, protect.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">Text message, Saturday morning</p><p>&ldquo;BANK ALERT: A payment of &pound;900 was attempted from your account. If this was NOT you, verify your details immediately: [link]&rdquo; &mdash; and it has appeared in the same thread as real texts from your bank.</p></div>', 'q': 'What do you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Tap the link straight away &mdash; &pound;900 is a lot of money', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That jolt of alarm is exactly what the message is engineered to produce &mdash; it happens to almost everyone. But real banks don&rsquo;t ask you to &ldquo;verify&rdquo; through a text link. Your own app or the number on your card will tell you the truth safely.'}, {'t': 'Don&rsquo;t tap it. Check your account through your own banking app, and forward the text free to 7726', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect on every count. Because numbers can be spoofed, even a text sitting among genuine ones proves nothing &mdash; but your own app tells you the truth in ten seconds. Forwarding to 7726 helps get the scam number shut down too.'}, {'t': 'Reply STOP so the texts stop coming', 'ok': False, 'why': 'With genuine companies STOP works a treat &mdash; but replying to a scam text just tells the criminals your number is live, so more may follow. Forward it to 7726, delete it, and check your account through your own app.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#129534;', 'tag': 'Lesson 7 of 7', 'title': 'The five-minute habit that keeps you safe', 'body': '<p>Here is online banking&rsquo;s best-kept secret: it can make you <em>safer</em> than paper statements ever did, because you can look at your account any day you like &mdash; not once a month when the envelope arrives.</p><p>Make it a little routine. Once a week or so, open the app with a cup of tea and glance down the list of payments, asking one simple question: <em>do I recognise all of these?</em> Don&rsquo;t panic over an unfamiliar shop name &mdash; companies sometimes appear under a different trading name on statements. But anything you truly can&rsquo;t place is worth a phone call.</p><p>If something really is wrong, speed is your friend. Tell your bank straight away &mdash; dial 159 or call the number on the back of your card &mdash; because banks can often stop or recover a fraudulent payment, and when you report promptly and the fraud wasn&rsquo;t your fault, you would normally be refunded. Fraud can also be reported to Action Fraud on 0300 123 2040.</p><p>And keep your phone and its apps updated &mdash; updates are free security repairs, quietly fixing the holes scammers hope to use.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">Your statement, Tuesday</p><p>&pound;34.99 to &ldquo;GRN*DIGITALSVCS&rdquo; &mdash; and you can&rsquo;t remember buying anything like that.</p></div>', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the best move?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Wait a few weeks and see whether it happens again', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Waiting feels sensible, but with fraud the clock matters &mdash; the sooner your bank knows, the more it can do, and acting quickly is what keeps you protected. One short call today beats a bigger worry next month.'}, {'t': 'Try to place the name, and if you still can&rsquo;t, call your bank today', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Just right. Quite often it turns out to be a familiar shop under an unfamiliar trading name &mdash; but if it is fraud, prompt reporting means the bank can act fast, and you&rsquo;re normally refunded when it wasn&rsquo;t your fault.'}, {'t': 'Post it on Facebook and ask if anyone recognises the company', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Friends mean well, but your bank can answer in two minutes flat &mdash; and it&rsquo;s best not to share anything from your statement publicly. Save the post; make the call.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': '<p>Well done &mdash; you&rsquo;ve finished the whole course, and you now know the rules that genuinely keep online banking safe. That honestly puts you ahead of most people of any age.</p><p>If you keep just one habit, keep this one: <strong>a real bank will never rush you.</strong> Anyone who hurries, frightens or pressures you on the phone is not your bank &mdash; so hang up, take a breath, and dial 159. And if you&rsquo;d ever like a friendly human alongside you, call us on 01202 775566, or come and see us at our in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth.</p>', 'certCourseName': 'Online Banking, Safely', 'certSubline': 'For learning the golden rules of safe online banking &mdash; official apps, one-time codes, payment name checks, 159 and spotting bank-impersonation scams', 'checklist': ['Get your banking app only from the Google Play Store on Android', 'Never log in through a link sent by text or email', 'Your bank will NEVER ask you to move money to a safe account', 'One-time codes are for typing into screens, never for reading aloud', 'Never give anyone your full PIN or full password', 'Suspicious call from the bank? Hang up, wait, then dial 159', 'Forward scam texts free to 7726, then delete them', 'Paying someone new? Check the name matches and triple-check the details', 'Glance through your payments each week and ring the bank about anything odd', 'Stuck or worried? Call 365 Techies on 01202 775566'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is online banking really safe for someone my age?', 'a': '<p>Yes &mdash; genuinely. UK banks are strictly regulated, an official compensation scheme stands behind the money in your account (up to a set limit), and payments you never authorised would normally have to be refunded. The real risk isn&rsquo;t the technology; it&rsquo;s scammers on the phone pretending to be your bank &mdash; and spotting them is exactly what this course teaches.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I need a computer, or will my phone do?', 'a': '<p>A smartphone or tablet is all you need &mdash; for most people, the bank&rsquo;s own app is the easiest and safest way in. Everything in this course works on Android phones and Windows computers; Apple devices have their own equivalents.</p>'}, {'q': 'What happens if I press the wrong button?', 'a': '<p>Nothing dramatic, we promise. Simply looking around your account can&rsquo;t move any money &mdash; a payment always needs several deliberate steps and a final confirmation. You can explore to your heart&rsquo;s content.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is this course really free? What&rsquo;s the catch?', 'a': '<p>Completely free &mdash; no card details, no trial, no catch. We&rsquo;re a family IT firm in Bournemouth, going since 1995, and we&rsquo;d simply rather local people were safe and confident. If you ever want hands-on help, we hope you&rsquo;ll think of us.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I get a certificate, and is it a qualification?', 'a': '<p>When you finish, you can type in your first name and print a 365 Techies certificate of completion. It&rsquo;s a lovely thing to keep on the fridge, though not a formal qualification &mdash; the real prize is the confidence.</p>'}, {'q': 'What if I get stuck partway through?', 'a': '<p>Help is one phone call away &mdash; ring us on 01202 775566 and a friendly human will talk it through with you. And if you&rsquo;d rather learn face to face, we run in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth.</p>'}], 'crumbName': 'Online Banking, Safely'},
{'slug': 'video-calling-course-for-beginners', 'title': 'Free Video Calling Course for Beginners | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'A free, gentle video-calling course for older beginners in the UK. Learn WhatsApp and Zoom calls step by step, fix common hiccups and print your certificate.', 'h1': 'See their faces &mdash; <em class="grad grad--green">video calling</em> made simple', 'heroLede': 'Grandchildren growing up fast, or friends on the other side of the world? This free course gently shows you how to see their faces on a video call &mdash; step by step, at your own pace, and with a printable certificate waiting at the end.', 'icon': '&#128249;', 'courseTitle': 'See Their Faces: Video Calling', 'tagline': 'Free · 6 short lessons · about 30 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>There&rsquo;s something rather wonderful about seeing a face light up when a call connects &mdash; a grandchild showing off a lost tooth, an old friend waving from their kitchen in Spain. That&rsquo;s what this little course is for.</p><p>You don&rsquo;t need any experience, and you almost certainly don&rsquo;t need to buy anything. Six short lessons, taken at whatever pace suits you &mdash; and by the end, the people you love will be one green button away.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#128241;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 6', 'title': 'You already have everything you need', 'body': '<p>Here&rsquo;s some good news to start with: if you own a smartphone, a tablet or a laptop made in the last ten years or so, it almost certainly has a camera and microphone built in. You very likely already own everything you need.</p><p>The other ingredient is an internet connection. And here&rsquo;s the honest bit about cost:</p><ul><li><strong>Over your home Wi-Fi:</strong> video calls cost nothing extra, however long you natter.</li><li><strong>Over mobile data</strong> (out and about, away from Wi-Fi): video uses up your monthly data allowance quite quickly, so the long catch-ups are best saved for home.</li></ul><p>That&rsquo;s genuinely it. No webcam to buy, no special equipment &mdash; just the device you already have, and your Wi-Fi.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'Your daughter suggests a Sunday video call. You have a smartphone and home Wi-Fi. What do you need to buy first?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Nothing at all &mdash; my phone and Wi-Fi are already everything I need', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. The camera and microphone are built in, and over home Wi-Fi the call costs nothing extra. You&rsquo;re ready to go.'}, {'t': 'A separate webcam and a proper headset', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Good instinct to come prepared &mdash; but there&rsquo;s no need. Your phone&rsquo;s built-in camera and microphone were made for exactly this. Save your money for biscuits.'}, {'t': 'A newer, more expensive phone', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Happily not! Almost any smartphone from the last decade handles video calls beautifully. The one you already have is very likely just fine.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128172;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 6', 'title': 'Your first WhatsApp video call', 'body': '<p>If your family already use WhatsApp for messages, video calling is the easiest possible next step &mdash; it lives inside the same app you already have.</p><p>Open your chat with the person, then look for the <strong>little video camera icon</strong> near the top of the screen. Its exact spot varies a little by phone and app version, but the icon is always a small camera. Tap it, and it rings them &mdash; just like a phone call, but with faces.</p><p>When someone video-calls <em>you</em>, your phone rings and shows who&rsquo;s calling. Tap or swipe the <strong>green accept button</strong>, exactly as you&rsquo;d answer any call. If the first thing you see is your own startled face, don&rsquo;t worry &mdash; that&rsquo;s just the camera waking up.</p><p>Best of all: WhatsApp calls travel over the internet, so on Wi-Fi they&rsquo;re <strong>free &mdash; even to family in Australia or Canada</strong>, however long you talk. The calls are also end-to-end encrypted &mdash; which simply means nobody in between, not even WhatsApp itself, can watch or listen in.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#128172; WhatsApp message from your son in Sydney</p>Fancy a video call on Sunday? The kids want to show you the new puppy! We&rsquo;d be on for at least an hour though&hellip;</div>', 'q': 'An hour-long video call to Australia &mdash; what will it cost you on your home Wi-Fi?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Nothing extra at all, however long we chat', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Spot on. WhatsApp calls travel over the internet, so on Wi-Fi an hour to Australia costs the same as a minute to next door: nothing extra. Enjoy the puppy!'}, {'t': 'International rates, charged by the minute like a phone call', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A very sensible worry &mdash; that&rsquo;s exactly how phone calls used to work! But WhatsApp calls go over the internet instead, so on Wi-Fi they&rsquo;re free wherever in the world your family happen to be.'}, {'t': 'It&rsquo;s free, but only for the first ten minutes', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Happily there&rsquo;s no time limit &mdash; over Wi-Fi you can chat all afternoon at no extra cost. The puppy will get bored before your Wi-Fi does.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128101;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 6', 'title': 'Zoom &mdash; joining the whole family at once', 'body': '<p>WhatsApp is lovely for one or two faces. For a whole family quiz, a church group or a club committee, many people use <strong>Zoom</strong>, which comfortably fits lots of people on screen at once.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s the part that surprises people: <strong>to join someone else&rsquo;s Zoom call, you don&rsquo;t need an account</strong>. Whoever organises the call sends you a link &mdash; by email or WhatsApp &mdash; and the link does all the work. Press it at the right time and follow the simple prompts. The very first time, your device may ask to install the free Zoom app, and it may ask permission to use your camera and microphone &mdash; both are perfectly normal, so say yes, and you&rsquo;re in.</p><p>Once you&rsquo;re in the call, tap or click the screen and the controls appear, usually along one edge: a <strong>microphone button</strong> for your voice and a <strong>camera button</strong> for your picture. Each switches on and off when pressed &mdash; a line through the icon means it&rsquo;s off.</p><p>Which brings us to the most famous sentence in video calling: <em>&ldquo;You&rsquo;re on mute!&rdquo;</em> If everyone&rsquo;s smiling but nobody can hear you, glance at the microphone button. It happens to prime ministers and grandparents alike &mdash; one tap fixes it.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#9993;&#65039; Email from your grandson</p>Hi Gran! Family quiz on Zoom, Saturday 7pm &mdash; just click this link to join. No need to set anything up!</div>', 'q': 'You&rsquo;ve never used Zoom and don&rsquo;t have an account. What do you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Press the link just before 7pm and follow the prompts &mdash; no account needed', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. The link does all the work &mdash; joining someone else&rsquo;s Zoom call needs no account and costs nothing. See you at the quiz!'}, {'t': 'Email back to say you&rsquo;ll need to set up a paid Zoom account first', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Kind of you to want to be ready &mdash; but there&rsquo;s no need. Joining a call someone else has organised is free and needs no account at all. The link really is all it takes.'}, {'t': 'Ask him to phone you instead &mdash; Zoom is only for offices', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Zoom did become famous through office meetings, but families use it every day for quizzes and birthdays. And since his link needs no account, you&rsquo;re only one press away from joining in.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128161;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 6', 'title': 'Looking and sounding your best', 'body': '<p>Three little tricks make a world of difference, and none of them costs a penny.</p><ul><li><strong>Put the light in front of you, not behind.</strong> Sit facing a window or a lamp. If the light is behind you, the camera sees only a mysterious silhouette &mdash; dramatic, but not what the grandchildren rang for.</li><li><strong>Prop the device at eye height.</strong> A phone flat on the table gives everyone a fine view up your nose. Lean it against a pile of books or a shelf so the camera looks straight at you &mdash; instantly more natural, and your arm gets a rest too.</li><li><strong>Speak normally.</strong> The microphone is designed to pick up an ordinary speaking voice from an arm&rsquo;s length away. There&rsquo;s no need to lean in and shout &mdash; just chat as you always do and you&rsquo;ll come through loud and clear.</li></ul><p>Do those three things and you&rsquo;ll look and sound better than half the people on television.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You join a call and the family say you&rsquo;re just a dark shadow against the window. What&rsquo;s the fix?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Turn round so the window light falls on your face instead of coming from behind you', 'ok': True, 'why': 'That&rsquo;s the one. Light in front, not behind &mdash; turn your chair or move the device, and you&rsquo;ll go from silhouette to star in two seconds.'}, {'t': 'Accept it &mdash; the camera must be too old', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It&rsquo;s almost never the camera! Even brand-new phones show a silhouette when a bright window sits behind you. Simply facing the light fixes it &mdash; no new equipment required.'}, {'t': 'Speak louder, so at least they can hear you clearly', 'ok': False, 'why': 'They can already hear you just fine &mdash; the trouble here is light, not sound. Face the window rather than sitting in front of it and they&rsquo;ll see you beautifully.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128260;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 6', 'title': 'When it goes wrong (and it does, for everyone)', 'body': '<p>First, the most reassuring fact in this whole course: <strong>when a video call misbehaves, it is almost never your fault</strong>, and you almost certainly haven&rsquo;t broken anything. It happens to everyone, regularly.</p><ul><li><strong>Frozen or blocky picture, robot-sounding voice:</strong> usually the Wi-Fi signal, not the device. Moving a little closer to your router (the box from your internet company, usually blinking away near the phone socket or the TV) often works wonders.</li><li><strong>Hearing your own voice echo back:</strong> a common quirk when two devices in the same house are on the same call. Turning the volume down a touch, muting one of the devices, or moving to another room usually settles it.</li><li><strong>A Zoom call that suddenly ends mid-conversation:</strong> Zoom&rsquo;s free version puts a time limit on group calls &mdash; around 40 minutes, at the time of writing. Nobody has broken anything: the organiser simply starts the call again, and everyone presses the same link.</li></ul><p>And when in doubt, use the fix the professionals use: <strong>leave the call and rejoin</strong>. Hang up, wait a moment, then call back &mdash; or press the Zoom link again. It cures most problems, nobody minds in the slightest, and &ldquo;sorry, I dropped out!&rdquo; is said on video calls every minute of every day.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#128249; Ten minutes into a lovely catch-up</p>Your niece&rsquo;s picture suddenly freezes mid-laugh, and her voice starts sounding like a robot underwater.</div>', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the best move?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Hang up, move a little closer to the router, and call back', 'ok': True, 'why': 'The classic fix. A freeze usually means the Wi-Fi hiccupped &mdash; leaving and rejoining, ideally a bit nearer the router, cures it far more often than not.'}, {'t': 'Keep talking to the frozen picture and hope it sorts itself out', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It occasionally recovers by itself, but you could be chatting away to a frozen photograph for quite a while! Leaving and rejoining is much quicker &mdash; and your niece will still be there.'}, {'t': 'Switch everything off for the day &mdash; you must have broken something', 'ok': False, 'why': 'You haven&rsquo;t broken a thing &mdash; promise. Frozen pictures are nearly always the Wi-Fi having a moment, and rejoining the call fixes it. No need to give up on the afternoon.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128737;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 6', 'title': 'Staying safe on camera', 'body': '<p>Video calling is one of the safest, happiest things you can do online &mdash; you can see exactly who you&rsquo;re talking to. Three sensible rules keep it that way.</p><ul><li><strong>Unexpected video call from a stranger? Decline it</strong> &mdash; just as you would a knock at the door from someone you don&rsquo;t know. Anyone genuine will message first or try again. Declining is never rude.</li><li><strong>Never do or show anything private on camera with someone you only know online.</strong> Sadly, some fraudsters spend weeks being charming precisely to coax people into intimate moments on video &mdash; then record them and demand money. It targets kind, trusting people of every age. The defence is simple: keep calls with online-only acquaintances strictly friendly, and calmly end any call that turns personal. If this ever happens to someone you know, the criminal is the only one who should feel ashamed &mdash; report it to Action Fraud on 0300 123 2040.</li><li><strong>Let video work for you.</strong> One common trick &mdash; often called the &ldquo;Hi Mum&rdquo; scam &mdash; is a message from an unknown number claiming to be family on a &ldquo;new phone&rdquo;, usually asking for money; fraudsters can even fake a relative&rsquo;s voice on a phone call these days. A video call where you can see the face settles it in seconds &mdash; and agreeing a family codeword adds an extra lock.</li></ul><p>One last note: if your family all use Apple devices, they may mention <strong>FaceTime</strong>, Apple&rsquo;s own video-calling app. Everything in this course &mdash; the lighting, the eye height, the leave-and-rejoin trick, these safety rules &mdash; applies there just the same.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#128249; Video call request</p>Someone you met on a friendship website a few weeks ago has been wonderfully attentive. Tonight they ask for a video call &ldquo;somewhere private&rdquo;, to be kept &ldquo;just between us&rdquo;.</div>', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the wise response?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Politely decline &mdash; keep calls with online-only friends strictly friendly, and never do anything on camera you wouldn&rsquo;t want recorded', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Wise, and kind to yourself. &ldquo;Private&rdquo; and &ldquo;just between us&rdquo; are exactly the phrases this scam leans on. A genuine friend will happily keep things ordinary &mdash; and you&rsquo;ve lost nothing by saying no.'}, {'t': 'Go along with it &mdash; video calls can&rsquo;t be recorded anyway', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Video calls sadly can be recorded, and that&rsquo;s precisely how this scam works. Nothing to feel silly about &mdash; the request itself is the warning sign, and declining is the confident move.'}, {'t': 'Agree, but ask them lots of questions first to make sure they&rsquo;re genuine', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A sensible instinct to check &mdash; but a practised fraudster answers questions smoothly. The safer rule is simpler: with someone you only know online, keep every call strictly friendly, whatever they say.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': '<p>Well done &mdash; truly. You&rsquo;ve gone from &ldquo;where&rsquo;s the camera?&rdquo; to knowing WhatsApp calls, Zoom links, the lighting tricks, the quick fixes and the safety rules that keep it all happy. The faces you love are now one green button away.</p><p>If you keep just one habit from this course, make it this: <strong>when a call misbehaves, don&rsquo;t panic &mdash; leave and rejoin.</strong> And if you&rsquo;re ever stuck, friendly local help is one phone call away on 01202 775566, or come and practise with us in person at our lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth.</p>', 'certCourseName': 'See Their Faces: Video Calling', 'certSubline': 'WhatsApp and Zoom video calls, looking your best on camera, quick fixes and video-call safety', 'checklist': ['Use your home Wi-Fi for video calls - they cost nothing extra', 'WhatsApp: open the chat, then tap the small video camera icon', 'Answer a video call with the green button, just like any phone call', 'Zoom: press the link you were sent - no account needed to join', 'Nobody can hear you? Check the microphone button is not on mute', 'Face the light - never sit with a bright window behind you', 'Prop the device at eye height - a pile of books works well', 'Frozen picture or robot voice? Leave the call and rejoin', 'Decline unexpected video calls from strangers', 'Never do anything private on camera - video calls can be recorded'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Do video calls really cost nothing?', 'a': '<p>Over your home Wi-Fi, yes &mdash; video calls cost nothing extra, wherever in the world the other person is. WhatsApp calls have no time limit at all, and while Zoom&rsquo;s free version does limit group calls (around 40 minutes at the time of writing), restarting costs nothing either &mdash; you just press the link again. The one thing to watch is mobile data: away from Wi-Fi, video eats into your monthly allowance quickly, so save the long catch-ups for home.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I need to buy a webcam or any special equipment?', 'a': '<p>Almost certainly not. Any reasonably recent smartphone, tablet or laptop has a camera and microphone built in, and they were made for exactly this. Start with what you already own &mdash; very few people ever need anything more.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I need a Zoom account to join the family quiz?', 'a': '<p>No. Joining a call that someone else has organised needs no account and costs nothing &mdash; the link they send you does all the work. You&rsquo;d only ever need an account if you wanted to organise calls yourself.</p>'}, {'q': 'My family all have iPhones and use FaceTime. Does this course still help?', 'a': '<p>Yes. FaceTime is Apple&rsquo;s video-calling app and works a little differently, but everything here about lighting, eye height, muting, fixing frozen calls and staying safe applies just the same. WhatsApp also works on both Android phones and iPhones, so it makes a lovely meeting point for mixed families.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is the course really free, and do I get a certificate?', 'a': '<p>Completely free &mdash; no account, no card details, nothing to install. At the end you can print a 365 Techies certificate of completion with your name on it. It&rsquo;s our way of saying well done rather than a formal qualification, but it looks rather smart on the fridge.</p>'}, {'q': 'What if I get stuck partway through?', 'a': '<p>Then you&rsquo;re exactly the person we made this for. Ring us on 01202 775566 and a friendly local person will help &mdash; or come along to our in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth and we&rsquo;ll practise a video call together.</p>'}], 'crumbName': 'See Their Faces: Video Calling'},
{'slug': 'computer-basics-course', 'title': 'Free Computer Basics Course for Beginners &amp; Over 60s', 'metaDesc': 'A free, gentle computer basics course for older beginners. Nine short lessons, a printable certificate and no jargon, from a family-run Bournemouth IT firm.', 'h1': 'Computer Basics, <em class="grad grad--green">Without the Fear</em>', 'heroLede': 'A free, gentle course for anyone who has ever felt the computer was in charge. Nine short lessons at your own pace, plain English throughout, and a printable certificate with your name on it at the end &mdash; from the family-run team at 365 Techies in Bournemouth, helping people with computers since 1995.', 'icon': '&#128187;', 'courseTitle': 'Computer Basics, Without the Fear', 'tagline': 'Free · 9 short lessons · about 50 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>If you have ever hovered over the mouse, afraid the wrong click might ruin everything &mdash; this course was written for you. It starts from the very beginning, explains every word it uses, and never assumes you already know something.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s how it works: nine short lessons, each ending with one gentle &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; question. There are no scores, no time limits and nobody watching &mdash; the questions simply help the ideas settle. Read at your own pace, reread anything you like, and when you finish there&rsquo;s a certificate to print with your name on it, plus a one-page checklist to keep beside the computer.</p><p>The course is written for Windows computers, with the odd note about Android phones. If you use an Apple device instead, the same ideas apply &mdash; though the buttons carry different names, and we don&rsquo;t walk through Apple screens step by step here. Screens vary from machine to machine and change over time, so we describe buttons by their names and jobs, never by exact position. Ready? Lesson one contains the best news in all of computing.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#128170;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 9', 'title': 'You Cannot Easily Break It', 'body': '<p>Let&rsquo;s begin with the single most useful thing anyone can tell you about computers: <strong>you are very unlikely to break one by pressing the wrong button.</strong></p><p>It doesn&rsquo;t feel that way, we know. Many people hover over the mouse worried that one wrong click will wipe everything or cost a fortune to repair. But modern computers are built with mistakes in mind. Click the wrong thing and the usual result is simply that something opens which you didn&rsquo;t want &mdash; and you can close it again. No harm done.</p><p>Three comforting truths to hold on to:</p><ul><li><strong>Restarting cures a remarkable amount.</strong> If the computer is slow, stuck or behaving oddly, turning it off and on again properly is genuinely the first thing professionals try &mdash; because it works so often.</li><li><strong>The worst realistic cases are fixable.</strong> Even the rare, scary-sounding problems can almost always be put right by someone who does this for a living. In thirty years we have met very few genuinely unfixable mistakes &mdash; and none of them were caused by a beginner clicking the wrong button.</li><li><strong>There is no such thing as a silly question.</strong> Everyone who is good with computers was once baffled by them. Asking is how they got good.</li></ul><p>So give yourself permission to click around, open things and explore. Screens vary from computer to computer and change over time, so yours may not look exactly like anyone else&rsquo;s &mdash; and that&rsquo;s fine. Curiosity is how confidence grows &mdash; and the handful of moments that do deserve a pause (they mostly involve strangers asking for money or phone calls) are covered in Lessons 5 and 6.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You click something by accident and a window you don&rsquo;t recognise fills the screen. Your heart sinks.', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the wisest way to see it?', 'opts': [{'t': 'I&rsquo;ve broken something &mdash; I&rsquo;d better not touch the computer again today', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That feeling is completely natural, but an unexpected window almost never means damage. Closing it &mdash; usually with the X in its corner &mdash; puts everything back as it was.'}, {'t': 'Nothing is broken &mdash; I can close the window and carry on', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. A stray click usually just opens something you didn&rsquo;t want. Close it and carry on &mdash; no harm done, and you handled it calmly.'}, {'t': 'I should switch the computer off at the wall to be safe', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A kind instinct, but the wall switch is the one thing best avoided &mdash; it doesn&rsquo;t give the computer time to tidy up. Simply closing the window is gentler and does the job.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128193;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 9', 'title': 'The Desktop: Your Filing Cabinet', 'body': '<p>The screen you see when the computer has finished starting up is called the <strong>desktop</strong> &mdash; and it really is meant to work like the top of a desk. Small pictures (called icons) sit on it like items on a real desk, and you open one by clicking it twice in quick succession.</p><p>Behind the desktop, the computer stores everything in <strong>files</strong> and <strong>folders</strong>, and the old filing-cabinet picture is genuinely the best way to think of it:</p><ul><li>A <strong>file</strong> is a single item &mdash; one letter, one photo, one recipe.</li><li>A <strong>folder</strong> is a drawer that holds files (and can hold other folders, like dividers inside a drawer).</li></ul><p>Windows gives you a few ready-made drawers, and knowing them solves most &ldquo;where did it go?&rdquo; mysteries:</p><ul><li><strong>Documents</strong> &mdash; letters, forms and anything you type usually go here.</li><li><strong>Pictures</strong> &mdash; photos and images.</li><li><strong>Downloads</strong> &mdash; anything you fetch from the internet lands here. (More on this surprisingly important drawer in Lesson 4.)</li></ul><p>To look inside the cabinet, open <strong>File Explorer</strong> &mdash; its icon looks like a small yellow folder, usually found on the bar along the bottom of the screen. Exactly where things sit varies between computers and versions, but the folders themselves are the same idea everywhere.</p><p>When you save something, the computer is simply asking: &ldquo;which drawer shall I put this in?&rdquo; Nothing vanishes when you close a saved file &mdash; it just goes back into its drawer, waiting for you.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'Yesterday you typed a letter to your insurance company and saved it. Today you want to read it again, but the screen where you typed it is long gone.', 'q': 'Where has the letter most likely gone?', 'opts': [{'t': 'It&rsquo;s sitting safely in the Documents folder, waiting for me', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Spot on. Saved work doesn&rsquo;t evaporate when you close it &mdash; it goes back into its drawer. Documents is the first drawer to check for anything you typed.'}, {'t': 'It disappeared when I closed it &mdash; I&rsquo;ll have to type it again', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A very common worry, but happily wrong &mdash; saving means the computer has kept it. Open the Documents folder and it should be there, exactly as you left it.'}, {'t': 'It could be anywhere &mdash; computers scatter things at random', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It can certainly feel that way! But computers are creatures of habit: typed work almost always goes to Documents unless you chose somewhere else.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#127760;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 9', 'title': 'The Internet and the Computer Are Different Things', 'body': '<p>Here&rsquo;s a distinction that clears up a great deal of confusion: <strong>the computer and the internet are two different things.</strong></p><p>The computer is the machine on your desk. It can do plenty on its own &mdash; write letters, look at photos, play patience &mdash; with no internet at all. The internet is more like the telephone network: a connection that lets your computer reach the outside world. When the internet is down, your computer isn&rsquo;t broken; it&rsquo;s just temporarily cut off, like a phone with no dial tone.</p><p>To visit the internet you use a program called a <strong>browser</strong>. Common ones are Microsoft Edge and Google Chrome &mdash; different makes of the same kind of vehicle. Whichever you have, it has a long box near the top called the <strong>address bar</strong>, and this is where a little knowledge goes a long way:</p><ul><li><strong>Type an address</strong> (like <strong>nhs.uk</strong>) and you go straight to that exact place &mdash; like dialling a phone number.</li><li><strong>Type ordinary words</strong> (like &ldquo;pharmacy near me&rdquo;) and you are not going anywhere yet &mdash; you are asking a search engine, usually Google, for suggestions. It answers with a list, and you choose.</li></ul><p>One thing worth knowing about that list: the entries at the top are often paid adverts, marked with a small word such as <strong>&ldquo;Sponsored&rdquo;</strong> or &ldquo;Ad&rdquo;. They paid to be first; that doesn&rsquo;t make them best. Let your eye slide past them to the ordinary results below.</p><p>So: when you know the address, type the address. When you don&rsquo;t, search &mdash; then choose slowly, and skip the sponsored entries.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You want your bank&rsquo;s website. You search for the bank&rsquo;s name, and the top of the list shows two entries marked &lsquo;Sponsored&rsquo;.', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the careful move?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Click the very top entry &mdash; Google always puts the right one first', 'ok': False, 'why': 'An easy assumption to make &mdash; but the top spots are often bought, not earned. The word &lsquo;Sponsored&rsquo; is your cue that someone paid to be there.'}, {'t': 'Give up on the internet and drive to the branch', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Always an option, but there&rsquo;s no need! You&rsquo;re one careful choice away &mdash; the skill is simply knowing which entries to skip.'}, {'t': 'Skip the &lsquo;Sponsored&rsquo; entries and pick the bank&rsquo;s own address below &mdash; or type the address directly next time', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly the habit to build. Sponsored entries are adverts; the bank&rsquo;s real address in the ordinary results &mdash; or typed straight into the address bar &mdash; takes you where you actually meant to go.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128190;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 9', 'title': 'Saving, Finding and Never Losing Your Work', 'body': '<p>Two habits banish nearly all lost-work heartache: <strong>save early</strong>, and <strong>know your drawers</strong>.</p><p><strong>Save early, save often.</strong> When you start typing something that matters, save it within the first minute &mdash; don&rsquo;t wait until the end. Look for &ldquo;Save&rdquo; in the program&rsquo;s menus, or hold the <strong>Ctrl</strong> key and tap <strong>S</strong> &mdash; a keyboard shortcut that has meant &ldquo;save&rdquo; for decades. The first time you save, the computer asks two things: what shall I call it, and which drawer shall it go in? Give it a name your future self will recognise &mdash; &ldquo;Letter to council about bins&rdquo; beats &ldquo;Document1&rdquo; every time.</p><p><strong>The Downloads mystery, solved.</strong> When you fetch anything from the internet &mdash; an attachment, a form, a photo &mdash; it almost never lands on the desktop, and it has not vanished. Unless you chose a different drawer, it goes quietly into the <strong>Downloads</strong> folder and waits. If you have ever downloaded something three times because it &ldquo;didn&rsquo;t work&rdquo;, all three copies are probably sitting in Downloads right now. Open File Explorer, click Downloads, and there they&rsquo;ll be.</p><p><strong>When you truly can&rsquo;t find something</strong>, the computer will look for you. Click the Start button (or the search box beside it) and simply type part of the file&rsquo;s name &mdash; the computer hunts through the whole cabinet and offers matches. Screens vary between versions, but a search box is always there somewhere.</p><p>Files very rarely vanish. Nine times out of ten they are exactly where they were put &mdash; just in a different drawer from the one being searched.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You download a form from your energy company&rsquo;s website. The website says the download is complete &mdash; but nothing new has appeared on the desktop.', 'q': 'Where&rsquo;s the form?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Sitting quietly in the Downloads folder &mdash; that&rsquo;s where downloaded things usually wait', 'ok': True, 'why': 'That&rsquo;s it exactly. Downloads don&rsquo;t announce themselves on the desktop; they file themselves in the Downloads drawer. Open File Explorer, click Downloads, and your form will almost certainly be waiting.'}, {'t': 'The download failed &mdash; try it another four or five times', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Understandable &mdash; but if the website said complete, it almost certainly is. Repeat downloads just make extra copies. Check the Downloads folder first; the original is very likely there.'}, {'t': 'It&rsquo;s lost inside the machine and only an engineer can find it', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It really isn&rsquo;t lost &mdash; it&rsquo;s filed. The Downloads folder is the standard drawer for anything fetched from the internet, and it&rsquo;s one click away in File Explorer.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128194;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 9', 'title': 'Folders That Make Sense', 'body': '<p>Last lesson you learned the drawers: Documents, Pictures, Downloads. This one is about tidying inside them &mdash; because a Documents folder with three hundred loose files is where letters go to hide.</p><p><strong>A folder is just a named box.</strong> Open <strong>File Explorer</strong> (the yellow folder on the taskbar) and click <strong>Documents</strong>. Look for the <strong>New</strong> button near the top of the window and choose <strong>Folder</strong>. If you can&rsquo;t see it, right-click on an empty patch of white space and choose New, then Folder. A folder appears, waiting for a name &mdash; type one and press Enter.</p><p><strong>Name folders by topic, not by date.</strong> Council, Health, Family, Holidays, Money. Five or six broad topics beat fifty narrow ones, and two levels deep is plenty &mdash; Health, then Hospital letters inside it. If you find yourself making a folder inside a folder inside a folder, stop: you&rsquo;ll never find it again.</p><p><strong>Moving a file into a folder.</strong> Point at the file, hold the left mouse button down, drag it onto the folder until the folder highlights, then let go. If dragging feels fiddly, click the file once, hold <strong>Ctrl</strong> and tap <strong>X</strong> (cut), open the folder, then hold Ctrl and tap <strong>V</strong> (paste). The file simply moves. Nothing is copied twice and nothing is lost.</p><p><strong>Can&rsquo;t find something?</strong> Every File Explorer window has a search box in its top corner. Type a word from the file&rsquo;s name and it appears. The <strong>Sort</strong> button lines files up by date, so the newest is always at the top.</p><p><strong>Deleted by mistake?</strong> It isn&rsquo;t gone. Deleted files wait in the <strong>Recycle Bin</strong> on the desktop until you empty it. Open it, right-click the file, choose <strong>Restore</strong>, and it goes straight back where it came from.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'Your Documents folder has grown into a long list of letters, forms and photos from the last three years. You want to find the letter from the hospital without scrolling through all of it.', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the tidiest fix?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Make a few broad topic folders &mdash; Health, Council, Family &mdash; and move the files into them', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. A handful of topic folders, no more than two levels deep, is the system that still works in three years&rsquo; time. The hospital letters go in Health, and the next one you save goes straight there.'}, {'t': 'Make a new folder for every single letter, named with its full date', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That trades one long list for another. Folders help when there are fewer of them than files &mdash; group by topic, and let the Sort button handle the dates.'}, {'t': 'Delete everything older than a year so the list is shorter', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Please don&rsquo;t &mdash; hospital and council letters have a habit of being needed years later. Filing takes a minute; replacing a deleted letter can take weeks.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128424;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 9', 'title': 'Printing, Without the Panic', 'body': '<p>Printing goes wrong for almost everyone at some point, and it&rsquo;s nearly always one of three small things: the wrong printer chosen, the printer asleep or switched off, or the wrong pages. None of them is your fault.</p><p><strong>The universal print command.</strong> Whatever you&rsquo;re looking at &mdash; a letter, a web page, an email, a photo &mdash; hold the <strong>Ctrl</strong> key and tap <strong>P</strong>. A print window appears. (You&rsquo;ll also find Print in the program&rsquo;s File menu, or behind the three-dots or three-lines menu in a web browser.) Nothing prints until you click the <strong>Print</strong> button in that window, so it&rsquo;s safe to open it and just look.</p><p><strong>Three things to check in that window.</strong> First, the <strong>Printer</strong>: the box at the top should show your printer&rsquo;s name, not &ldquo;Microsoft Print to PDF&rdquo; or &ldquo;Fax&rdquo; &mdash; those are pretend printers that make files instead of paper. Second, <strong>Pages</strong>: choose &ldquo;All&rdquo;, or type the page numbers you want, such as 1-2, so a web page doesn&rsquo;t print twelve sheets of adverts. Third, <strong>Copies</strong>: usually 1.</p><p><strong>Nothing came out?</strong> Look at the printer before the computer. Is it switched on, with a light showing? Is there paper, and is there a message on its little screen? A wireless printer must be on the same Wi-Fi as the computer &mdash; if the router was restarted recently, turning the printer off and on again usually reconnects it. Then wait a moment before printing again: jobs sometimes queue up and all appear at once.</p><p><strong>The pretend printer is useful too.</strong> Choosing <strong>Microsoft Print to PDF</strong> instead of your printer saves whatever you&rsquo;re looking at as a PDF file &mdash; a snapshot anyone can open &mdash; which is handy for keeping a receipt or an order confirmation without using paper. It asks where to save; Documents is fine.</p><p>If the printer refuses no matter what, that&rsquo;s a job for a helper, not a reason to feel foolish. Printers are the most temperamental things in the house.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You hold Ctrl and tap P on a letter, click Print, and nothing comes out. Thinking back, the print window said &ldquo;Microsoft Print to PDF&rdquo; at the top, and after a moment it asked where to save a file.', 'q': 'What went wrong?', 'opts': [{'t': 'The pretend printer was selected &mdash; choose your real printer&rsquo;s name in the Printer box and print again', 'ok': True, 'why': 'That&rsquo;s it. Microsoft Print to PDF makes a file rather than paper. Pick your printer&rsquo;s name from the list at the top of the print window and the letter will come out. The PDF it offered to save is harmless &mdash; and sometimes useful.'}, {'t': 'The printer is broken and needs replacing', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Almost certainly not &mdash; the printer was never asked to do anything. The computer sent the letter to a pretend printer that makes files. Choose your real printer&rsquo;s name and try again.'}, {'t': 'Click Print another five times to make it go', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Each click would create another PDF file, not a printout. The fix is at the top of the print window: choose your printer&rsquo;s name, then click Print once.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128260;', 'tag': 'Lesson 7 of 9', 'title': 'Updates, and Switching Off Properly', 'body': '<p>Few messages cause more suspicion than &ldquo;Updates are available&rdquo;. It arrives uninvited, interrupts what you were doing, and sounds vaguely like a sales pitch. So let&rsquo;s set the record straight.</p><p><strong>Updates are protection, not a sales pitch.</strong> The updates your Windows computer or Android phone offers you itself, through its own Settings, are free &mdash; the manufacturer repairing weaknesses that criminals have learned to exploit, plus the odd improvement. Skipping them is like leaving a broken window latch unrepaired because the repairman&rsquo;s knock interrupted lunch. And you don&rsquo;t need to drop everything: finish what you&rsquo;re doing, then let the update run &mdash; overnight suits many people.</p><p>One important flip side: real updates come from the computer itself, quietly, through its own Settings. So <strong>an &ldquo;update&rdquo; that springs up while you&rsquo;re browsing the internet and demands payment, card details or a phone call is fake</strong> &mdash; a pop-up (a box that appears uninvited on a website) dressed up to look official. A real update never asks you to ring anyone.</p><p><strong>Switching off properly matters too.</strong> When you finish for the day, use the computer&rsquo;s own power option: click the Start button, choose the power symbol, then &ldquo;Shut down&rdquo;. This gives the computer a moment to file its papers and lock up. Switching off at the wall is the equivalent of shoving everything into a drawer mid-sentence &mdash; it usually survives, but it&rsquo;s one of the few habits that genuinely can cause trouble. If it offers &ldquo;Update and shut down&rdquo;, that&rsquo;s normal and good &mdash; let it.</p><p>And those &ldquo;Do you want to allow&hellip;?&rdquo; questions the computer asks from time to time? It&rsquo;s simply checking you meant to do what&rsquo;s happening. Read them slowly &mdash; if you started it, carry on; if it appeared out of nowhere, saying no is always safe.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You&rsquo;re halfway through writing an email when a message appears: &lsquo;Updates are available. Restart to install.&rsquo;', 'q': 'What do you make of it?', 'opts': [{'t': 'It&rsquo;s a ploy to sell me something &mdash; ignore it permanently', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A healthy instinct to question things! But updates offered by Windows itself are free and protective. The ones to distrust are &lsquo;updates&rsquo; that pop up on websites asking for money or a phone call &mdash; those are fakes.'}, {'t': 'It&rsquo;s genuine housekeeping &mdash; finish my email, then let it update', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect judgement. No need to drop everything, and no need to fear it: finish what you&rsquo;re doing, then let the update do its free, protective work.'}, {'t': 'Restart instantly &mdash; losing my half-written email is the price of safety', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Your safety instinct is admirable, but there&rsquo;s no such rush. Updates wait patiently. Finish and send the email first &mdash; safety and your afternoon can both survive.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#129520;', 'tag': 'Lesson 8 of 9', 'title': 'When Something Goes Wrong', 'body': '<p>Sooner or later a grey box will pop up with a message you didn&rsquo;t ask for. Here is the reframe that changes everything: <strong>an error message is a note, not an accusation.</strong> The computer is not telling you off. It is a machine leaving a message: &ldquo;something didn&rsquo;t work, and here&rsquo;s roughly why&rdquo;.</p><p>Most error messages, read slowly, turn out to say something quite mundane: a program is busy, a file is already open, the printer isn&rsquo;t switched on, the internet has hiccupped. The words may be clumsy, but they are information &mdash; and information is on your side.</p><p>A calm little routine for when one appears:</p><ul><li><strong>1. Breathe, and read it slowly.</strong> Twice if needed. There is no countdown; it will wait.</li><li><strong>2. Write down what it says</strong> &mdash; or photograph the screen with your phone. The exact words are gold dust to whoever helps you.</li><li><strong>3. Close it and try once more.</strong> Plenty of errors are one-off hiccups that never return.</li><li><strong>4. Restart the computer.</strong> The famous cure, and famous because it works: restarting clears the tangle behind a great many errors.</li><li><strong>5. Still stuck? Ask someone</strong> &mdash; a family member, or a firm like us. With the message written down, most problems are quick to diagnose.</li></ul><p>What that routine never includes: panicking, assuming it&rsquo;s your fault, or ringing a number that appears in a scary pop-up. A real error message from your own computer never asks you to phone anyone &mdash; a &ldquo;warning&rdquo; with a phone number in it is a scam, however loud it shouts.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'Mid-morning, a box appears on the screen: &lsquo;The program is not responding.&rsquo; Your finger hovers over the mouse.', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the best first move?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Click anything and everything to make the box go away', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Tempting &mdash; boxes like this feel like wasps! But clicking blind means missing the one thing the box offers: information. A slow read first costs nothing.'}, {'t': 'Switch off at the wall &mdash; the computer has clearly failed', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It hasn&rsquo;t failed &mdash; one program is just stuck, which happens to everyone. The wall switch is the harshest tool in the box; a calm read, then a proper restart if needed, is far kinder.'}, {'t': 'Take a breath and read it slowly &mdash; it&rsquo;s a note, not an accusation', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. Read it, note what it says, then close it or restart. That calm routine handles the vast majority of everyday errors &mdash; and it means you stay in charge, not the box.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#11088;', 'tag': 'Lesson 9 of 9', 'title': 'Small Wins to Try Today', 'body': '<p>You&rsquo;ve done the thinking &mdash; now for some quick, satisfying wins. Each of these takes a few minutes, makes the computer nicer to live with, and can&rsquo;t hurt anything. Screens vary between computers and versions, so treat these as directions to a destination rather than exact turnings.</p><p><strong>1. Make the text bigger.</strong> If you find yourself squinting, the computer should change &mdash; not your eyes. Open Settings (the little cog, findable from the Start button), look for the accessibility or ease-of-access section, and find the text size control &mdash; a slider that makes writing bigger everywhere. Set it wherever reading feels effortless. There are no prizes for struggling.</p><p><strong>2. Zoom any webpage instantly.</strong> In your browser, hold the <strong>Ctrl</strong> key and tap the <strong>plus</strong> key &mdash; the page grows. Ctrl and the <strong>minus</strong> key shrinks it back. This works on practically every website, and it&rsquo;s wonderfully undoable.</p><p><strong>3. Pin your favourite websites.</strong> When you&rsquo;re on a page you visit often, look for the <strong>star</strong> near the address bar and click it &mdash; the page joins your favourites (sometimes called bookmarks), one click away forever after. No more remembering addresses.</p><p><strong>4. Pin your favourite programs.</strong> Press the mouse&rsquo;s right button on a program&rsquo;s icon and look for an option to <strong>pin</strong> it to the bar along the bottom of the screen. Your daily essentials, always within reach.</p><p>If you use an Apple computer or iPad instead, similar options exist for each of these &mdash; the buttons carry different names, and we don&rsquo;t cover Apple screens step by step here.</p><p>Small adjustments like these are the real secret of comfortable computing: the machine bending to fit you, one notch at a time.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'Your neighbour admits she can barely read the words on her screen, but says she doesn&rsquo;t want to &lsquo;fiddle with the settings&rsquo; in case she spoils something.', 'q': 'What would you tell her?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Settings like text size are safe to change and easy to change back &mdash; the computer should fit her, not the other way round', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Beautifully put. Comfort settings are made to be adjusted &mdash; nothing about them is fragile, and every one of them can be undone. That&rsquo;s a lesson worth passing over the fence.'}, {'t': 'She&rsquo;s right &mdash; settings are best left exactly as they came', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A common belief, but settings exist precisely so people can change them. Text size in particular is completely safe &mdash; and instantly reversible if she prefers how it was.'}, {'t': 'She should put up with it &mdash; screens are just hard to read at our age', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Not at all! Hard-to-read text is a settings problem, not an age problem. Two minutes with the text size slider and her screen can suit her eyes perfectly.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': 'That&rsquo;s the whole course &mdash; and it&rsquo;s no small thing. You now understand your computer better than most people ever bother to: what it can&rsquo;t hurt you to try, where your things live, and exactly what to do when it grumbles. Print your certificate, pop the checklist somewhere near the computer, and be a little proud of yourself. And remember: if anything ever has you stuck, help is one phone call away on 01202 775566 &mdash; and we run friendly in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth. No question is too small.', 'certCourseName': 'Computer Basics, Without the Fear', 'certSubline': 'has completed all nine lessons of this free course from 365 Techies of Bournemouth &mdash; family-run and helping people with computers since 1995', 'checklist': ['You cannot easily break it. A wrong click usually just opens something &mdash; close it and carry on.', 'Restart first. Turning it off and on again properly genuinely cures a huge share of everyday problems.', 'Know your drawers: typed work lives in Documents, photos in Pictures, and anything from the internet waits in Downloads.', 'Typing an address (like nhs.uk) goes straight there; typing words asks a search engine, usually Google. Skip results marked &lsquo;Sponsored&rsquo;.', 'Save early, and name files so your future self recognises them &mdash; &ldquo;Letter to council about bins&rdquo;, not &ldquo;Document1&rdquo;.', 'Tidy with a few broad topic folders &mdash; Council, Health, Family &mdash; never more than two levels deep. Deleted by mistake? It&rsquo;s waiting in the Recycle Bin.', 'Printing: hold Ctrl and tap P, check the Printer box shows your printer&rsquo;s name (not &ldquo;Microsoft Print to PDF&rdquo;), choose the pages you want, then click Print once.', 'Updates offered through the computer&rsquo;s own Settings are free protection &mdash; let them run. An &lsquo;update&rsquo; popping up on a website wanting money or a phone call is fake.', 'Shut down with the Start button&rsquo;s power option, not the wall switch.', 'An error message is a note, not an accusation. Read it slowly, write down what it says, then close it or restart.', 'A pop-up with a phone number in it is a scam, however loud it shouts. Real error messages never ask you to ring anyone.', 'Stuck? Help is one phone call away: 365 Techies, 01202 775566 &mdash; no question is too small.'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is this course really free?', 'a': 'Completely. No payment details, no catch and no time limit. We&rsquo;re a family-run IT firm in Bournemouth, and courses like this are our way of helping local people feel confident with technology.'}, {'q': 'I&rsquo;ve never really used a computer &mdash; is this too advanced for me?', 'a': 'Not at all &mdash; this is the course written precisely for you. It assumes nothing, explains every term it uses, and moves at whatever pace you set. You can reread any lesson as many times as you like, and nobody is watching or marking you.'}, {'q': 'Do I get a certificate?', 'a': 'Yes &mdash; when you finish, you can print a 365 Techies certificate of completion with your name on it. It&rsquo;s our own certificate rather than a formal qualification, but plenty of people frame it, and it&rsquo;s lovely proof of a real achievement.'}, {'q': 'Will this work on my computer?', 'a': 'The course is written for Windows computers, and the ideas &mdash; folders, browsers, updates, error messages &mdash; apply almost everywhere, including Android phones and tablets. If you use an Apple device, the same ideas apply and similar options exist, though the buttons carry different names and we don&rsquo;t walk through Apple screens step by step. Screens also vary between versions, so we describe buttons by name and purpose rather than exact position.'}, {'q': 'What if I get stuck partway through?', 'a': 'Take your time &mdash; there&rsquo;s no clock, and the course will wait for you. If something has you truly stuck, help is one phone call away on 01202 775566, and we also run friendly in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth.'}, {'q': 'Can I honestly not break the computer by pressing a wrong button?', 'a': 'Honestly, it&rsquo;s remarkably hard. The realistic worst cases &mdash; a strange window, a stuck program &mdash; are cured by closing things or restarting. The few genuinely risky habits, like switching off at the wall or ringing numbers from scary pop-ups, are covered in the course, so you&rsquo;ll know exactly what to sidestep.'}], 'crumbName': 'Computer Basics, Without the Fear'},
{'slug': 'windows-11-course-for-beginners', 'title': 'Free Windows 11 Course for Beginners &amp; Over 60s', 'metaDesc': 'A free, gentle Windows 11 course for older beginners: the Start button, Settings, updates, bigger text, files, OneDrive and PINs. Seven short lessons.', 'h1': 'Windows 11, <em class="grad grad--green">Without the Head-Scratching</em>', 'heroLede': 'A free, gentle course for anyone whose computer moved things about and never explained itself. Seven short lessons at your own pace, plain English throughout, and a printable certificate with your name on it at the end &mdash; from the family-run team at 365 Techies in Bournemouth, helping people with computers since 1995.', 'icon': '&#129695;', 'courseTitle': 'Windows 11, Without the Head-Scratching', 'tagline': 'Free · 7 short lessons · about 45 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>If you have a Windows 11 computer &mdash; most laptops sold since late 2021 &mdash; and it feels as though someone rearranged the furniture without asking, this course was written for you. It covers the handful of things that make Windows 11 comfortable: where the Start button went, where the settings live, why updates matter and which &ldquo;updates&rdquo; are scams, how to make everything bigger and clearer, where your files are and what OneDrive is doing with them, PINs and passwords, and what to do when the computer sulks.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s how it works: seven short lessons, each ending with one gentle &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; question. There are no scores, no time limits and nobody watching &mdash; the questions simply help the ideas settle. Read at your own pace, reread anything you like, and when you finish there&rsquo;s a certificate to print with your name on it, plus a one-page checklist to keep beside the computer.</p><p>Windows 11 changes a little with every update, so we describe buttons by their names and jobs, never by exact position, and where something varies between computers we say so. If you are still on Windows 10, most of this applies too, and lesson three explains what changed for Windows 10 in 2025. Ready? Lesson one starts with the button that moved.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#129695;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 7', 'title': 'The Desktop, the Taskbar and the Start Button', 'body': '<p>Windows 11 is the version of Windows on most computers sold since late 2021, and if you moved to it from an older version, the first thing you noticed was probably that everything shifted to the middle. Nothing is missing &mdash; it has just been rearranged.</p><p><strong>The desktop</strong> is the big picture behind everything, and the little pictures on it are shortcuts &mdash; doors to programs and folders. Along the bottom runs the <strong>taskbar</strong>, a strip that shows what&rsquo;s open and keeps your favourite programs one click away.</p><p><strong>The Start button</strong> is the Windows logo on the taskbar &mdash; now in the centre rather than the corner. Click it and a panel opens with your pinned programs at the top and a <strong>Recommended</strong> list of recent files below. Click <strong>All</strong> (top right of the panel) to see every program in alphabetical order. If you&rsquo;d rather have the button back in the corner, that&rsquo;s a setting, and we&rsquo;ll get to settings in the next lesson.</p><p><strong>The fastest way to find anything</strong> is not to hunt for it. Click the Start button, or the magnifying glass beside it, and simply start typing what you want &mdash; &ldquo;Word&rdquo;, &ldquo;printer&rdquo;, &ldquo;bluetooth&rdquo;, the name of a photo. Windows searches programs, settings and your files together and shows the best match at the top. Most people who use Windows all day never open the full list at all.</p><p><strong>Pinning.</strong> When you find a program you&rsquo;ll use often, right-click it in the Start panel and choose <strong>Pin to taskbar</strong>. It then lives on the strip at the bottom permanently, one click from anywhere. Unpinning is the same right-click. Nothing about pinning deletes the program; it only decides where the door is.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'A friend has set up your new laptop. The Start button sits in the middle of the bottom edge, and you want to open the program that came with your printer but can&rsquo;t see it anywhere.', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the quickest way to find it?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Click the Start button and just start typing the printer&rsquo;s make &mdash; Windows searches everything for you', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. Typing straight after clicking Start searches programs, settings and files together. It is the single habit that makes Windows 11 feel simple, and it works whatever the program is called.'}, {'t': 'Look through every icon on the desktop until you spot it', 'ok': False, 'why': 'The desktop only shows shortcuts someone chose to put there, so plenty of programs never appear on it. Click Start and type the name &mdash; three letters is usually enough.'}, {'t': 'Ask your friend to come back and move the Start button to the corner', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Its position makes no difference to what it does, and moving it is a one-click setting you can change yourself. Click it, type the printer&rsquo;s make, and the program appears.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#9881;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 7', 'title': 'Settings: Where Everything Lives Now', 'body': '<p>Older versions of Windows hid their controls in something called the Control Panel. Windows 11 keeps a tidier, friendlier place called <strong>Settings</strong>, and almost every change you will ever want to make lives there.</p><p><strong>Opening it.</strong> Click the Start button and click the cog-wheel labelled Settings &mdash; or hold the <strong>Windows key</strong> (the one with the logo, bottom left of the keyboard) and tap <strong>I</strong>. A window opens with a list down the left: System, Bluetooth &amp; devices, Network &amp; internet, Personalisation, Apps, Accounts, Time &amp; language, Accessibility, Privacy &amp; security, Windows Update.</p><p><strong>The search box at the top of Settings</strong> is the trick worth remembering. Rather than guessing which of those headings hides the thing you want, type it: &ldquo;text size&rdquo;, &ldquo;printer&rdquo;, &ldquo;taskbar&rdquo;, &ldquo;night light&rdquo;. Settings shows the matching pages and takes you straight there.</p><p><strong>A few settings worth knowing on day one.</strong> <strong>Personalisation, then Taskbar</strong> is where the Start button can be moved to the left corner if you preferred it there (look for taskbar alignment). <strong>Bluetooth &amp; devices, then Printers &amp; scanners</strong> shows your printer and lets you add one. <strong>System, then Display</strong> is where the whole screen can be made larger &mdash; more on that in the lesson on accessibility.</p><p><strong>Nothing in Settings is dangerous to look at.</strong> Every page describes itself in a sentence, changes take effect immediately, and the ones that matter can be switched straight back. If a page asks you to confirm something and you&rsquo;re unsure, close the window; nothing happens until you say so. The Control Panel still exists for the curious, but you will not need it.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You&rsquo;d like the screen to be a little brighter and the Start button back in the corner, and you don&rsquo;t know where either option is.', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the reliable way to find them?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Open Settings (Windows key and I) and type what you want into its search box &mdash; &ldquo;brightness&rdquo;, then &ldquo;taskbar&rdquo;', 'ok': True, 'why': 'That&rsquo;s the habit. Settings&rsquo; own search box turns a hunt through ten headings into a couple of words. Brightness lives under System, Display; the Start button&rsquo;s position under Personalisation, Taskbar &mdash; and you needn&rsquo;t remember either.'}, {'t': 'Search the internet for &ldquo;Windows 11 settings&rdquo; and follow a video', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It can work, but videos show someone else&rsquo;s screen and often an older version. The search box inside Settings is faster and shows your own computer&rsquo;s options.'}, {'t': 'Leave both as they are &mdash; changing settings might break something', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Neither of these can harm anything, and both switch straight back. Brightness and the Start button&rsquo;s position are exactly the kind of thing Settings is for.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128260;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 7', 'title': 'Windows Update, and Why You Let It Run', 'body': '<p>Every few weeks Windows fetches updates: fixes for security holes, small improvements and occasionally new features. They are free, they arrive through Settings, and letting them run is the single most protective habit a computer owner has.</p><p><strong>Where they live.</strong> Open Settings and click <strong>Windows Update</strong> at the bottom of the list. The page tells you plainly whether you are up to date, and a <strong>Check for updates</strong> button asks Microsoft directly. Updates download quietly in the background; you only notice when one asks for a restart.</p><p><strong>The restart.</strong> Some updates finish only when the computer restarts, and Windows will nag until it happens. Save your work, close your programs, and choose <strong>Restart now</strong> when it suits you &mdash; or use <strong>Update and restart</strong> from the Start button&rsquo;s power menu at the end of the day. A restart for an update can take several minutes with a percentage on screen. Leave it alone; switching off partway through is the one thing that can genuinely cause trouble.</p><p><strong>Real updates never pop up on a website.</strong> Windows updates arrive only through Settings. A box on a web page, or an email, saying your computer is out of date, infected, or needs an urgent update &mdash; especially one with a phone number &mdash; is a scam, however official it looks. Close it. If you are ever unsure, open Settings, click Windows Update, and believe what it says there and nowhere else.</p><p><strong>Still on Windows 10?</strong> Windows 10 stopped receiving free security updates in October 2025. Some Windows 10 computers can move to Windows 11 free of charge; others cannot, and Microsoft offers extended updates for those with certain conditions attached. It&rsquo;s a decision worth making deliberately rather than ignoring, and it&rsquo;s exactly the kind of question we&rsquo;re happy to talk through.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'A box appears while you are reading a website: &ldquo;WINDOWS UPDATE REQUIRED &mdash; your computer is at risk. Call this number now.&rdquo;', 'q': 'What do you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Close it &mdash; real updates only ever arrive through Settings, and they never ask you to ring anyone', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Just so. Windows Update lives in Settings and nowhere else. A page that shouts, threatens or offers a phone number is a scam every time. Close the browser window, and if you want reassurance, open Settings, Windows Update, and read what it actually says.'}, {'t': 'Ring the number &mdash; better safe than sorry', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That number goes to the scammer, who will ask to connect to your computer and then for money. Microsoft never puts phone numbers in warnings. Close the page and, if you like, check Settings, Windows Update, which will tell you the truth.'}, {'t': 'Click the box so the update can install', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Clicking is how the scam starts &mdash; it downloads their program, not an update. Real updates are already handled in Settings, quietly, without any box on a website.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128065;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 7', 'title': 'Bigger, Clearer, Louder: Windows Accessibility', 'body': '<p>Windows 11 has a whole section devoted to making the computer easier to see, hear and use, and none of it costs anything or needs an expert. If the screen has ever felt too small or too busy, this lesson pays for itself in five minutes.</p><p><strong>Bigger text everywhere.</strong> Open Settings, click <strong>Accessibility</strong>, then <strong>Text size</strong>. Drag the slider until the sample text is comfortable and click <strong>Apply</strong>. Every menu, web page and letter follows suit. If everything &mdash; icons included &mdash; should be bigger, use <strong>System, Display, Scale</strong> instead, and pick a larger percentage.</p><p><strong>A magnifying glass on demand.</strong> Hold the Windows key and tap the <strong>plus</strong> key: a magnifier opens and enlarges whatever the mouse points at. Windows key and <strong>minus</strong> zooms back out; Windows key and <strong>Escape</strong> closes it. It is the fastest way to read small print on a website without changing anything permanently.</p><p><strong>A mouse pointer you can find.</strong> Under Accessibility, <strong>Mouse pointer and touch</strong> lets you make the pointer larger and change its colour &mdash; a big black pointer with a white edge is far easier to spot than the default. <strong>Text cursor</strong> does the same for the flashing line where you type.</p><p><strong>Easier on the eyes and ears.</strong> <strong>Night light</strong> (under System, Display) warms the screen in the evening. <strong>Contrast themes</strong> under Accessibility make text stand out sharply for anyone who finds pale grey on white a strain. <strong>Narrator</strong> reads the screen aloud, and <strong>Live captions</strong> puts subtitles on any video or call. Under Accessibility, <strong>Audio</strong> can flash the screen when a sound plays, for anyone who misses the chimes.</p><p>Every one of these switches straight back. Try them; the computer is supposed to fit you, not the other way round.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'The writing on websites has become a strain to read, and you don&rsquo;t want to change how the computer looks for everyone else in the house.', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the gentlest fix?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Hold the Windows key and tap plus for the Magnifier when you need it, and raise Accessibility, Text size for your own account', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Both are right and both are yours alone. The Magnifier appears only when you call it and vanishes with Windows key and Escape; text size applies to whoever is signed in. Nobody else&rsquo;s screen changes.'}, {'t': 'Lean closer and squint &mdash; it&rsquo;s just how modern websites are', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It needn&rsquo;t be. Windows has a Magnifier one key press away and a text-size slider built for exactly this. Five minutes in Accessibility settings will change every day after.'}, {'t': 'Buy a bigger monitor', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A bigger screen with the same settings shows the same small text, only further away. Text size and the Magnifier cost nothing and work on the screen you already have.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128193;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 7', 'title': 'Where Your Things Live: File Explorer and OneDrive', 'body': '<p>Every file on the computer &mdash; letters, photos, downloads &mdash; sits in a folder, and <strong>File Explorer</strong> (the yellow folder on the taskbar) is the window that shows them. Windows 11&rsquo;s version opens on a <strong>Home</strong> page of recent and favourite files, with the familiar drawers down the left: Desktop, Documents, Downloads, Pictures.</p><p><strong>The OneDrive question.</strong> On many new computers, Documents, Pictures and Desktop are quietly kept in <strong>OneDrive</strong>, Microsoft&rsquo;s online storage, as well as on the machine. You can tell by the small cloud icon beside a file, or the cloud near the clock in the bottom-right corner. This is often a good thing: if the laptop is lost or dies, the files survive online, and they can be reached from another device. It can also be a surprise: OneDrive&rsquo;s free space is limited, and when it fills up you will see warnings.</p><p><strong>Seeing what is where.</strong> Click the cloud icon near the clock and it tells you whether OneDrive is signed in, how much space is used, and whether it is currently backing up your folders. Nothing there needs changing today; the point is to know which drawers are also in the cloud, so nothing later feels like a mystery.</p><p><strong>A tidy computer stays quick.</strong> Under Settings, System, <strong>Storage</strong>, Windows shows what is filling the drive and offers <strong>Storage Sense</strong>, which empties the Recycle Bin and clears temporary files on a schedule. Switch it on and forget it.</p><p><strong>The habits that matter</strong> are the same as ever: save your work into Documents with a name your future self will recognise; know that anything from the internet lands in Downloads; and when a file seems lost, use the search box in File Explorer&rsquo;s top corner before you worry. Our free Computer Basics course spends two whole lessons on folders and finding things again, if that would help.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'A letter you saved yesterday shows a small cloud icon beside it, and you&rsquo;re worried it&rsquo;s &ldquo;gone to the internet&rdquo; and off your computer.', 'q': 'What does the cloud actually mean?', 'opts': [{'t': 'The letter is on your computer and also safely copied to OneDrive online &mdash; the cloud marks a backed-up file', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. The cloud icon means OneDrive has a copy online as well; the file is still yours and still on the machine. If anything ever happened to the laptop, that letter would survive.'}, {'t': 'The letter has been moved off the computer and you need an internet connection to open it', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A file that lives only online shows a hollow outline cloud, and even then a click brings it back. A letter you saved yesterday is on the machine and backed up &mdash; not taken away.'}, {'t': 'Someone else can see it, so it should be deleted', 'ok': False, 'why': 'OneDrive is private to your Microsoft account unless you deliberately share a file. Nobody else can see the letter, and deleting it would remove your only copies.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128273;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 7', 'title': 'Signing In: PINs, Passwords and Your Microsoft Account', 'body': '<p>Windows 11 usually asks for a <strong>PIN</strong> when it starts &mdash; four or more digits &mdash; and the PIN causes more confusion than any other part of the computer. Here is the whole picture in one breath: the <strong>PIN</strong> unlocks this one computer; the <strong>Microsoft account password</strong> is the bigger key behind it, used for OneDrive, the Microsoft Store and email at Outlook or Hotmail.</p><p><strong>The PIN is safe precisely because it is small.</strong> It only works on this machine, so a thief who learned it could not sign in to your account from anywhere else. Some laptops offer <strong>Windows Hello</strong> &mdash; a fingerprint reader or face recognition &mdash; which unlocks the same way without typing at all. Both are set under Settings, Accounts, <strong>Sign-in options</strong>.</p><p><strong>Forgotten the PIN?</strong> On the sign-in screen, click <strong>I forgot my PIN</strong>. Windows asks for the Microsoft account password and usually sends a code to your phone or email to prove it is you, then lets you set a new PIN. That is why the account needs a phone number or second email address on file &mdash; check it under Accounts, Your info, while everything is calm.</p><p><strong>Write the important one down.</strong> The Microsoft account password is worth keeping on paper somewhere sensible at home, along with the email address it belongs to. A password on a card in a drawer is far safer than one in your head that has gone.</p><p><strong>The lock screen.</strong> Hold the Windows key and tap <strong>L</strong> to lock the computer when you walk away; your work stays exactly as it was behind the PIN. And when a grandchild wants to use the laptop, a separate account for them under Accounts, <strong>Family</strong>, keeps your files and settings out of reach.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'The laptop asks for a PIN and you can&rsquo;t remember it. You do know your Microsoft account email and password.', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the way back in?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Click &ldquo;I forgot my PIN&rdquo; on the sign-in screen, prove it&rsquo;s you with the account password and the code it sends, then set a new PIN', 'ok': True, 'why': 'That&rsquo;s the designed route, and it takes about two minutes. The PIN was only ever a local shortcut; the account password is the real key, and it lets you replace the PIN without losing anything.'}, {'t': 'Type the Microsoft password into the PIN box instead', 'ok': False, 'why': 'The PIN box only accepts the PIN. But the password is still your way in: click &ldquo;I forgot my PIN&rdquo; beneath the box and Windows will ask for it properly.'}, {'t': 'Reset the whole computer to get rid of the PIN', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A reset wipes your files and programs to solve a two-minute problem. &ldquo;I forgot my PIN&rdquo; on the sign-in screen keeps everything and simply sets a new one.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128736;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 7 of 7', 'title': 'When Windows Grumbles: Restarts, Notifications and Getting Help', 'body': '<p>Windows will occasionally sulk &mdash; a program that stops responding, a message that makes no sense, a noise you didn&rsquo;t ask for. Almost all of it is cured by three small habits.</p><p><strong>Restart, not just shut down.</strong> Here is a genuine oddity of Windows 11: <strong>Shut down</strong> does not fully clear the machine, because a feature called fast start-up keeps part of the last session to speed up the next boot. <strong>Restart</strong> does clear everything. So when something is misbehaving, choose Restart from the Start button&rsquo;s power menu &mdash; it fixes far more than shutting down and switching on again.</p><p><strong>Tame the notifications.</strong> The little boxes sliding in at the bottom right come from Settings, System, <strong>Notifications</strong>. You can turn off the chatty ones program by program, and <strong>Do not disturb</strong> silences everything while you concentrate. Nothing important is lost; notifications wait in the panel that opens when you click the clock.</p><p><strong>Capture the message.</strong> When an error appears, don&rsquo;t just close it. Hold the Windows key, then <strong>Shift</strong>, and tap <strong>S</strong>: the screen dims and you drag a box around the message to take a picture of it, saved to your Pictures under Screenshots. That picture is worth ten minutes of description when you ask for help.</p><p><strong>Asking for help safely.</strong> Windows includes <strong>Quick Assist</strong>, a built-in way to let someone you trust see your screen and help &mdash; you read them a six-digit code, you watch everything they do, and you can end it at any moment. That is how we help our customers, and it is the opposite of the scam where a stranger rings out of the blue. The rule is simple: you make the call, you read out the code; nobody who phones you unasked gets either.</p><p>That is Windows 11 well enough to live with it comfortably. Everything else is detail, and detail can wait for the day you need it.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'A program has frozen, the mouse moves but nothing responds, and you&rsquo;ve already tried closing it.', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the fix most likely to work first time?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Restart the computer from the Start button&rsquo;s power menu &mdash; Restart, not Shut down', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Right. Restart clears everything and starts clean; Shut down on Windows 11 keeps part of the old session because of fast start-up, which is why the same problem sometimes survives it. Save anything you can first.'}, {'t': 'Hold the power button until it goes off, then switch it on', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A last resort, not a first one: cutting the power can interrupt something mid-write. Try Restart from the Start button first; it succeeds far more often than people expect.'}, {'t': 'Leave it overnight &mdash; it will probably sort itself out', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A frozen program rarely thaws on its own, and the computer stays unusable meanwhile. Restart takes two minutes and clears the fault properly.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': 'That&rsquo;s the whole course &mdash; and Windows 11 is now a machine you understand rather than one you tiptoe around. You know where the Start button went and how to find anything by typing, where settings live, why updates run and which ones are fake, how to make the screen fit your eyes, where your files are and what the cloud icon means, what a PIN is for, and the restart that actually works. Print your certificate, pop the checklist beside the computer, and be a little proud of yourself. And remember: if anything ever has you stuck, help is one phone call away on 01202 775566 &mdash; and we run friendly in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth. No question is too small.', 'certCourseName': 'Windows 11, Without the Head-Scratching', 'certSubline': 'has completed all seven lessons of this free course from 365 Techies of Bournemouth &mdash; family-run and helping people with computers since 1995', 'checklist': ['Find anything by typing: click the Start button (the Windows logo, now in the middle) and just start typing its name.', 'Settings is where everything lives &mdash; Windows key and I &mdash; and it has its own search box at the top.', 'Real updates arrive only through Settings, Windows Update. A website or email demanding an urgent update is a scam.', 'Save your work before a restart for an update, then leave it alone until it finishes &mdash; never switch off partway through.', 'Too small? Accessibility, Text size. Small print in a hurry? Windows key and plus opens the Magnifier; Windows key and Escape closes it.', 'A cloud icon beside a file means OneDrive has a safe copy online too &mdash; the file is still on your computer.', 'Switch on Storage Sense (Settings, System, Storage) and let the computer tidy itself.', 'The PIN unlocks this computer only; the Microsoft account password is the real key. Keep the password on paper at home.', 'Something misbehaving? Restart, not Shut down &mdash; on Windows 11 only Restart clears everything.', 'An error message? Windows key, Shift and S to take a picture of it before you close it. Stuck? 365 Techies, 01202 775566.'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is this course really free?', 'a': 'Completely. No payment details, no catch and no time limit. We&rsquo;re a family-run IT firm in Bournemouth, and courses like this are our way of helping local people feel confident with technology.'}, {'q': 'I&rsquo;m still on Windows 10. Is this course any use to me?', 'a': 'Most of it, yes &mdash; Settings, updates, accessibility, File Explorer and PINs all work in much the same way, though the Start button sits in the corner and some pages look a little different. Lesson three explains what changed for Windows 10 in October 2025 and why it&rsquo;s worth deciding what to do rather than ignoring it. We&rsquo;re happy to talk it through.'}, {'q': 'Why does the Start button sit in the middle now?', 'a': 'Microsoft moved it there in Windows 11. It does exactly what it always did. If you preferred it in the corner, Settings, Personalisation, Taskbar has a taskbar alignment option that puts it back &mdash; lesson two shows you where.'}, {'q': 'Can I break something by changing settings?', 'a': 'Not the ones in this course. Text size, the Magnifier, notifications, the Start button&rsquo;s position and Storage Sense all switch straight back. Where a setting matters more &mdash; signing in, updates &mdash; the lessons say so and explain what to expect.'}, {'q': 'Do I get a certificate?', 'a': 'Yes &mdash; when you finish, you can print a 365 Techies certificate of completion with your name on it. It&rsquo;s our own certificate rather than a formal qualification, but plenty of people pin it up, and it&rsquo;s earned.'}, {'q': 'What if I get stuck partway through?', 'a': 'Take your time &mdash; there&rsquo;s no clock, and the course will wait for you. If something has you truly stuck, help is one phone call away on 01202 775566, and we run friendly in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth.'}], 'crumbName': 'Windows 11, Without the Head-Scratching'},
{'slug': 'email-basics-course', 'title': 'Free Email Course for Beginners &amp; Over-60s | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'Learn email at your own gentle pace: reading, replying, reply-all, attachments and staying safe. Free, senior-paced lessons with a printable certificate at the end.', 'h1': 'Email, <em class="grad grad--green">without the worry</em>', 'heroLede': 'A free, friendly course from the family-run firm that has been untangling technology in Bournemouth since 1995. Seven short lessons take the worry out of email &mdash; how it works, replying without fear, attachments, and keeping your account safe &mdash; all at your own pace, with a printable certificate at the end. No jargon, nothing to install, nothing to pay.', 'icon': '&#128231;', 'courseTitle': 'Email, Comfortably', 'tagline': 'Free &middot; 7 short lessons &middot; about 40 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>Welcome &mdash; and well done for taking this on. Email is one of those things that looks fiddly from the outside and turns out to be wonderfully simple once someone shows you calmly. That is exactly what this course does. It was written for people who would rather have things explained properly, at a comfortable pace, with no jargon and no showing off.</p><p>Work through the lessons in order, or dip in wherever you like. After each one there is a gentle &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; question &mdash; not a test, just a chance to feel the ideas click into place. There is no timer and no pass mark, and your certificate is waiting at the end.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#128238;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 7', 'title': 'How email actually works', 'body': '<p>Let&rsquo;s begin with what email really is, because once you picture it clearly the rest feels easy. An email address is simply your own postbox on the internet &mdash; a place where messages arrive with your name on them. It is written as something like <strong>jane.smith@gmail.com</strong>: your chosen name, then the @ symbol (which just means &ldquo;at&rdquo;), then the name of the company that looks after your postbox.</p><p>Most email these days is &ldquo;webmail&rdquo;, and that word hides a lovely convenience. It means your emails live safely on the company&rsquo;s computers, not on any one gadget of yours. So you can read the very same inbox on your laptop, your tablet or your phone, and nothing is lost if a device breaks. You simply sign in and there it all is.</p><p>The two best-known free ones, at the time of writing, are <strong>Gmail</strong> (from Google) and <strong>Outlook.com</strong> (from Microsoft). If your address came from your internet provider &mdash; one ending in the provider&rsquo;s name, such as <strong>@btinternet.com</strong> &mdash; that is perfectly fine too, and it works in just the same way. There is no &ldquo;best&rdquo; one you ought to feel you must switch to.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'A friend tells you that you must always use the same computer to read your email, or your messages will be lost. Is that right?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Yes &mdash; email is tied to one machine, so you should never read it anywhere else', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That was more true many years ago, but not today. With modern webmail your messages live safely online, so you can read the same inbox on any device and lose nothing.'}, {'t': 'No &mdash; with webmail your emails live safely online, so you can read them on any device and nothing is lost if one breaks', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. That is the quiet magic of webmail: your postbox lives online, so a broken laptop never means lost letters.'}, {'t': 'Only if you pay extra for the online version', 'ok': False, 'why': 'No need &mdash; the big free services like Gmail and Outlook.com already keep your email online at no cost. There is nothing extra to buy for this.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128233;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 7', 'title': 'Reading and replying without fear', 'body': '<p>Opening an email to read it is as safe as opening a letter &mdash; simply select it from the list and it opens for you to read. (The two things that deserve a moment&rsquo;s care &mdash; attachments and suspicious links &mdash; get their own gentle lessons, 4 and 6.) When you want to answer, you have three buttons that turn up again and again, and knowing what each one does takes all the worry away.</p><p><strong>Reply</strong> sends your answer back to the one person who wrote to you. This is the one you want most of the time. <strong>Reply all</strong> sends your answer to that person <em>and</em> to everyone else who was on the original message &mdash; occasionally handy, but often not what you intend. <strong>Forward</strong> passes the message on to somebody new who was not part of it before, so you can share it along.</p><p>Here is the kindly warning every email user learns sooner or later: if a message went out to a big group and you only mean to answer the sender, choose <strong>Reply</strong>, not <strong>Reply all</strong> &mdash; otherwise your note lands in a dozen inboxes at once. It is a slip almost everyone makes once. No harm done, but a moment&rsquo;s pause saves a little blush.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'An email about a street party has gone out to all twenty of your neighbours. You just want to tell the organiser, Pam, that you&rsquo;ll bring a cake.', 'q': 'Which button do you press?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Reply &mdash; that sends it just to Pam, who wrote the message', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect. Reply goes to the sender alone, so only Pam hears about the cake. You have just sidestepped the classic reply-all slip.'}, {'t': 'Reply all &mdash; it&rsquo;s quickest, and everyone likes cake', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A generous thought! But Reply all would send your cake message to all twenty neighbours, not just Pam. No harm if it happens &mdash; but Reply is the tidier choice here.'}, {'t': 'Forward, and type Pam&rsquo;s address in myself', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That would work in the end, but it is the long way round and easy to fumble. Since Pam is the one who wrote to you, a plain Reply goes straight back to her.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128221;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 7', 'title': 'Sending well: subjects, To, CC and BCC', 'body': '<p>Now for sending your own emails, which is where a couple of small habits make you look like an old hand. The first is the <strong>subject line</strong> &mdash; the short title at the top that tells the reader what your message is about before they open it. &ldquo;Lunch on Thursday?&rdquo; or &ldquo;Photos from the garden&rdquo; is far kinder than leaving it blank. Think of it as the label on a parcel.</p><p>Underneath you&rsquo;ll see boxes labelled <strong>To</strong>, <strong>CC</strong> and <strong>BCC</strong>. In plain English: <strong>To</strong> is for the person you are actually writing to. <strong>CC</strong> (it stands for &ldquo;carbon copy&rdquo;, an old typing term) is for anyone you simply want to keep in the loop. Everyone in To and CC can see each other&rsquo;s addresses.</p><p><strong>BCC</strong> is the quietly useful one. It stands for &ldquo;blind carbon copy&rdquo;, and it means the people you put there each get the message, but none of them can see the others&rsquo; addresses. When you email a whole group who do not all know each other &mdash; a club, say &mdash; putting their addresses in BCC is the polite, private choice. It keeps everyone&rsquo;s email address to themselves.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You want to email news about the bowls club to thirty members, most of whom have never met. How do you protect everyone&rsquo;s privacy?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Put all thirty addresses in the To box, so it feels friendly', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It is a warm thought, but that would show all thirty addresses to all thirty people &mdash; handing everyone&rsquo;s email around without asking. BCC does the kind thing quietly.'}, {'t': 'Put them all in CC, which keeps addresses hidden', 'ok': False, 'why': 'An easy mix-up: CC still shows everyone&rsquo;s address to everyone. It is BCC &mdash; the &ldquo;blind&rdquo; copy &mdash; that keeps addresses private.'}, {'t': 'Put the members&rsquo; addresses in BCC, so nobody can see anyone else&rsquo;s address', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Well judged. BCC means each person gets the news while everyone&rsquo;s address stays private &mdash; exactly the courteous way to email a big group.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128206;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 7', 'title': 'Attachments, both ways', 'body': '<p>An <strong>attachment</strong> is a file clipped to an email &mdash; a photo, perhaps, or a document. You can usually spot one by a little paperclip symbol, or a small picture or file shown below the message. Attachments are wonderfully useful, and they are also the one part of email where a moment&rsquo;s care matters.</p><p>The golden rule for <em>opening</em> them is simple: only open an attachment when you both <strong>know the sender</strong> and <strong>were expecting it</strong>. A photo from your daughter that she just mentioned on the phone &mdash; open away. A &ldquo;delivery note&rdquo; or &ldquo;invoice&rdquo; you were not expecting, from a name you do not recognise &mdash; do not open it, because that is one of the ways trouble sneaks in. When in doubt, leave it and check with the person first.</p><p><em>Sending</em> a photo is the happy opposite. Start a new email as usual, then look for the <strong>paperclip</strong> button (or one that says &ldquo;Attach&rdquo; or &ldquo;Insert&rdquo;). Selecting it lets you find the photo on your device and choose it, and it clips itself to your message ready to send. The exact wording and screens vary a little between Gmail, Outlook.com and the rest, and they change over time &mdash; so take your time and read what is in front of you.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'An email arrives from a company name you do not recognise. The message is empty apart from an attachment called &ldquo;Invoice_overdue.zip&rdquo;, which you were not expecting.', 'q': 'What is the wise thing to do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Leave the attachment unopened &mdash; you do not know the sender and were not expecting it', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Just right. You have used the golden rule perfectly: unknown sender, not expected, so it stays shut. If you are ever unsure a bill is real, contact the company on a number you find yourself, not one from the email.'}, {'t': 'Open it straight away to see whether you really do owe money', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That is just the worry the email is hoping to trigger. An unexpected attachment from a name you do not know is exactly what to leave unopened &mdash; opening it is how trouble gets in.'}, {'t': 'Reply to ask the sender whether the attachment is safe', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It seems polite, but a sender with bad intentions will simply say &ldquo;yes, it&rsquo;s safe&rdquo; &mdash; and replying tells them your address is live. Better to leave it and, if needed, check with the company directly.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#129529;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 7', 'title': 'Taming the inbox', 'body': '<p>As the emails pile up, two buttons keep the clutter down. <strong>Delete</strong> throws a message in the bin &mdash; the right choice for junk and things you will never want again. <strong>Archive</strong> is the gentler cousin: it tidies a message out of your inbox but keeps it safely stored, so you can search it out later if you ever need it. If you are the sort who likes to keep things &ldquo;just in case&rdquo;, archive is your friend.</p><p>Most adverts and newsletters carry a tiny <strong>Unsubscribe</strong> link, usually right at the bottom, which stops them being sent to you. Here is the safe way to use it: only click Unsubscribe on emails from companies you genuinely signed up to and recognise. On a suspicious or unexpected email, never click it &mdash; to a scammer, your click simply confirms a real person is reading. For those, delete instead.</p><p>Finally, meet the <strong>Junk</strong> or <strong>Spam</strong> folder. Your email service tries to guess which messages are rubbish and quietly tucks them in there for you &mdash; a great help. It is not perfect, though, so every week or two have a quick peek: now and then a genuine message from a friend or your bank lands there by mistake, and you can move it back to the inbox.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'A newsletter you never remember signing up for keeps arriving, and the sender looks a bit odd. What is the safest move?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Click its Unsubscribe link to make it stop for good', 'ok': False, 'why': 'With a normal, trusted company that is fine &mdash; but on an email that looks odd and you do not remember joining, an Unsubscribe click can just tell a scammer you are real. Better not to click it here.'}, {'t': 'Reply and firmly ask them to remove you', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Understandable, but replying has the same catch: it confirms your address is live to someone you do not trust. Save your replies for people you know.'}, {'t': 'Delete it &mdash; and if it keeps coming, let your email service move it to Junk', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Nicely judged. Deleting costs you nothing and gives a doubtful sender no clue you are there. Marking it as junk teaches your inbox to filter the rest away.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128274;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 7', 'title': 'Email safety essentials', 'body': '<p>This lesson ties everything to one big idea, and it is worth saying plainly: your email account is the <strong>key to everything else</strong>. If someone gets into it, they can ask your bank, your shops and your other accounts to send their &ldquo;reset your password&rdquo; messages there &mdash; and read them. That is why email deserves your <em>strongest</em> password, and one you use <strong>nowhere else</strong>. A memorable line of three or four random words makes a fine, strong password.</p><p>A quick recap of the scam-spotting habit from our online-safety course, because email is where a great many scams begin. Nearly every scam works the same way: it tries to <strong>rush you or scare you</strong> &mdash; &ldquo;your account will close today&rdquo;, &ldquo;confirm now or lose access&rdquo;. So the single best thing you can do is slow down. No genuine bank or company will ever email asking for your password or a one-time code (one of those short security numbers sent by text), or tell you to move money to a &ldquo;safe account&rdquo;. And if an email urges you to click a link and sign in, skip the link &mdash; open the website yourself, the way you usually would.</p><p>If a suspicious message slips through, you can act. Forward scam texts to <strong>7726</strong> (free), forward suspicious emails to <strong>report@phishing.gov.uk</strong>, and if money is involved, call your bank straight away &mdash; dialling <strong>159</strong> puts you through to most UK banks &mdash; then report what happened to <strong>Action Fraud</strong> (in Scotland, ring Police Scotland on <strong>101</strong>). And if you are ever locked out of your own email, do not panic &mdash; each provider has a &ldquo;forgot password&rdquo; link to get you back in, and we are always one phone call away to help you through it calmly.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'An email that looks like it is from your email provider says: &ldquo;Unusual activity! Confirm your password within 2 hours or your account closes.&rdquo;', 'q': 'What does the slow-down habit tell you?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Confirm the password quickly, before the two hours run out', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That racing clock is precisely the trick &mdash; it wants you flustered. Real providers never ask for your password this way. Slow down, and do not hand it over.'}, {'t': 'It is using rush and fear &mdash; genuine providers do not ask for your password by email, so do not click; if worried, go to the provider&rsquo;s own site yourself', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Beautifully done. You spotted the two warning signs &mdash; a ticking clock and a request for your password &mdash; and chose the calm, safe path. That instinct will protect you again and again.'}, {'t': 'Reply to ask whether the email is genuine', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A fair instinct, but the reply would only reach the sender &mdash; who will happily insist it is real. Never use the email itself to check; go straight to the provider&rsquo;s own website, or ring someone you trust.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128193;', 'tag': 'Lesson 7 of 7', 'title': 'A little housekeeping', 'body': '<p>Congratulations &mdash; the essentials are behind you, and this last lesson is a gentle extra: a few &ldquo;housekeeping&rdquo; touches that make email feel like your own comfortable armchair. None of it is essential, so treat it as a menu to enjoy at leisure.</p><p>The first is your <strong>contacts</strong>, sometimes called an address book. When you save someone&rsquo;s details there, you can start typing their name and their address fills itself in &mdash; no more squinting at a scrap of paper. It also helps your email recognise friends, so their messages are less likely to be mistaken for junk.</p><p>Two more nice-to-haves. You can create <strong>folders</strong> (or labels) to file messages you want to keep &mdash; &ldquo;Family&rdquo;, &ldquo;Holiday&rdquo;, &ldquo;Bills&rdquo; &mdash; rather like drawers in a cabinet. And you can set up a <strong>signature</strong>: a little sign-off, such as your name, that adds itself to the bottom of every email you write, so you never have to type it again. Where these live varies between Gmail, Outlook.com and the others, and the screens change over time &mdash; so look for the settings or gear symbol, and take it slowly.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You often email the same handful of family and friends. Which small step would save you the most fiddling?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Save them in your contacts, so their address fills in as you type their name', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Just the thing. With people saved as contacts, a couple of letters of their name is enough and the address appears &mdash; quicker, and no more typos.'}, {'t': 'Retype each person&rsquo;s address carefully every single time', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That is a lot of careful typing &mdash; and one slipped letter sends your message astray. Saving people as contacts does the remembering for you.'}, {'t': 'Write all their addresses on a note stuck to the screen', 'ok': False, 'why': 'We have all done it! But a sticky note can fall off or be read by anyone passing. Your contacts list keeps them tidy, private and always to hand.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': '<p>Well done &mdash; you have worked all the way through, and email should feel a good deal friendlier now. If you keep just one habit, make it this: <strong>slow down before you click</strong> &mdash; only open attachments you were expecting from people you know, and give your email account a strong password you use nowhere else, because it is the key to everything.</p><p>And remember, you are never on your own with technology. Real, friendly help is one phone call away on <strong>01202 775566</strong>, and we run relaxed in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth. Do come and say hello.</p>', 'certCourseName': 'Email, Comfortably', 'certSubline': 'How email works, replying and the reply-all trap, To, CC and BCC, opening and sending attachments safely, taming your inbox, and keeping your account secure', 'checklist': ['Picture your email address as your own postbox online - with webmail you can read the same inbox on a laptop, tablet or phone.', 'Use Reply to answer only the sender; use Reply all only when you truly mean everyone on the message to see it.', 'Give every email a short, clear subject line so the reader knows what it is about before opening.', 'Emailing a group who do not all know each other? Put their addresses in BCC so each address stays hidden from the rest.', 'Only open an attachment when you know the sender and were expecting it - if in doubt, leave it and check first.', 'Delete junk; archive things you may want later; and glance in your Junk or Spam folder now and then for genuine mail.', 'Only click Unsubscribe on emails from companies you signed up to - never on suspicious ones, where you should delete instead.', 'Give your email account your strongest password and use it nowhere else - it is the key to your other accounts.', 'Scams rush you or scare you, so slow down: no genuine bank or company will email asking for your password or a code, or tell you to move money to a safe account.', 'If a scam strikes: forward scam texts to 7726, forward dodgy emails to report@phishing.gov.uk, and call your bank - 159 puts you through to most banks. Stuck? Call 365 Techies on 01202 775566.'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is this course really free?', 'a': '<p>Completely free, with no catch and no card details asked for. We are a family-run IT firm that has looked after Bournemouth homes and businesses since 1995, and this course is our way of helping local people feel confident with everyday technology.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I need to install anything or create an account?', 'a': '<p>Nothing at all for the course itself &mdash; it runs right here in your web browser. To send and receive email you will need an email account, and the big free ones such as Gmail and Outlook.com are easy to set up when you feel ready. If your internet provider gave you an address ending in the provider&rsquo;s name &mdash; @btinternet.com, for example &mdash; that works perfectly well too.</p>'}, {'q': 'Which should I use &mdash; Gmail or Outlook.com?', 'a': '<p>Honestly, either is excellent, and they are more alike than different. Gmail comes from Google and Outlook.com from Microsoft; both are free and both keep your email safely online. Our gentle advice is to pick one, get comfortable, and not worry that you have chosen the &ldquo;wrong&rdquo; one &mdash; you have not. A provider address such as @btinternet.com is fine to keep using as well &mdash; though it is worth knowing that if you ever switch internet provider, some providers close the address that came with the account, which is one reason many people like a free address that stays with them for good.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is the certificate a qualification?', 'a': '<p>It is a 365 Techies certificate of completion &mdash; a lovely thing to print, keep or show the grandchildren, but not an accredited qualification. The real prize is the confidence and the safe habits you take away with you.</p>'}, {'q': 'I have never sent an email before. Is this too advanced for me?', 'a': '<p>Quite the opposite &mdash; it was written especially for complete beginners. Every idea is explained in plain English, every lesson is short, and there is no timer, no pass mark and no way to fail. You set the pace, and you can revisit any lesson as often as you like.</p>'}, {'q': 'Can I get help in person?', 'a': '<p>Absolutely. We run relaxed, friendly in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth &mdash; no question too small. Call us on 01202 775566 and we will find a time that suits you.</p>'}], 'crumbName': 'Email, Comfortably'},
{'slug': 'word-basics-course', 'title': 'Free Microsoft Word Course for Beginners &amp; Over 60s', 'metaDesc': 'A free, gentle Microsoft Word course for older beginners: typing, fonts, margins, saving, printing and emailing a letter. Seven short lessons, no jargon.', 'h1': 'Microsoft Word, <em class="grad grad--green">Without the Worry</em>', 'heroLede': 'A free, gentle course for anyone who wants to write a letter, set the margins, print it and send it &mdash; without the program fighting back. Seven short lessons at your own pace, plain English throughout, and a printable certificate with your name on it at the end &mdash; from the family-run team at 365 Techies in Bournemouth, helping people with computers since 1995.', 'icon': '&#128196;', 'courseTitle': 'Microsoft Word, Without the Worry', 'tagline': 'Free · 7 short lessons · about 40 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>If you once knew your way around a typewriter or an older computer and now find Word bristling with buttons, this course was written for you. It covers the handful of things almost every letter needs &mdash; typing it, making it look right, setting the margins and page size, saving it, printing it and sending it &mdash; and leaves the other four hundred buttons where they are.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s how it works: seven short lessons, each ending with one gentle &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; question. There are no scores, no time limits and nobody watching &mdash; the questions simply help the ideas settle. Read at your own pace, reread anything you like, and when you finish there&rsquo;s a certificate to print with your name on it, plus a one-page checklist to keep beside the computer.</p><p>The course is written for Microsoft Word on a Windows computer &mdash; the version that comes with Microsoft 365 &mdash; though almost everything applies to older versions too, where a tab may carry a slightly different name. Don&rsquo;t have Word? A simpler free version runs in your web browser with a free Microsoft account (at the time of writing, at microsoft365.com), and the free LibreOffice Writer works much the same way. Screens vary and change over time, so we describe buttons by their names and jobs, never by exact position. Ready? Lesson one begins with the most useful advice about Word there is: just type.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#128196;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 7', 'title': 'Opening Word and Starting a Letter', 'body': '<p><strong>Word</strong> is the program for writing things down: letters, lists, a notice for the village hall, the family news at Christmas. It isn&rsquo;t email and it isn&rsquo;t the internet &mdash; it&rsquo;s the modern typewriter, with the enormous advantage that you can change your mind.</p><p><strong>Opening it.</strong> Click the <strong>Start</strong> button, type the word <strong>Word</strong>, and click the blue W that appears. It opens on a start screen full of designs; ignore them all and click <strong>Blank document</strong>. A white page appears with a thin flashing line near the top-left corner. That line is where your letters will go &mdash; it&rsquo;s called the cursor, and it waits patiently for as long as you like.</p><p><strong>The wall of buttons.</strong> Across the top runs a row of words &mdash; Home, Insert, Layout, View and so on. Each is a tab, and clicking one shows a strip of buttons for that job. This strip is called the ribbon. You do not need to learn it. Rest the mouse pointer on any button without clicking and a little label appears telling you what it does. You&rsquo;ll meet the handful that matter in the lessons ahead; the rest can stay strangers.</p><p><strong>Just type.</strong> The best first move in Word is to start typing your letter. Nothing you type is permanent, nothing can be broken, and every change is undoable. The buttons can wait until you want something from them.</p><p>One honest note: Word comes with Microsoft 365, which is a paid subscription, and many computers already have it. If yours doesn&rsquo;t, a simpler free version of Word runs in your web browser with a free Microsoft account, and everything in this course still applies &mdash; the buttons carry the same names.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You open Word for the first time in years and the ribbon is a wall of buttons you don&rsquo;t recognise.', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the sensible first move?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Click Blank document and start typing &mdash; the buttons can wait', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Just so. Nothing you type is permanent and nothing can break. You&rsquo;ll meet the buttons that matter one at a time, when you actually want something from them.'}, {'t': 'Close it &mdash; you need to learn every button first', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Nobody learns every button &mdash; most people who use Word every day know a dozen. Start typing; the rest of this course introduces the few you need.'}, {'t': 'Click every button in turn to see what each one does', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Harmless, but a long way round. Rest the pointer on a button and a label tells you its job without clicking. Better still, type first and look things up when you need them.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#9000;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 7', 'title': 'Typing Without Fighting It', 'body': '<p>A few habits make typing in Word calm rather than exasperating. They&rsquo;re small, and they undo most of the frustration people feel.</p><p><strong>Let Word turn the lines.</strong> Keep typing when you reach the right-hand edge &mdash; Word moves to the next line by itself. Press <strong>Enter</strong> only when you want a new paragraph. Pressing Enter at the end of every line is the single most common habit from typewriter days, and it makes every later change go wrong.</p><p><strong>Two deleting keys.</strong> <strong>Backspace</strong> removes the letter to the left of the cursor; <strong>Delete</strong> removes the one to the right. To remove a whole word or sentence, select it first: drag across it with the mouse button held down, or double-click a word to select just that word, or triple-click to select the whole paragraph. Then press Backspace once.</p><p><strong>The most useful key in Word.</strong> Hold <strong>Ctrl</strong> and tap <strong>Z</strong>. That is Undo, and it takes back the last thing that happened &mdash; a deleted paragraph, a paste in the wrong place, text that suddenly went bold. Tap Z again to go back further. Hold Ctrl and tap <strong>Y</strong> to redo if you undo too far. Whatever has just gone wrong, try Ctrl and Z before anything else.</p><p><strong>Squiggles are suggestions, not scoldings.</strong> A red or blue wavy line under a word means Word thinks it may be misspelt or oddly phrased. Right-click the word to see its ideas, or ignore it &mdash; Word is often wrong about names and places, and the squiggles never print.</p><p><strong>Don&rsquo;t line things up with the space bar.</strong> It looks right on screen and wrong on paper. Use the <strong>Tab</strong> key to jump across, and leave the rest to the lesson on layout. And if everything comes out IN CAPITALS, the Caps Lock key has been pressed &mdash; tap it once.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You&rsquo;ve typed a whole paragraph of a letter, pressed something by accident, and the paragraph has vanished.', 'q': 'What do you do first?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Hold Ctrl and tap Z &mdash; Undo brings it straight back', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. Undo takes back the last thing that happened, whatever it was, and you can tap Z again to go back further. It is the first thing to try whenever Word surprises you.'}, {'t': 'Retype the whole paragraph from memory', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It&rsquo;s not gone &mdash; Word remembers every step. Hold Ctrl and tap Z and the paragraph returns exactly as it was, spelling and all.'}, {'t': 'Close Word without saving and start the letter again', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That really would lose it, along with everything else you&rsquo;d written. Undo first: hold Ctrl and tap Z. Closing without saving is the one thing that can&rsquo;t be undone.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#127912;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 7', 'title': 'Making It Look Right: Fonts, Size and Bold', 'body': '<p>Once the words are down, you can dress them. Everything here lives on the <strong>Home</strong> tab, and one rule governs all of it: <strong>select first, then change</strong>. Word applies your choice to whatever is selected. Select nothing, and it applies to whatever you type next.</p><p><strong>Font and size.</strong> Near the left of the Home tab are two boxes: the first names the typeface, the second gives its size. Select your text (hold Ctrl and tap <strong>A</strong> to select everything), then pick a size from the list. Size 12 is the everyday size for a letter; 14 is easier on the eyes; a notice for the noticeboard might want 28 or more. Any clear typeface will do &mdash; there is no prize for the fancy ones.</p><p><strong>Bold, italic, underline.</strong> The <strong>B</strong>, <em>I</em> and U buttons sit just below the font boxes. Select a word, click B, and it&rsquo;s bold; click B again to undo it. Hold Ctrl and tap B for the same thing from the keyboard.</p><p><strong>Bullets, numbering, centring.</strong> For a list, click the <strong>Bullets</strong> button and type one item per line; Word adds the dots. To centre a heading on the page, click anywhere in it and click the <strong>Center</strong> button (the one with lines balanced in the middle), or hold Ctrl and tap <strong>E</strong>. The <strong>Line and Paragraph Spacing</strong> button, also on the Home tab, spreads lines out if a letter looks cramped.</p><p><strong>Bigger for you is not bigger on paper.</strong> If the words look tiny on screen but the letter must print at a normal size, don&rsquo;t change the font. Use <strong>Zoom</strong> instead: the slider in the bottom-right corner of the window, or hold Ctrl and roll the mouse wheel. Zoom enlarges the view for your eyes only and changes nothing about the printout. Font size changes the paper.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'The words in your letter look far too small on screen, but the letter itself must print at an ordinary size for the council.', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the right fix?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Use Zoom &mdash; the bottom-right slider, or Ctrl and the mouse wheel &mdash; which enlarges the view but not the printout', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. Zoom is for your eyes; it leaves the letter itself untouched, so the council gets an ordinary-sized letter and you get to read it comfortably while you write it.'}, {'t': 'Change the font size to 24 so you can read it', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That would print a letter in giant type, and the council would receive four pages instead of one. Font size changes the paper; Zoom changes only what you see.'}, {'t': 'Buy a bigger monitor', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A bigger screen makes everything bigger, including the bill. Zoom does the same job for this letter, free, in one second: hold Ctrl and roll the mouse wheel.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128207;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 7', 'title': 'Margins, Page Size and Where the Words Sit', 'body': '<p><strong>Margins</strong> are the empty border around the page &mdash; the space between the edge of the paper and the words. They are the reason a letter looks like a letter rather than a wall of text, and they are set in one place.</p><p><strong>Setting them.</strong> Click the <strong>Layout</strong> tab (called Page Layout in older versions of Word) and click <strong>Margins</strong>. A short list drops down. <strong>Normal</strong> is the everyday choice and suits almost every letter. <strong>Narrow</strong> fits more on the page; <strong>Wide</strong> gives a generous border. If someone has asked for a particular measurement, choose <strong>Custom Margins</strong> at the bottom and type the numbers in. The change applies to the whole document at once.</p><p><strong>The page size trap.</strong> Next to Margins are <strong>Orientation</strong> (upright or sideways) and <strong>Size</strong>. Click Size and check it says <strong>A4</strong>, the paper used in Britain. A computer set up for America defaults to &ldquo;Letter&rdquo;, which is a little shorter and wider, and the result is a letter that prints with an odd gap at the bottom or spills one lonely line onto a second page. If you find Letter selected, choose A4 &mdash; and in the Custom Margins window there is a <strong>Set As Default</strong> button that makes every new document start with your choices.</p><p><strong>Margin or indent?</strong> If one paragraph sits further in than the rest, that&rsquo;s an indent, not a margin. Click in that paragraph and look at the ruler above the page (if there is no ruler, click the <strong>View</strong> tab and tick <strong>Ruler</strong>). The small triangles at the left end show the indent; drag them back to the margin line and the paragraph falls into line.</p><p><strong>Starting a new page.</strong> Never press Enter twenty times to reach the next sheet &mdash; it looks fine today and slides about the moment you change a word. Hold <strong>Ctrl</strong> and tap <strong>Enter</strong> instead: a clean page break that stays where you put it.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'Your letter looked fine on screen, but it prints with a large empty gap at the bottom of the page and one line spilling onto a second sheet.', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the likely cause, and the fix?', 'opts': [{'t': 'The page size is set to Letter &mdash; click Layout, then Size, choose A4, and set the margins to Normal', 'ok': True, 'why': 'That&rsquo;s the one. Letter paper is shorter and wider than A4, so a document set to Letter prints oddly on British paper: a gap at the bottom, and less room, so the last line spills over. A4 plus Normal margins puts it right.'}, {'t': 'Press Enter fewer times at the end of the letter', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Stray blank lines can push text onto a new page, but they don&rsquo;t explain the gap at the bottom. That gap is the signature of the wrong page size &mdash; check Layout, then Size, for A4.'}, {'t': 'Print it a second time &mdash; printers usually get it right on the next go', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Printers don&rsquo;t improve with practice; they print exactly what the page size tells them to. Set the size to A4 and the second print will be the good one.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128190;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 7', 'title': 'Saving, Naming and Finding It Again', 'body': '<p>A letter that exists only on screen is a letter one power cut away from oblivion. Saving takes a second and becomes automatic within a week.</p><p><strong>Save early.</strong> The moment you&rsquo;ve typed anything you&rsquo;d mind losing, hold <strong>Ctrl</strong> and tap <strong>S</strong>. The first time, Word asks two questions: what to call the file, and where to put it. Give it a name your future self will recognise &mdash; &ldquo;Letter to council about bins&rdquo; beats &ldquo;Document1&rdquo; every time &mdash; and choose <strong>Documents</strong>. After that first save, Ctrl and S simply saves quietly, and you can tap it as often as you like.</p><p><strong>Where it offers to put things.</strong> Word that comes with Microsoft 365 may suggest <strong>OneDrive</strong>, which is Microsoft&rsquo;s online storage. It&rsquo;s a fine place if you already use it &mdash; your letter is then kept safely off the computer as well as on it, and the <strong>AutoSave</strong> switch in the top-left corner keeps saving for you. If you&rsquo;d rather keep things on this computer only, choose <strong>This PC</strong>, then Documents. Both are right; just know which you chose.</p><p><strong>Save, or Save As?</strong> <strong>Save</strong> updates the file you have open. <strong>Save As</strong> (or Save a Copy) in the File menu makes a second file with a new name, leaving the original untouched &mdash; handy when this year&rsquo;s Christmas letter starts from last year&rsquo;s.</p><p><strong>Finding it again.</strong> Open Word and click <strong>Open</strong> in the File menu: recent files are listed by name. Or open <strong>File Explorer</strong>, click Documents, and there it is. Word files end in <strong>.docx</strong> &mdash; that ending is how the computer knows to open them in Word when you double-click. If Word ever closes unexpectedly, reopen it: it usually offers a recovered copy of whatever you had open.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You&rsquo;re halfway through a long letter and the phone rings.', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the wise move before you answer?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Hold Ctrl and tap S &mdash; it takes a second, and the letter is safe while you talk', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. One second, and a power cut, a flat battery or a curious grandchild can no longer lose your work. Answer the phone; the letter will be there when you come back.'}, {'t': 'Leave it &mdash; Word keeps everything automatically', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Only sometimes. AutoSave works when the file is on OneDrive; a letter kept on this computer relies on you. Ctrl and S is the habit that never lets you down either way.'}, {'t': 'Close Word so nothing can happen to it', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Closing without saving is the one way to lose it for certain. Save first &mdash; Ctrl and S &mdash; and there&rsquo;s no need to close anything at all.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128424;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 7', 'title': 'Printing It, and Saving as a PDF', 'body': '<p>When the letter is right, hold <strong>Ctrl</strong> and tap <strong>P</strong>. Word shows a print window with a preview of the page on the right &mdash; look at it before printing. If the layout is wrong here, it will be wrong on paper, and the previous lesson fixes it.</p><p><strong>Three checks before you click Print.</strong> The <strong>Printer</strong> box should show your printer&rsquo;s name, not &ldquo;Microsoft Print to PDF&rdquo; or &ldquo;Fax&rdquo;, which are pretend printers that make files instead of paper. <strong>Pages</strong> should say all pages, or the page numbers you want &mdash; type 1 to print just the first page of a long document. <strong>Copies</strong> is usually 1. Then click <strong>Print</strong>, once.</p><p><strong>If nothing comes out</strong>, look at the printer before blaming the computer: switched on, paper in, no message on its little screen, and on the same Wi-Fi as the computer if it&rsquo;s wireless. Our free Computer Basics course has a whole lesson on printing, and if a printer defeats everyone, that is what we&rsquo;re for.</p><p><strong>Saving as a PDF.</strong> A PDF is a snapshot of your document that looks identical on any computer, phone or tablet, opens without Word, and can&rsquo;t be accidentally edited by whoever receives it. That makes it the right format for anything you send to be read &mdash; a letter, a form, a notice. Click <strong>File</strong>, then <strong>Save As</strong> (or Save a Copy), and in the box that shows the file type, choose <strong>PDF</strong>. Word keeps your original .docx file too, so you can still change the letter later.</p><p>The pretend printer does the same job from the print window: choose <strong>Microsoft Print to PDF</strong> as the printer and click Print, and it asks where to save the PDF. Either way, you now have a file that goes anywhere and looks the same when it arrives.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You&rsquo;ve filled in a form for the council. They must not be able to change it, and you&rsquo;re not sure they even have Word.', 'q': 'What should you send them?', 'opts': [{'t': 'A PDF &mdash; File, Save As, choose PDF, and send that file', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. A PDF opens on anything, looks the same everywhere, and can&rsquo;t be quietly altered. Your original Word file stays on your computer in case you need to change it later.'}, {'t': 'The Word file, and hope they have Word', 'ok': False, 'why': 'They may well have it, but a Word file can be edited by anyone who opens it and may lay out differently on their machine. A PDF removes both worries in one click.'}, {'t': 'Print it, photograph it with your phone, and send the photo', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It works in an emergency, but a photo of a form is hard to read, huge to send, and the council can&rsquo;t search or print it cleanly. Save As PDF gives them the real thing.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128206;', 'tag': 'Lesson 7 of 7', 'title': 'Sending It: Attaching Your Document to an Email', 'body': '<p>The final step is the one people find hardest, and it&rsquo;s really just two things: knowing where the file is, and telling the email to bring it along.</p><p><strong>The reliable way.</strong> Save the letter (Ctrl and S) and notice its name and that it&rsquo;s in Documents. Open your email, start a new message, and address it as usual. Now look for the <strong>paperclip</strong>, or a button called <strong>Attach</strong> or <strong>Attach file</strong>. Click it, and a window opens for you to find the file: click <strong>Documents</strong> on the left, click your letter once, and click <strong>Open</strong>. The file&rsquo;s name now appears in the message &mdash; usually just under the subject line, or at the bottom. That is the attachment. Check it&rsquo;s there, then click Send.</p><p><strong>The shortcut from inside Word.</strong> Click <strong>File</strong>, then <strong>Share</strong>, and choose to send a copy as a Word document or as a PDF. If your computer has an email program set up, a new message opens with the file already attached. If nothing happens, don&rsquo;t worry &mdash; that shortcut only works with certain email set-ups. The reliable way above works with every email there is, including the ones you read in a web browser.</p><p><strong>Which format to send?</strong> Send a PDF when the other person only needs to read or print it: letters, forms, notices. Send the Word file when they need to edit it &mdash; a document you&rsquo;re writing together. When in doubt, PDF.</p><p><strong>Size is rarely a problem.</strong> A letter is tiny; even twenty pages of typing is smaller than one photo from a phone. If an email refuses an attachment as too large, it is nearly always because it contains pictures.</p><p>Attachments have a second direction, too: opening the ones other people send you. Our free Email course covers that in its lesson on attachments, and it makes a natural next step from here. You can now write a letter, lay it out, print it and send it. That is the whole of Word that most people ever need.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You&rsquo;ve finished the letter and want to email it to your daughter so she can check it before it goes to the council.', 'q': 'How do you send it?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Save it, start a new email, click the paperclip, choose the letter from Documents, check it appears in the message, then Send', 'ok': True, 'why': 'That&rsquo;s the whole recipe, and it works with every email service there is. Once the file&rsquo;s name shows in the message, the letter travels with it.'}, {'t': 'Copy all the words out of Word and paste them into the email', 'ok': False, 'why': 'The words arrive, but the layout, the margins and the look you worked on do not, and she can&rsquo;t print it as you laid it out. Attach the file and she sees exactly what you see.'}, {'t': 'Type the file&rsquo;s name into the email so she can find it', 'ok': False, 'why': 'She can&rsquo;t reach your Documents folder from her house &mdash; a name on its own is a description, not a delivery. The file has to travel inside the email as an attachment.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': 'That&rsquo;s the whole course &mdash; and Word is now a tool rather than a puzzle. You can write a letter, lay it out with proper margins on the right paper, save it where you&rsquo;ll find it, print it or turn it into a PDF, and send it on its way. Print your certificate, pop the checklist beside the computer, and be a little proud of yourself. And remember: if anything ever has you stuck, help is one phone call away on 01202 775566 &mdash; and we run friendly in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth. No question is too small.', 'certCourseName': 'Microsoft Word, Without the Worry', 'certSubline': 'has completed all seven lessons of this free course from 365 Techies of Bournemouth &mdash; family-run and helping people with computers since 1995', 'checklist': ['Blank document, then type. The buttons can wait until you actually want something from them.', 'Press Enter for a new paragraph only &mdash; Word turns the lines itself.', 'Made a mess? Hold Ctrl and tap Z. Undo is the most useful key in Word.', 'Select first, then change: fonts, size, bold, bullets and centring all live on the Home tab.', 'Zoom makes it bigger for you; font size makes it bigger on paper.', 'Margins: Layout, then Margins, then Normal. Page size: Layout, then Size, then A4 &mdash; not Letter.', 'Save early: hold Ctrl and tap S, name it so your future self recognises it, and keep it in Documents.', 'Printing: hold Ctrl and tap P, check the Printer box shows your printer&rsquo;s name, then click Print once.', 'Sending something people shouldn&rsquo;t change? File, Save As, choose PDF.', 'Attach it: save, then the paperclip in your email, Documents, click the file, check it shows in the message, then Send. Stuck? 365 Techies, 01202 775566.'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is this course really free?', 'a': 'Completely. No payment details, no catch and no time limit. We&rsquo;re a family-run IT firm in Bournemouth, and courses like this are our way of helping local people feel confident with technology.'}, {'q': 'Which version of Word does this cover?', 'a': 'It&rsquo;s written for Microsoft Word on a Windows computer &mdash; the version that comes with Microsoft 365 &mdash; but the ideas are the same in older versions, where a tab may have a slightly different name (Layout was once called Page Layout). The free version of Word that runs in a web browser, and the free LibreOffice Writer, work the same way for everything in this course.'}, {'q': 'I don&rsquo;t have Word. Do I have to buy it?', 'a': 'Not to follow this course. A simpler free version of Word runs in your web browser with a free Microsoft account &mdash; at the time of writing, at microsoft365.com &mdash; and the free LibreOffice Writer does everything here too. If you&rsquo;d like the full program, Microsoft 365 is a subscription, and we&rsquo;re happy to talk through whether it&rsquo;s worth it for you.'}, {'q': 'Is this a Microsoft course?', 'a': 'No. Microsoft Word is Microsoft&rsquo;s product; this course is ours, written by a family-run IT firm in Bournemouth for the people we help every week. The certificate at the end is a 365 Techies certificate of completion, not a Microsoft qualification.'}, {'q': 'Do I get a certificate?', 'a': 'Yes &mdash; when you finish, you can print a 365 Techies certificate of completion with your name on it. It&rsquo;s our own certificate rather than a formal qualification, but plenty of people pin it up, and it&rsquo;s earned.'}, {'q': 'What if I get stuck partway through?', 'a': 'Take your time &mdash; there&rsquo;s no clock, and the course will wait for you. If something has you truly stuck, help is one phone call away on 01202 775566, and we run friendly in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth.'}], 'crumbName': 'Microsoft Word, Without the Worry'},
{'slug': 'nhs-app-course', 'title': 'The NHS App, Gently: A Free Course for Beginners | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'A free, gentle online course for older people: set up the NHS App, order repeat prescriptions and, where offered, book GP appointments. From 365 Techies.', 'h1': 'The NHS App, <em class="grad grad--green">Gently</em>', 'heroLede': 'A free, unhurried online course for older beginners: set up the NHS App, order repeat prescriptions, see test results and book GP appointments where your surgery offers them &mdash; one short lesson at a time, with no jargon and no rush.', 'icon': '&#128153;', 'courseTitle': 'The NHS App, Gently', 'tagline': 'Free &middot; 7 short lessons &middot; about 35 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>The NHS App can save you real time and real worry &mdash; repeat prescriptions ordered from your armchair, test results without waiting by the phone, appointments booked while the surgery line is engaged. (Exactly which of these your own surgery has switched on does vary &mdash; we are honest about that all the way through.) But most of the help out there is a leaflet, or a video made for people in a hurry.</p><p>This course is different. It is written for older beginners, in plain English, at a gentle pace. Seven short lessons, each ending with one friendly &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; moment &mdash; no scores, no timers, no way to fail. We are 365 Techies, an independent, family-run computer help firm in Bournemouth &mdash; not part of the NHS &mdash; and we wrote this course with the care the subject deserves.</p><p>Read a lesson with a cup of tea, come back tomorrow for the next &mdash; everything waits for you. At the end there is a certificate with your name on it and a one-page checklist to keep by the computer.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#128241;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 7', 'title': 'What the NHS App is &mdash; and honestly is not', 'body': '<p>The NHS App is the NHS&rsquo;s own free, official app, for anyone registered with a GP surgery in England. It lets you do genuinely useful things from your armchair: order repeat prescriptions, see parts of your GP health record and your test results, and &mdash; at many surgeries &mdash; book or manage GP appointments.</p><p>One thing to clear up straight away: this is <strong>not</strong> the old COVID app. That was a separate app for a separate time, and it has been retired. The NHS App is different &mdash; think of it as an everyday front door to your GP surgery.</p><p>And here is an honest note we will repeat through this course, because it saves a great deal of frustration: <strong>exactly what you can do in the app depends on your GP surgery.</strong> Some surgeries have switched on appointment booking; some have not. Some show your full record; some show recent entries only. If a feature seems to be missing, your app is not broken and you have not done anything wrong &mdash; it is simply how your surgery has set things up.</p><p>Finally: the app is not for emergencies &mdash; in an emergency you would still ring 999, and for urgent advice there is 111. It never replaces talking to a real person. It is a helpful extra, not a replacement for your GP.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'A friend says: &ldquo;I deleted that NHS app years ago &mdash; it was the COVID tracking one, wasn&rsquo;t it?&rdquo;', 'q': 'Is your friend right?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Yes &mdash; the NHS App is just the old COVID app under a new name.', 'ok': False, 'why': 'An easy mix-up &mdash; lots of people think this, so you are in good company. But the COVID app was a completely separate app, and it has been retired. The NHS App is the NHS&rsquo;s everyday app for prescriptions, records and appointments.'}, {'t': 'No &mdash; the COVID app was a different app entirely, and it is long gone. The NHS App is for prescriptions, appointments and your GP record.', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. The two apps simply had similar names. The NHS App is the one well worth having on your phone today.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128273;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 7', 'title': 'Getting the app and signing up &mdash; without panic', 'body': '<p>On an Android phone or tablet, the safe place to get the NHS App is the <strong>Google Play</strong> store &mdash; the app shop already on your device. Open Google Play, search for &ldquo;NHS App&rdquo;, and choose the official one from the NHS. It is free, and it will never ask you for payment. (If you use an Apple phone or tablet instead, the very same NHS App is in Apple&rsquo;s App Store &mdash; everything in this course still applies.)</p><p>To use the app you create an <strong>NHS login</strong>. You will need an email address and a mobile number. For full access &mdash; seeing your record, ordering prescriptions &mdash; there are extra identity checks, because this is your medical information and it deserves proper protection. You may be asked to confirm details your surgery holds, or to photograph an identity document. Screens vary and change from time to time, so just follow them at your own pace.</p><p>And here is the reassurance most people need: <strong>signing up can take a whole sitting, and that is completely normal.</strong> Make a cup of tea and take it step by step. If you get stuck, simply stop &mdash; nothing is lost, you can pick it up later, and your GP surgery&rsquo;s reception can often help you finish setting up.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You are partway through signing up. The app asks for extra identity checks, and it is all taking longer than you expected. You start to worry you have done something wrong.', 'q': 'What is the wise move?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Delete the app &mdash; if it were genuine, it would only take a minute.', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It can feel that way, but the careful checks are a good sign, not a bad one. Your medical record deserves far more protection than a shopping app, and that is why sign-up takes longer.'}, {'t': 'Search online for a paid &lsquo;fast-track NHS login&rsquo; service.', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Please never do this one. NHS login is free, and anyone charging to &lsquo;speed it up&rsquo; is at best unnecessary and at worst a scam. Patience costs nothing, and your surgery&rsquo;s reception will help you for free.'}, {'t': 'Take your time &mdash; the extra checks are normal because it is your medical record. Pause and finish later if you like, or ask your surgery&rsquo;s reception for a hand.', 'ok': True, 'why': 'That is exactly it. The careful checks are there to protect you, and taking a whole sitting is very common. Nothing is broken, and nothing is lost by pausing.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128138;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 7', 'title': 'Repeat prescriptions &mdash; the feature that wins people over', 'body': '<p>If you take regular medication, this is the reason to bother with the app at all. Instead of ringing the surgery or dropping off a paper slip, you open the app, find the button for ordering a repeat prescription (the exact wording varies, but it is usually clearly named), tick the medicines you need, and confirm. Once you are used to it, it genuinely takes about a minute.</p><p>Your request goes to your GP to approve, and the prescription is then sent electronically to your <strong>nominated pharmacy</strong> &mdash; the chemist you have chosen to collect from. Allow a few working days before you collect, just as you would with a paper request.</p><p>You can change your nominated pharmacy &mdash; perhaps to one nearer home &mdash; inside the app at many surgeries, or simply by asking the pharmacy or your surgery to change it for you. Either way is fine.</p><p>As ever: whether repeat ordering is switched on, and exactly how it looks, depends on your surgery. If you cannot see it, ask reception &mdash; and do not assume you have done something wrong, because you almost certainly have not.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You ordered your repeat prescription in the app yesterday. You pop to the pharmacy today, and it is not ready yet.', 'q': 'What is most likely going on?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Nothing is wrong &mdash; a repeat request needs a few working days to be approved and sent across. If it is urgent or you are unsure, ring the pharmacy or the surgery.', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Spot on. The order travels from the app to your GP and then to the pharmacy, and each step takes a little time. A quick phone call settles any worry.'}, {'t': 'The app must have lost your order &mdash; order it again several times to be safe.', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A very human instinct, but ordering again and again can cause real confusion at the surgery. One order is enough &mdash; it simply needs time to be approved and sent on.'}, {'t': 'Apps cannot really order medicine &mdash; you will have to go back to paper slips.', 'ok': False, 'why': 'The app really does work &mdash; your GP just has to approve the request first, exactly as they would a paper one. Give it a little time.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128197;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 7', 'title': 'Appointments and your record &mdash; with one human note', 'body': '<p>At many surgeries you can book or cancel GP appointments in the app &mdash; a blessing at eight in the morning when the phone line is engaged. Look for a button named something like &ldquo;Appointments&rdquo;. If your surgery has not switched booking on, you simply will not see it &mdash; that is them, not you.</p><p>You may also be able to see parts of your GP health record and your test results. Here is the human note, because it matters: <strong>results appear in the app when they are ready</strong> &mdash; sometimes before anyone has rung you about them. If you see a result you do not understand, or one that worries you, ring the surgery and ask. A line of medical shorthand on a screen is not a conversation, and the app is never a substitute for talking to your GP.</p><p>Many people find the opposite comfort too: seeing &ldquo;normal &mdash; no further action&rdquo; against a test can save days of quiet worrying by the phone. That, in our experience, is the app at its best.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'A blood test result appears in the app. It contains an abbreviation you do not recognise, and you feel a small knot of worry.', 'q': 'What would you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Type the abbreviation into the internet and prepare for the worst.', 'ok': False, 'why': 'The internet is a poor doctor &mdash; it tends to show you the scariest possibility first. Real answers about your own result come from your own surgery.'}, {'t': 'Ring the surgery, say you have seen the result in the app, and ask what it means for you.', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. Results appear when they are ready, sometimes before anyone has spoken to you &mdash; and asking is precisely what the surgery is there for.'}, {'t': 'Say nothing &mdash; if it mattered, surely someone would have phoned by now.', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Often they would &mdash; but if something is worrying you, you never need permission to ring and ask. That is not being a nuisance; it is being sensible.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#129517;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 7', 'title': 'If your surgery uses something else', 'body': '<p>Here is something that catches people out: not every surgery runs everything through the NHS App. Some use other systems instead, or as well &mdash; two common ones are <strong>Patient Access</strong> and <strong>SystmOnline</strong>. These are legitimate services that many GP surgeries have used for years. Coming across one of them is not a sign that anything is wrong.</p><p>The simplest step is to ask your surgery&rsquo;s reception one plain question: <strong>&ldquo;Which app or website should I use to order prescriptions and book appointments?&rdquo;</strong> They will tell you, and they can usually hand you a leaflet or the sign-up details there and then.</p><p>The good news is that everything you have learned in this course transfers. Ordering a repeat prescription, booking where it is offered, ringing when you are unsure, keeping your login to yourself &mdash; the buttons sit in slightly different places, but the ideas are identical. Learn one system gently and you have really learned them all.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'Your surgery&rsquo;s receptionist says: &ldquo;We mostly use SystmOnline for repeat prescriptions.&rdquo; You have never heard of it.', 'q': 'What do you make of that?', 'opts': [{'t': 'It sounds made up &mdash; only the NHS App is real, so refuse to use it.', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Understandable caution, and caution is a good habit &mdash; but SystmOnline is genuine. Your own surgery telling you face to face is a world away from a stranger&rsquo;s text message.'}, {'t': 'SystmOnline is a legitimate system that some surgeries use. Ask reception to help you get set up &mdash; everything you have learned here still applies.', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Just right. Patient Access and SystmOnline are established services used by many surgeries, and the skills from this course carry straight across.'}, {'t': 'It means you can never manage your prescriptions online.', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Quite the opposite &mdash; it is simply a different door into the same services, and reception will happily show you where the handle is.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128737;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 7', 'title': 'NHS App safety &mdash; and the scams that borrow its name', 'body': '<p>Your medical record is precious, so the NHS App protects it with proper sign-in checks &mdash; and your side of the bargain is wonderfully simple: <strong>never share your NHS login password or security codes with anyone.</strong> Not with a caller, not by text, not by email. No genuine person will ever ask for them.</p><p>Now for the scams. Because so many of us use the NHS, crooks send texts and emails pretending to be it &mdash; a &ldquo;pending prescription fee&rdquo;, a &ldquo;health refund&rdquo;, a &ldquo;missed appointment fine&rdquo;. Remember the one rule from our online safety course: nearly every scam tries to rush you or scare you, so the single best thing you can do is slow down. Then hold on to this plain fact: <strong>the NHS App is free, and the NHS will never ask for your bank or card details by text or email to &ldquo;release&rdquo; a prescription, a result or a refund.</strong></p><p>If a suspicious text arrives, forward it free of charge to <strong>7726</strong>. A suspicious email can go to <strong>report@phishing.gov.uk</strong>. If you ever fear you have given out card or bank details, dial <strong>159</strong> &mdash; a safe line that connects you to most UK banks. And if money has actually been taken, report it to <strong>Action Fraud</strong> as well (in Scotland, you would ring Police Scotland on 101). None of this makes you foolish. Reporting is what sensible, experienced people do.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'A text arrives: &ldquo;NHS: your prescription is ready. Pay &pound;2.99 delivery to release it&rdquo; &mdash; with a link to a payment page.', 'q': 'Scam or real?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Probably real &mdash; &pound;2.99 is far too small an amount for a scammer to bother with.', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Small amounts are exactly how these scams work &mdash; the &pound;2.99 is bait, and the real prize is the card details you would type into their page.'}, {'t': 'Not sure &mdash; click the link just to have a quick look.', 'ok': False, 'why': 'The link itself is the trap: the page will look convincing and ask for your card details. When in doubt, do not click &mdash; check with your pharmacy directly instead.'}, {'t': 'A scam. The NHS does not charge &lsquo;release fees&rsquo; by text. Delete it, forward it free to 7726, and ring your pharmacy if you really are expecting a prescription.', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect judgement. Rush, fear, a small fee and a link &mdash; every classic sign at once. Forwarding to 7726 costs nothing and helps get the senders shut down.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#129309;', 'tag': 'Lesson 7 of 7', 'title': 'Helping a partner or relative', 'body': '<p>Many of us keep an eye on someone else&rsquo;s health &mdash; a husband or wife, a parent, a neighbour. There is a proper way to help with their prescriptions and appointments: it is called <strong>proxy access</strong>, and it means the surgery formally links their care to your account, with their permission. Ask your surgery&rsquo;s reception about it &mdash; they handle these requests all the time, and they will tell you exactly what they need.</p><p>What we gently advise against is sharing logins &mdash; using someone else&rsquo;s NHS login on your phone, or letting them use yours. It feels harmless between family, but a login is designed for one person, and keeping them separate protects you both. If a relative would like the app, the kindest help of all is sitting beside them while they set it up on <strong>their own</strong> device, with their own login &mdash; and after this course, you know every step.</p><p>And if the two of you get stuck along the way, you are not on your own. Ring us, or come and see us &mdash; the details are just below.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'Your other half says: &ldquo;Just put my NHS login on your phone, love &mdash; then you can order my tablets for me.&rdquo;', 'q': 'What is the better way to help them?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Ask the surgery about proxy access, which officially links their prescriptions to your own account &mdash; or help them set the app up on their own phone.', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. Proxy access is the proper, safe way to manage someone else&rsquo;s prescriptions with their permission &mdash; and reception will happily set it in motion.'}, {'t': 'Do as they ask &mdash; sharing logins is fine between husband and wife.', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A kind thought, and a very common one &mdash; but logins are designed for one person each, and there is an official route that works better and protects you both.'}, {'t': 'Tell them nobody is allowed to help anyone else with their prescriptions.', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Helping is allowed and warmly encouraged &mdash; the NHS simply provides a proper way to do it, and that way is called proxy access.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': 'You have finished the whole course &mdash; genuinely well done. You now know more about the NHS App than most people of any age: what it can do, what depends on your surgery, and how to spot the scams that borrow the NHS name. Print your certificate, keep the checklist somewhere handy, and remember: help is only ever one phone call away on 01202 775566 &mdash; and we run friendly in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth. You would be very welcome.', 'certCourseName': 'The NHS App, Gently', 'certSubline': 'has completed this free 365 Techies course on setting up and using the NHS App with care and confidence', 'checklist': ['The NHS App is the NHS&rsquo;s free, official app &mdash; downloading and using it costs nothing.', 'Get it from the Google Play store on an Android phone or tablet &mdash; on an Apple phone or tablet, the same app is in Apple&rsquo;s App Store.', 'Your NHS login is yours alone &mdash; never share the password or security codes with anyone, however nicely they ask.', 'What you can do in the app depends on your GP surgery &mdash; if a feature seems missing, ask reception. It is not you.', 'Repeat prescriptions: order in the app, then allow a few working days before collecting from your nominated pharmacy.', 'Test results appear when they are ready &mdash; if one worries you, ring the surgery. The app never replaces your GP.', 'Patient Access and SystmOnline are legitimate systems some surgeries use instead &mdash; the same good habits apply.', 'The NHS will never ask for bank or card details by text or email to &lsquo;release&rsquo; a prescription, result or refund.', 'Suspicious text? Forward it free to 7726. Suspicious email? Send it to report@phishing.gov.uk. Given out card details? Dial 159 to reach most UK banks &mdash; and if money has been taken, tell Action Fraud too.', 'Stuck or unsure? 365 Techies: 01202 775566 &mdash; or join our in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre, Bournemouth.'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is the NHS App really free?', 'a': 'Yes. At the time of writing it is free to download from Google Play (or Apple&rsquo;s App Store) and free to use. Any message asking you to pay a fee to &lsquo;release&rsquo; a prescription, result or refund is not from the NHS &mdash; delete it and forward it free to 7726.'}, {'q': 'Is this the old COVID app?', 'a': 'No. The COVID app was a separate app for a separate time, and it has been retired. The NHS App is the NHS&rsquo;s everyday app for repeat prescriptions, appointments and your GP health record.'}, {'q': 'My friend can book appointments in the app but I cannot &mdash; is mine broken?', 'a': 'Almost certainly not. Features are switched on surgery by surgery, so your friend&rsquo;s surgery has enabled booking and yours may not have &mdash; or may use a different system such as Patient Access or SystmOnline. Ask your reception what is available. Nothing is wrong with your phone or your app.'}, {'q': 'Do I need to be good with computers to take this course?', 'a': 'Not at all. It is written for complete beginners, in plain English, one short lesson at a time. There is no sign-up, no timer and no score &mdash; you cannot fail, and you can come back whenever suits you.'}, {'q': 'Is my medical information safe in the app?', 'a': 'The app uses proper identity checks precisely because medical records deserve strong protection &mdash; that is why signing up takes a little longer than a shopping app. Your side of the bargain is simple: keep your NHS login password and codes to yourself, always.'}, {'q': 'Can I get help in person?', 'a': 'Yes, gladly. 365 Techies is an independent, family-run firm in Bournemouth &mdash; not part of the NHS &mdash; and we have been going since 1995. Ring us on 01202 775566, or come along to our friendly in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre &mdash; bring your phone and your questions.'}], 'crumbName': 'The NHS App, Gently'},
{'slug': 'digital-photos-course', 'title': 'Free Digital Photos Course for Over-60s | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'Keep every precious photo safe. Free, senior-paced lessons on backing up your Android phone, finding old pictures, sharing on WhatsApp and printing them.', 'h1': 'Your photos, <em class="grad grad--green">safe and sorted</em>', 'heroLede': 'A free, friendly course from the family-run firm that has been untangling technology in Bournemouth since 1995. Six short lessons show you where your photos really live, how to back them up so a lost phone never means lost memories, and how to find, share, print and tidy them &mdash; all at your own pace, with a printable certificate at the end. No jargon, nothing to install, nothing to pay.', 'icon': '&#128247;', 'courseTitle': 'Your Photos, Safe and Sorted', 'tagline': 'Free &middot; 6 short lessons &middot; about 35 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>Photos are not like other things on a phone. They are the grandchildren on the beach, the golden wedding, the garden at its best, faces we love and faces we miss. Losing them hurts in a way losing an app never could &mdash; which is why this little course exists. In six short lessons you will learn where your photos actually live, how to make sure a lost or broken phone can never take them with it, and then the nice part: finding them again, sharing them with the family, printing the special ones and giving your phone a good tidy.</p><p>Work through the lessons in order if you can &mdash; the first two matter most &mdash; or dip in wherever you like. After each lesson there is a gentle &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; question. It is not a test; there is no timer and no pass mark, just a chance to feel the ideas settle in. Your certificate is waiting at the end.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#128241;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 6', 'title': 'Where your photos actually live', 'body': '<p>Here is the single most important idea in this course, and it is worth a cup of tea and a moment&rsquo;s thought: a photo on your phone normally lives <strong>in the phone itself</strong>, on a little memory chip inside the handset. Unless something has been set up to say otherwise, that is the only copy in the world. Drop the phone in the bath, leave it on a bus, or have it simply give up one morning, and the photos go with it.</p><p>The answer is a <strong>backup</strong>: a second copy of every photo, kept somewhere safe away from the phone. Once a backup is working, the phone becomes the least important part of the arrangement. Phones can be replaced; the pictures of the grandchildren cannot. People often assume every phone does this automatically &mdash; some are set up that way and some are not, and the only way to be certain is to check, which is exactly what the next lesson walks you through.</p><p>A gentle way to test where you stand: imagine your phone vanished this afternoon. If the thought of the photos makes your stomach drop, this course has arrived at just the right time &mdash; by the end of the next lesson, that feeling should be gone. (One quick note: Apple iPhones have their own equivalent arrangements. This course sticks to Android phones, but the ideas are the same everywhere.)</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'A friend rings you, heartbroken. Her phone was stolen on holiday, and every photo of her late husband was on it. Nothing had been set up on the phone beforehand.', 'q': 'What would have kept those photos safe?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Nothing &mdash; when a phone goes, its photos are always lost with it', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It certainly feels that way, and without a backup it is sadly true. But it does not have to be: a backup keeps a second copy safely away from the phone, so even a stolen handset cannot take the photos with it. That is exactly what this course sets up.'}, {'t': 'A backup &mdash; a second copy of every photo, kept safely away from the phone itself', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. Once a backup is working, a lost phone is an expensive nuisance &mdash; but never a lost lifetime of pictures. The next lesson shows you how to check yours.'}, {'t': 'A stronger phone case', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A good case is a sensible thing to have, but it would not have helped here &mdash; the phone was stolen, not dropped. Only a second copy kept away from the phone protects against every mishap: theft, loss, water and old age alike.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#9729;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 6', 'title': 'Backing up without tears', 'body': '<p>On Android phones, the usual tool for the job is the <strong>Google Photos</strong> app &mdash; its icon looks like a small, colourful pinwheel. When its backup is switched on, every photo you take is quietly copied to your Google account over the internet. You do not have to do anything; it happens in the background, usually while the phone is on Wi-Fi.</p><p>People talk about photos going &ldquo;to the cloud&rdquo;, which sounds mysterious and slightly flimsy. It is neither. The cloud simply means <strong>Google&rsquo;s own secure computers</strong>, kept in professionally run buildings &mdash; think of it as a safe-deposit box for your pictures. Your photos still appear on your phone exactly as before; there is simply a second copy elsewhere. As for cost: backup is free within the <strong>15&nbsp;GB of storage</strong> that comes with every Google account (shared with Gmail and Google Drive), at the time of writing &mdash; comfortably room for thousands of ordinary photos.</p><p>To check yours is on: open Google Photos and tap your <strong>profile picture or initial</strong>, usually in a top corner. A short message tells you whether backup is on, off, or still working. Screens vary between phones and change over time, so do not worry about exact wording &mdash; you are simply looking for the word <strong>backup</strong> alongside something reassuring like &ldquo;on&rdquo; or &ldquo;complete&rdquo;. If it says backup is off and you are unsure what to press, that is a perfect thing to ask a family member &mdash; or us &mdash; to check with you. It takes two minutes, and it is the best two minutes in this whole course.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You open Google Photos and tap your profile picture, but the message on the screen is worded differently from anything this lesson described, and you cannot quite tell whether backup is on.', 'q': 'What is the wisest move?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Look for the word &ldquo;backup&rdquo; on that screen and read what it says &mdash; and if you are still unsure, ask someone you trust to check it with you before assuming anything', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect. Screens change their wording all the time, but the word &ldquo;backup&rdquo; is your landmark. And asking for a second pair of eyes is not a failure &mdash; it is exactly what careful people do with precious things.'}, {'t': 'Assume it must be on &mdash; phones do all this automatically these days', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A very common assumption, and sometimes it is even true &mdash; but sometimes it is not, and with photos this precious, &ldquo;sometimes&rdquo; is not good enough. Two minutes of checking buys years of peace of mind.'}, {'t': 'Turn the phone off and on again until the message changes', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Restarting cures many small oddities, but it will not switch a backup on. Reading the backup screen &mdash; with a helper alongside if you like &mdash; is the sure way to know.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128269;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 6', 'title': 'Finding photos again', 'body': '<p>Once your photos are safe, the next pleasure is finding them again. Google Photos keeps everything in <strong>date order</strong>, newest at the top, so you can simply scroll back through the months like turning the pages of a diary. On most phones there is also a quick way to skim &mdash; sliding a finger along the edge of the screen whisks you back through whole years in a second or two.</p><p>The real magic, though, is the <strong>search box</strong>. Type a month, a year or a place &mdash; &ldquo;June 2023&rdquo;, &ldquo;Weymouth&rdquo; &mdash; and matching photos usually appear. Better still, it can often recognise what is <em>in</em> the pictures: try typing &ldquo;dog&rdquo;, &ldquo;beach&rdquo;, &ldquo;birthday cake&rdquo; or &ldquo;roses&rdquo; and prepare to smile. It is not perfect and may miss the odd one, but it beats scrolling through years by hand every time.</p><p>For the truly special photos, make an <strong>album</strong> &mdash; the digital version of the leather-bound book on the shelf. Look for an option called something like &ldquo;New album&rdquo;, give it a name (&ldquo;Golden Wedding&rdquo;, &ldquo;Cornwall 2024&rdquo;) and add your favourites. Nothing is moved or lost &mdash; an album is simply a tidy way of gathering the best ones, so you and your family can enjoy an occasion without wading through every snap of the car park.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'A friend visits, and you want to show her a photo of your dog paddling at the beach &mdash; taken two or three summers ago, somewhere among thousands of pictures.', 'q': 'What is the quickest way to find it?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Scroll back from today, photo by photo, until you reach it', 'ok': False, 'why': 'You would get there in the end, and there is nothing wrong with a good scroll down memory lane &mdash; but your friend might need a second pot of tea. The search box can make the same journey in seconds.'}, {'t': 'Accept that a photo that old is as good as lost', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Happily, no! Nothing in your library is lost &mdash; it is all still there, in date order. And with the search box, even a years-old photo is only a few taps away.'}, {'t': 'Tap the search box and try a word like &ldquo;dog&rdquo; or &ldquo;beach&rdquo;, or the year you think it was', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. Search understands places, dates and often what is in the picture itself. Between &ldquo;dog&rdquo;, &ldquo;beach&rdquo; and a year, that paddling photo does not stand a chance of staying hidden.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128140;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 6', 'title': 'Sharing the joy', 'body': '<p>Photos double in value when they are shared. For <strong>one or two pictures</strong>, the easiest route for most families is WhatsApp: open the chat, tap the attach or camera symbol, choose the photos and send. Each person receives their own copy to keep. (If WhatsApp is new to you, we have a free step-by-step course for that too &mdash; it pairs beautifully with this one.)</p><p>For a <strong>whole occasion</strong> &mdash; forty photos of a christening, say &mdash; sending them one by one becomes a chore at both ends. This is where a <strong>shared album</strong> shines. In Google Photos you can share an album so the family can browse the whole set in one tidy place, full-size and in order; you can usually keep adding photos afterwards and everyone sees the new ones too. Think of it as passing the album round the room rather than posting out individual prints.</p><p>One small piece of judgement to finish: some sharing works by creating a <strong>link</strong>, and depending on the options chosen, anyone who has that link may be able to view the album. So share albums with family and friends you know, and think twice before posting such a link anywhere public. Family joy, family audience.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You took forty lovely photos at your granddaughter&rsquo;s birthday party, and the whole family is asking to see them.', 'q': 'What is the kindest way to share the whole set?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Send all forty photos one by one in WhatsApp messages', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It would work, and nobody ever complained about too many pictures of the birthday girl &mdash; but it is a lot of tapping for you and a flood of pings for them. WhatsApp is perfect for a handful; for forty, an album is kinder all round.'}, {'t': 'Have all forty printed and post a set to every household', 'ok': False, 'why': 'What a generous thought &mdash; and prints do make wonderful gifts, which is exactly what the next lesson is about. But for simply letting everyone see the day, a shared album delivers the joy today, and you can save printing for the very best few.'}, {'t': 'Gather the best ones into an album and share that, so everyone can browse the full set in one place', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Lovely choice. One share, one tidy place, full-size photos &mdash; and you can add the stragglers later, and everyone sees those too.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128424;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 6', 'title': 'Printing them', 'body': '<p>A photo on a screen is lovely; a photo on the mantelpiece is part of the furniture. Happily, printing from a phone is far easier than most people expect. Supermarkets and high-street chemists offer <strong>photo printing</strong> &mdash; many have a self-service kiosk in store where you can print straight from your phone, and staff are generally happy to walk you through it the first time. If you prefer, photos can usually be brought in on a <strong>USB stick</strong> instead.</p><p>Before you go, spend a happy ten minutes choosing. An album you made in Lesson 3 is the perfect shopping list &mdash; the best of the bunch, already gathered in one place. There is no need to print everything; a handful of well-chosen prints beats a drawer full of unopened packets, and you can always go back for more.</p><p>And for a present that beats socks every single year: a <strong>photo book</strong> &mdash; a proper printed book of your own pictures. A year of the grandchildren, a golden-wedding album, a life in the garden. The same shops and their websites can usually help you make one. Services and prices vary from place to place, so it is worth a browse &mdash; but the short version is this: those pictures do not have to stay inside the phone.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'There is a photo on your phone from your daughter&rsquo;s wedding that deserves a frame on the wall &mdash; but you have never printed anything from a phone before.', 'q': 'What is a straightforward way to get it printed?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Accept that phone photos cannot really be printed &mdash; that needs a proper camera', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A very understandable belief, but happily wrong. Modern phone photos print beautifully, often at surprisingly large sizes. That wedding photo is one short errand away from its frame.'}, {'t': 'Take your phone to a supermarket or high-street chemist with a photo counter or kiosk &mdash; and ask the staff to help you the first time', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. Printing from a phone is an everyday request in those shops, and asking for help the first time is precisely what the kiosks and staff are there for. Next time, you will do it without thinking.'}, {'t': 'Take a photo of the phone screen with another camera and print that instead', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Full marks for ingenuity &mdash; but there is no need, and the copy would look far worse than the original. The shops can print directly from the phone itself, at full quality.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#129529;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 6', 'title': 'A tidy-up that frees your phone', 'body': '<p>Sooner or later, every phone complains that its storage is nearly full &mdash; and photos are usually the reason. The temptation is to start deleting. So here is the golden rule of this whole course, and it deserves the bold type: <strong>never delete photos until you have confirmed your backup is on and up to date</strong>. Deleting before backing up is how memories get lost; deleting after is just housekeeping.</p><p>Once backup is confirmed (Lesson 2 showed you how to check), tidying is rather satisfying. Blurry shots, the seventeen near-identical attempts at the same sunset, the accidental photos of the inside of a pocket &mdash; out they can go. And do not panic if you delete the wrong one: deleted photos sit in a <strong>bin</strong> inside the app for a good while before they disappear for good, so a slip can usually be rescued.</p><p>Better still, Google Photos has a feature &mdash; usually called something like <strong>&ldquo;Free up space&rdquo;</strong> &mdash; that does the clever part for you. It removes from the handset only the copies that are <em>already safely backed up</em>; the photos still appear in the app exactly as before, fetched from your backup whenever you look at them. The result feels like magic: a phone with room to breathe, and not a single memory harmed. Menu names shift over time, but the idea never does &mdash; back up first, then let the phone shed the copies it no longer needs to carry.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'Your phone warns that its storage is nearly full, and photos are clearly the culprit. You would like the space back today.', 'q': 'What is the SAFE first step?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Check that backup is on and up to date first &mdash; and only then delete, or use the free-up-space feature', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly the right order, and the single most important habit in this course. Backup confirmed first, tidy-up second &mdash; the phone gets its space back, and every memory stays safe.'}, {'t': 'Start deleting a few hundred photos straight away &mdash; the message did say nearly full', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Tempting, and quick &mdash; but this is the one order of events that can genuinely lose memories. If backup happens to be off, deleted photos may be gone for good. Check the backup first; the deleting can wait an hour.'}, {'t': 'Ignore the warning and simply stop taking new photos', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That avoids the danger, but it also means missing the next birthday &mdash; and the warning will only come back. There is a safe way to reclaim the space; it just begins with a backup check, not with deleting.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': '<p>Congratulations &mdash; you have finished the course, and your photos are now safer and better sorted than most people&rsquo;s ever get. If you keep just one habit, make it this: <strong>check your backup before you ever delete</strong>. Everything else &mdash; the finding, the sharing, the printing, the tidying &mdash; flows happily from knowing there is always a second copy.</p><p>And remember, you are never on your own with technology. Real, friendly help is one phone call away on <strong>01202 775566</strong>, and we run relaxed in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth &mdash; we would gladly sit down and check your photo backup with you. Do come and say hello.</p>', 'certCourseName': 'Your Photos, Safe and Sorted', 'certSubline': 'Backing up phone photos, finding and organising them, sharing with family, printing keepsakes and tidying safely', 'checklist': ['A photo on your phone is usually the only copy in the world &mdash; until a backup is switched on.', 'Check your backup in Google Photos: tap your profile picture or initial and look for the word &ldquo;backup&rdquo; &mdash; you want &ldquo;on&rdquo; or &ldquo;complete&rdquo;.', 'Backup is free within your Google account&rsquo;s 15 GB allowance (shared with Gmail and Google Drive), at the time of writing.', 'The cloud simply means a second copy kept on Google&rsquo;s secure computers &mdash; your photos still appear on the phone as normal.', 'Find any photo with the search box: try a place, a month, a year, or a word like &ldquo;dog&rdquo; or &ldquo;beach&rdquo;.', 'Gather the special ones into albums &mdash; the digital version of the album on the shelf.', 'Share one or two photos by WhatsApp; share a whole occasion as an album, so everyone sees the full set in one place.', 'Only share album links with people you know &mdash; anyone holding a link may be able to view it.', 'Supermarkets and high-street chemists print straight from your phone or a USB stick &mdash; and photo books make wonderful gifts.', 'The golden rule: never delete photos until backup is confirmed &mdash; then tidy freely, or use the free-up-space feature.'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is this course really free?', 'a': '<p>Completely free, with no catch and no card details asked for. We are a family-run IT firm that has looked after Bournemouth homes and businesses since 1995, and this course is our way of helping local people keep the photos that matter most to them.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I need to pay for photo storage?', 'a': '<p>Usually not. Every Google account comes with 15&nbsp;GB of free storage (shared with Gmail and Google Drive) at the time of writing, which holds thousands of ordinary phone photos. If you are a very keen photographer and ever approach the limit, paid options exist &mdash; but most people never need them, and we are happy to advise if you do.</p>'}, {'q': 'I have an Apple iPhone &mdash; is this course still for me?', 'a': '<p>The big ideas apply to every phone: back up first, use albums for the special ones, share kindly, print the keepers, and never delete until the backup is confirmed. The step-by-step details here are written for Android phones, but Apple has its own equivalents of everything mentioned &mdash; Apple&rsquo;s own guides and a confident family member are the best companions for the iPhone-specific steps, and every habit this course teaches will serve you just as well there.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is the cloud actually safe? What if my photos disappear?', 'a': '<p>A backed-up photo lives on professionally run, well-guarded computers &mdash; and it still lives on your phone too, so you have two copies instead of one. No arrangement in life is beyond all doubt, but two copies are enormously safer than the single copy most people rely on. For the handful of truly irreplaceable pictures, a printed copy in a frame or album is a lovely third layer of protection.</p>'}, {'q': 'What happens to my photos when I get a new phone?', 'a': '<p>This is where backup pays for itself. Sign in to the new phone with the same Google account, open Google Photos, and your backed-up pictures appear &mdash; the whole collection, ready and waiting. A broken or lost phone works the same way: the photos were never really in the handset once backup was on; they were safe in your account all along.</p>'}, {'q': 'Can someone help me set all this up in person?', 'a': '<p>Gladly. Ring us on <strong>01202 775566</strong> and we will happily check your backup over the phone or arrange a visit &mdash; and we run friendly, unhurried in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth, where checking a photo backup is one of the most popular requests. Bring your phone and your questions.</p>'}], 'crumbName': 'Your Photos, Safe and Sorted'},
{'slug': 'online-shopping-safely-course', 'title': 'Free Online Shopping Safety Course for Over-60s | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'Shop online with confidence. A free, gentle course for older beginners covering safe payment, Section 75, refund rights and shopping scams. Printable certificate.', 'h1': 'Online shopping, <em class="grad grad--green">with confidence</em>', 'heroLede': 'A free, gentle course from the family-run firm that has looked after Bournemouth&rsquo;s computers since 1995. Seven short lessons cover where to buy, how to pay wisely, your UK refund rights and how to spot the scams &mdash; all at your own pace, with a printable certificate at the end. No jargon, no rushing, nothing to pay.', 'icon': '&#128722;', 'courseTitle': 'Online Shopping, Safely', 'tagline': 'Free &middot; 7 short lessons &middot; about 40 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>Welcome &mdash; and what a sensible thing to learn. Shopping online is one of the genuine joys of the internet: the whole high street from your armchair, delivered to your door. Millions of people across the UK do it safely every single day &mdash; and with a handful of simple habits, you can too. This course gives you those habits, plus something most people never learn: your actual rights as a UK shopper, in plain English.</p><p>Work through the seven lessons in order, or dip in wherever you like. After each one there is a gentle &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; question &mdash; not a test, just a chance to feel the ideas settle. There is no timer and no pass mark, and your certificate is waiting at the end.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#128717;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 7', 'title': 'Why shopping online is genuinely fine', 'body': '<p>Let&rsquo;s start with the honest truth: shopping online is not a young person&rsquo;s trick or a gamble. Millions of purchases are made safely in the UK every single day &mdash; groceries, birthday presents, garden plants, the spare part nobody stocks in town &mdash; and the overwhelming majority arrive without a hitch. The whole high street comes to your armchair, with no bus, no queue and no carrying.</p><p>Here is the part that surprises people: <strong>paying by card online usually protects you better than cash ever did.</strong> Hand over cash at a market stall and, if the radio turns out to be broken, getting your money back can be somewhere between difficult and hopeless. Pay by card online and there is a record of exactly what you bought and when, a legal right to return most purchases, and &mdash; for bigger buys on a credit card &mdash; the card company itself standing behind the purchase. We will meet each of these protections properly in the coming lessons.</p><p>So why does it feel risky? Because bad stories travel and quiet successes do not. Nearly every shopping scam works the same way: it tries to rush you or dazzle you with a bargain. Which means the single best habit costs nothing at all: <strong>slow down before you pay.</strong> This course is simply that habit, unpacked into a handful of easy checks.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'A well-meaning neighbour hears you are thinking of ordering a new kettle online. &ldquo;Ooh, I wouldn&rsquo;t,&rdquo; she says. &ldquo;My cousin&rsquo;s friend got scammed. Cash in a real shop is the only safe way.&rdquo;', 'q': 'What is closest to the truth?', 'opts': [{'t': 'She is right &mdash; shopping online is a gamble, and cash is always safer', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A very understandable feeling &mdash; bad stories travel fast. But cash comes with no record and no way of calling the payment back once it leaves your hand. Pay by card online and you have a record, return rights and often the card company standing behind you.'}, {'t': 'Shopping online is genuinely safe with a few simple habits &mdash; and card payments often protect you better than cash', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. Millions of UK purchases happen safely every day, and the rest of this course gives you the small handful of habits that keep it that way.'}, {'t': 'It is safe, but only if you never spend more than a few pounds', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A gentle instinct, but the amount is not what keeps you safe &mdash; the habits are. In fact, larger purchases on a credit card come with extra legal protection, as you will see in Lesson 3.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128269;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 7', 'title': 'Choosing where to buy', 'body': '<p>The easiest rule in this whole course: <strong>start with names you already know.</strong> The big supermarkets, the department stores, the household-name shops &mdash; their websites are run as carefully as their tills. Type the shop&rsquo;s web address into your browser yourself, or use their official app, rather than following links from emails or adverts.</p><p>Found something lovely on a website you have never heard of? Nothing wrong with that &mdash; some of the best small shops live only online. But before you buy, do one thing: <strong>search the shop&rsquo;s name together with the word &ldquo;reviews&rdquo;</strong> and spend two minutes reading. A genuine shop leaves a trail of real customers; a fake one leaves a trail of warnings. While you are at it, look for a proper UK contact address and phone number &mdash; and be wary of prices that undercut everyone else by half or more. <strong>Too cheap to be true is the oldest lure there is.</strong></p><p>One myth to retire while we are here. The little padlock (and the &ldquo;https&rdquo;) beside a website&rsquo;s address means the <strong>connection</strong> between you and the site is private &mdash; nobody can eavesdrop on what you type. It does <strong>not</strong> mean the shop itself is honest. Scam websites can have padlocks too; they are cheap and easy to get. So enjoy the padlock as a nice-to-have, but judge a shop by its name, its reviews and its prices.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'Searching for a garden bench, you find a website you have never heard of selling a well-known brand at a quarter of the price everywhere else. The site looks smart and has a padlock beside its address. A banner says only two are left.', 'q': 'What would you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Pause, and search the shop&rsquo;s name plus the word &ldquo;reviews&rdquo; before spending a penny', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect. An unknown name, an unbelievable price and a hurry-up message are exactly the moment to slow down. Two minutes of reading what other customers say will usually tell you everything you need to know.'}, {'t': 'Buy it &mdash; the padlock proves the site is trustworthy', 'ok': False, 'why': 'One of the most common mix-ups, so you are in good company. The padlock only means the connection is private; scam sites can have padlocks too. It says nothing about whether the shop is honest.'}, {'t': 'Buy it quickly, before the last two are gone', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That &lsquo;only two left&rsquo; message is doing exactly what it was designed to do &mdash; rush you. Nearly every scam works the same way: it hurries or frightens you. A real bargain will survive a cup of tea and a little checking.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128179;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 7', 'title': 'Paying wisely', 'body': '<p>How you pay matters just as much as where you buy. For anything costing more than &pound;100, reach for a <strong>credit card</strong> if you have one. Under a rule called <strong>Section 75</strong>, when an item costs more than &pound;100 (and up to &pound;30,000) the card company is jointly responsible with the shop. In plain English: if the goods never arrive, arrive broken, or the shop disappears, you can claim your money back from the card company itself. It is one of the strongest consumer protections in Britain &mdash; and it comes free with the card.</p><p>Paying by <strong>debit card</strong>? You still have a helpful route called <strong>chargeback</strong>, where your bank asks for the money back from the shop&rsquo;s bank. It often works &mdash; but it is a voluntary industry scheme rather than a legal right, so it is worth knowing the difference. Services such as <strong>PayPal</strong> add another kind of comfort: you pay through them, so the shop never sees your card details at all &mdash; handy with smaller or less familiar shops.</p><p>And one payment method to refuse outright: <strong>never pay a shop or a stranger by bank transfer.</strong> A transfer is like posting cash &mdash; once sent, it is very hard to recover, which is exactly why fraudsters love it. Most UK banks now check that the name on the receiving account matches (a system called Confirmation of Payee), but a matching name does not make a seller honest. If a &ldquo;shop&rdquo; will only take a bank transfer, that is your cue to leave.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You have found a &pound;600 sofa on a furniture website. At the checkout, the shop suggests paying by bank transfer &ldquo;to avoid card fees&rdquo; and gives you their account details.', 'q': 'How would you pay?', 'opts': [{'t': 'By bank transfer &mdash; saving a fee sounds sensible', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A very natural thought, but a bank transfer is like posting cash: if the sofa never arrives, there is usually no way to pull the money back. Honest shops take cards &mdash; be very wary of any that steer you towards a transfer.'}, {'t': 'Half by transfer and half by card, to spread the risk', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Careful thinking, but sadly the transferred half would still be unprotected &mdash; and a shop steering you away from cards at all is itself the warning sign here.'}, {'t': 'Politely refuse and pay by credit card &mdash; or walk away if they will not take one', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Spot on. On a &pound;600 purchase, a credit card brings Section 75 protection: the card company is jointly responsible if things go wrong. A shop that refuses cards and insists on a transfer is telling you something important about itself.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#9878;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 7', 'title': 'Your rights, in plain English', 'body': '<p>Here is something that surprises many people: when you shop online in the UK, your rights are generally <strong>stronger</strong> than in a physical shop. Because you could not see or touch the goods before buying, the law gives most online purchases a <strong>14-day cooling-off period</strong> &mdash; at the time of writing, you can tell the shop you have changed your mind within 14 days of delivery and return the item for a refund, even if there is nothing wrong with it. Not goodwill; a right.</p><p>There are a few sensible exceptions, exactly where you would expect them: perishables such as fresh flowers and food, items personalised or made specially for you, and sealed goods such as software or discs once opened. Returning something usually means paying the return postage unless the shop offers free returns &mdash; worth a glance at their returns page before you buy anything bulky.</p><p>The second right matters even more: <strong>faulty goods rights do not run out after 14 days.</strong> If the kettle fails after three weeks or the zip breaks in month two, that is a fault, and the shop that sold it must put things right with a repair, replacement or refund. Your contract is with the shop that took your money &mdash; not the manufacturer &mdash; so go back to the shop first and be politely persistent. If you ever feel fobbed off, Citizens Advice offers free help with exactly this sort of thing.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'Three weeks after arriving, the kettle you bought online stops working. A friend says: &ldquo;You have had it more than 14 days &mdash; nothing you can do now.&rdquo;', 'q': 'Is your friend right?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Yes &mdash; after 14 days you have no rights at all', 'ok': False, 'why': 'An easy mix-up, because the two things sound similar. The 14-day period is for changing your mind. Faulty goods are different &mdash; those rights last much longer, so the shop still owes you a repair, replacement or refund.'}, {'t': 'It depends on whether you kept the box', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Good news: no. Keeping packaging can make returns a little easier, but your rights over faulty goods do not depend on a cardboard box. Get in touch with the shop and explain.'}, {'t': 'No &mdash; the 14 days is for changing your mind; faulty goods rights last much longer', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. A kettle that fails after three weeks is a faulty-goods matter, and your contract is with the shop that sold it. Contact them, explain what has happened, and don&rsquo;t be fobbed off.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#129309;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 7', 'title': 'Buying from people, not shops', 'body': '<p>Websites such as <strong>Facebook Marketplace</strong> &mdash; and auction sites such as eBay when the seller is a private individual &mdash; are a different world from shopping with a business. Think of them as a giant car-boot sale. There are genuine bargains to be had, but the consumer rights from the last lesson mostly apply when you buy from a business. Buying from an individual, you have far fewer protections &mdash; so your habits have to do the protecting instead.</p><p>The golden rule: <strong>see it before you pay for it.</strong> Arrange to view and collect locally, bring a friend or family member along for anything big, and hand over payment only when the item is in front of you. A genuine seller will find all of that perfectly normal. And <strong>never send a bank transfer to secure something you have not seen</strong> &mdash; not the full price, and not a &ldquo;small deposit&rdquo; either. Money sent to hold an item you have never laid eyes on is one of the most common ways people lose out on marketplaces.</p><p>Be gently sceptical of pressure, too. &ldquo;Lots of interest,&rdquo; &ldquo;first to pay gets it,&rdquo; a seller who cannot manage a viewing but can somehow arrange a courier &mdash; these are the rhythms of a marketplace scam. Friendly messages and lovely photos cost a scammer nothing; photos are often simply copied from real adverts elsewhere. If you miss one greenhouse, another will come along. Nothing on a marketplace is ever the last one.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You spot a greenhouse for sale on Facebook Marketplace, thirty miles away. The seller says there has been &ldquo;lots of interest&rdquo; and asks for a bank transfer today to secure it &mdash; they will even deliver it free.', 'q': 'What would you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Say you will come and see it, and pay only when you collect it in person', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly the golden rule: see it, then pay. Buying from an individual, you have far fewer protections than with a shop, so never send money for something you have not seen. A genuine seller will understand completely.'}, {'t': 'Send a small deposit to hold it &mdash; that limits the risk', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A very common compromise &mdash; and sadly the exact shape of this scam. Deposits sent to strangers vanish just as thoroughly as full payments, and &lsquo;lots of interest&rsquo; is usually just pressure. Hold your nerve and offer to pay on collection.'}, {'t': 'Send the full amount &mdash; the messages have been friendly and the photos look real', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Friendly messages and nice photos cost a scammer nothing &mdash; photos are often copied from real adverts elsewhere. None of it proves the greenhouse exists. Pay when it is standing in front of you.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128680;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 7', 'title': 'Spotting shopping scams', 'body': '<p>Shopping scams almost never start with a shop &mdash; they start with a <strong>message</strong>. Among the most common in Britain is the fake delivery text: &ldquo;your parcel is held, pay a small redelivery fee,&rdquo; with a link. It often borrows the name of Royal Mail or a well-known courier, and the fee is tiny on purpose &mdash; &pound;1.99 feels too small to question. But the fee is not the prize. The page behind the link is designed to collect your card details, and sometimes a follow-up phone call pretending to be your bank finishes the job.</p><p>Its cousin is the fake <strong>order confirmation</strong>: an email or text thanking you for a &pound;500 order you never made, with a link or phone number to &ldquo;cancel.&rdquo; The panic is the point. The safe response to both is the same, and it is wonderfully simple: <strong>never click the link, never call the number.</strong> If you want to check, go to the shop or courier yourself &mdash; type the address into your browser or open their official app &mdash; and look at your orders there. Anything real will be waiting for you; anything missing was never real.</p><p>And if a scam does slip through, act quickly and without a shred of embarrassment. Forward scam texts to <strong>7726</strong> (free), and scam emails to <strong>report@phishing.gov.uk</strong>. If you typed in card or bank details, ring your bank straight away &mdash; dialling <strong>159</strong> connects you safely to most UK banks. Then report it to Action Fraud on <strong>0300 123 2040</strong> (in Scotland, ring Police Scotland on 101). These scams fool solicitors, teachers and IT engineers &mdash; falling for a good one is not foolishness, it is bad luck.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You are expecting a parcel this week. A text arrives: &ldquo;Your package is held at our depot. A &pound;1.99 redelivery fee is required,&rdquo; with a link to pay. It looks quite official.', 'q': 'What would you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Pay it &mdash; &pound;1.99 is hardly worth worrying about', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That is exactly what the scam relies on &mdash; the fee sounds too small to question. But the page behind the link is after your card details, not your &pound;1.99. The small amount is the bait, not the prize.'}, {'t': 'Don&rsquo;t click &mdash; check your order by going to the shop or courier yourself, and forward the text to 7726', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect on every count. Fake delivery texts are among the most common scams in Britain &mdash; and because you really were expecting a parcel, the timing feels convincing. Check from your side, never through their link; forwarding to 7726 is free and helps shut them down.'}, {'t': 'Click the link just to have a look, without paying anything', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Understandable curiosity, but those pages are built to look perfectly real, and clicking can lead you gently into typing details before you realise. Safer never to open the door at all.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128203;', 'tag': 'Lesson 7 of 7', 'title': 'Keeping records: your paper trail', 'body': '<p>One last habit turns you from a hopeful shopper into a well-armed one: <strong>keep your paper trail.</strong> When you order something, the shop sends a confirmation email &mdash; your receipt, your proof of the price, and the record of what was promised. Keep it at least until the item has arrived and you are happy with it. Better still, make a little folder in your email called &ldquo;Shopping&rdquo; and pop confirmations in there &mdash; tidy and safe in one move.</p><p>For bigger purchases, take a <strong>screenshot</strong> of the item&rsquo;s page showing the price and description before you buy. Most phones and computers can capture the screen with a button press or two &mdash; screens vary from device to device, so if you are not sure how on yours, ask and we will happily show you. If a price or promise quietly changes later, your screenshot remembers exactly what you were offered.</p><p>And if something does go wrong, keep notes: the date you got in touch, who you spoke to, what was agreed. Put complaints in writing &mdash; email is perfect &mdash; so there is a record on both sides. This is exactly the evidence that makes a return, a Section 75 claim or a chargeback quick and painless. Ninety-nine times in a hundred you will never need any of it; the hundredth time, you will be very glad it is there.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': 'You have just ordered a winter coat, and the confirmation email has arrived. You are having a tidy-up of your inbox and are tempted to delete it.', 'q': 'What would you do with it?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Keep it at least until the coat has arrived and you are happy with it', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Just right. That one email is your receipt, your proof of price and your ticket to any refund, replacement or card-company claim. A little &lsquo;Shopping&rsquo; folder in your email keeps things tidy without losing anything.'}, {'t': 'Delete it &mdash; the shop keeps records, so you don&rsquo;t need to', 'ok': False, 'why': 'The shop does keep records &mdash; but records that suit the shop. If anything goes wrong, that email is your proof of what you ordered, when, and for how much. It weighs nothing; let it stay.'}, {'t': 'Forward it straight to your bank, just in case', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A cautious instinct, which is no bad thing &mdash; but there is no need to involve the bank unless something actually goes wrong. Simply keeping the email safe is all the preparation you need.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': '<p>Congratulations &mdash; you have finished the course, and you now know more about safe online shopping than most people who do it every week. If you keep just one habit, make it this: <strong>pause before you pay.</strong> Right shop (checked), right price (believable), right payment (credit card, debit card or PayPal &mdash; never a bank transfer to a stranger). Everything else follows from that one calm moment.</p><p>And remember, you are never on your own with any of this. Real, friendly help is one phone call away on <strong>01202 775566</strong>, and if you would like a human being beside you while you practise, our relaxed in-person lessons run at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth. Happy shopping!</p>', 'certCourseName': 'Online Shopping, Safely', 'certSubline': 'For learning the golden rules of safe online shopping &mdash; choosing trustworthy shops, paying wisely, UK refund rights and spotting delivery-text scams', 'checklist': ['Start with shops you know &mdash; and type the web address yourself rather than following links in emails or adverts.', 'New shop? Search its name plus the word &ldquo;reviews&rdquo; before you spend a penny.', 'A price that undercuts everyone else by half is a warning, not a bargain.', 'The padlock means the connection is private &mdash; it does not mean the shop is honest.', 'Over &pound;100? Use a credit card &mdash; Section 75 makes the card company jointly responsible, up to &pound;30,000.', 'Never pay a shop or a stranger by bank transfer &mdash; it is like posting cash.', 'Changed your mind? Most online purchases can go back within 14 days &mdash; and faulty goods rights last far longer.', 'Buying from an individual on a marketplace? See it before you pay, collect locally, and never send money for something unseen.', 'Delivery text asking for a small fee? Do not click &mdash; check your order yourself and forward the text to 7726 (free).', 'Caught out? Ring your bank straight away &mdash; dialling 159 reaches most UK banks &mdash; report to Action Fraud on 0300 123 2040 (in Scotland, ring Police Scotland on 101), and call us on 01202 775566 for friendly help.'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is this course really free?', 'a': '<p>Completely free, with no catch and no card details asked for. We are a family-run IT firm that has looked after Bournemouth homes and businesses since 1995, and this course is our way of helping local people shop online with confidence.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is it actually safe to type my card details into a website?', 'a': '<p>At a reputable shop, yes &mdash; your card details are scrambled on their way across the internet, and the lessons show you how to judge which shops deserve them. Better still, paying by credit card or through a service such as PayPal adds real protection on top. The one thing we never recommend is paying a shop or a stranger by bank transfer.</p>'}, {'q': 'What is Section 75, in plain English?', 'a': '<p>A British rule that makes your credit card company jointly responsible for items costing more than &pound;100 and up to &pound;30,000. If the goods never arrive, arrive faulty, or the shop vanishes, you can claim your money back from the card company itself. Lesson 3 walks through it gently.</p>'}, {'q': 'I think I have already been caught out by a shopping scam. What should I do?', 'a': '<p>First: no shame whatsoever &mdash; these scams are professionally made and fool people of every age. If card or bank details were involved, ring your bank straight away; dialling 159 connects you safely to most UK banks. Forward scam texts to 7726 (free) and scam emails to report@phishing.gov.uk, and report it to Action Fraud on 0300 123 2040 &mdash; in Scotland, ring Police Scotland on 101. And do call us on 01202 775566 if you would like a friendly hand with any of it.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I get a certificate?', 'a': '<p>Yes &mdash; a 365 Techies certificate of completion with your name on it, ready to print, plus a one-page checklist to keep by the computer. It is our own friendly certificate rather than an accredited qualification, but it marks something genuinely worth knowing.</p>'}, {'q': 'Can someone show me all this in person?', 'a': '<p>Gladly. We run relaxed, in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth &mdash; bring your own phone, tablet or laptop and your questions. Ring 01202 775566 and we will save you a seat.</p>'}], 'crumbName': 'Online Shopping, Safely'},
]
